st Palace at Nimrud (ancient Kalhu) in Iraq was built by Neo-Assyrian king Ashurnasirpal II (r. 883-859 BC) as part of his transformation of Nimrud into the imperial capital [1] [2]. The palace featured hundreds of carved gypsum (''Mosul marble'') relief panels depicting royal hunts, warfare, and protective spirits, with the ''Standard Inscription'' repeating the king''s titles in cuneiform [1] [2] [3] [4]. Monumental lamassu -- winged human-headed bulls with five legs -- guarded its entrances [5] [6].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:338684", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "insert-2026-10-06-001-001: thumbnail_url None -> ''/data/images/wiki/02c9fcff/Relief with Two Registers - Room I, Northwest Palace, Nimrud (Kalhu), Iraq, Neo-Assyrian Period, reign of Ashur-nasir-pal II, c. 883-859 BC, alabaster - Brooklyn Museum - Brooklyn, NY - DSC08608.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
