slopes of Mount Ida (Psiloritis) in Crete, at 1,538 m altitude [1] [2] [3]. In myth, Rhea hid the infant Zeus here, guarded by the Curetes [4] [5]. Excavations by Halbherr (1885) and Sakellarakis (1982) yielded bronze shields with Assyrian influence, gold jewelry, clay figurines, and an Altar of Zeus carved into rock at the entrance [2] [3] [6]. King Minos reportedly visited every nine years to receive laws from Zeus [4] [7].''"}]'::jsonb),
