e most well known of all the Norwegian petroglyphs\". Discovered in 1842, the site now comprises around 30 figures including a bear, elk, seabird, and skier. When the carvings were made, Snasa Lake was still part of the Trondheim Fjord with water levels 35-40 metres higher than today. In Sami culture, the reindeer is interpreted as either a reindeer cow or a staajne, a typically infertile female.'' -> ''Bolareinen is a celebrated petroglyph site near Stod in Steinkjer, Norway, featuring a life-size reindeer carving (180 x 136 cm) dated to 3400-3200 BC and described as \"probably the most well known of all the Norwegian petroglyphs\" [1]. Discovered in 1842, the site now comprises around 30 figures including a bear, elk, seabird, and skier [1]. When the carvings were made, Snasa Lake was still part of the Trondheim Fjord with water levels 35-40 metres higher than today [1]. In Sami culture, the reindeer is interpreted as either a reindeer cow or a staajne, a typically infertile female [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:304732", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-001-001: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/08088896/Bølareinen rock art SNK6.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/08088896/Bøla_rock_carvings_the_reindeer.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:305939", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-001-001-rollback-rest-001: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/08088896/Bøla_rock_carvings_the_reindeer.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/08088896/Bølareinen rock art SNK6.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:306818", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-001: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/08088896/Bølareinen rock art SNK6.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/08088896/Bøla_rock_carvings_the_reindeer.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
