    ('08122cd2-522f-4ebc-b363-aabc7bd60de3'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:08122cd2-522f-4ebc-b363-aabc7bd60de3:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''6'' restored on Ringlemere Barrow', '27f216407e2a5b75712cb9ca61db0a70', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Barrow'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 34 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43901", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0082:chunk-0001: description ''Ringlemere Barrow near Sandwich, Kent, is an Early Bronze Age funerary site (c. 2300 BC) famous for the discovery of the Ringlemere Gold Cup. Found by metal detectorist Cliff Bradshaw on 4 November 2001, the crushed gold cup dates to 1700-1500 BC and was crafted from a single gold ingot with a riveted handle and horizontal ribbing. Excavations by Canterbury Archaeological Trust (2002-2005) revealed the cup had been ploughed from a massive barrow sited upon a single-entranced henge. The dig also uncovered Mesolithic activity, Neolithic features, and an Anglo-Saxon cemetery. The British Museum acquired the cup for 270,000 pounds; it is one of only seven similar Bronze Age gold vessels known from Europe.'' -> ''The Ringlemere barrow is an archaeological site near Sandwich in the English county of Kent most famous as being the find site of the Ringlemere gold cup [1]. No contemporary burials have been found at the site although later Iron Age ones have since been found along with a Saxon cemetery [1]. Excavation work has continued at the site, funded by English Heritage, the BBC, the British Museum and the Kent Archaeological Society [1]. This work has indicated that the now ploughed-away barrow was as high as 5m and had a diameter of more than 40m [1]. The flat-bottomed ditch that surrounded it was 5–6 m wide and 1.35 m deep [1]. Considerable evidence of much earlier Neolithic activity has now been found on the site including by far the largest assemblage of grooved ware in the county [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:142637", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-002: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8c/Claw_beaker_from_Ringmere_Farm_BM_2005.12-5.1.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/08122cd2/hero.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
