 during the Late Neolithic and Early Bronze Age, over a period between 3,300 and 900 BCE [1]. The site has not been excavated or been subject to in-depth archaeological investigation [1]. Kingston Russell is the largest surviving stone circle in Dorset [1]. The stones are arranged in an oval with a diameter measuring from 24 by 27 metres (79 by 89 feet) [1]. The ring consists of eighteen stones, the longest of which are located at the north of the circle [1]. As of 1939, all the stones were lying on the ground, although there is a record that in 1815 one stone on the southern side was still standing upright [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:92555", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s001: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:92556", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s001: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:142685", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/09fcbc60/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/09fcbc60/Kingston Russell stone circle I.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:245653", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s001: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:245654", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s001: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:346288", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s001: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:346289", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s001: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-2500''"}]'::jsonb),
