t contemporary with the earthen long barrows on Cranborne Chase: many of these are found near, on, or within the Cursus and since they are still in existence they help trace the Cursus'' course in the modern landscape [1]. The relationship between the Cursus and the alignment of these barrows suggests that they had a common ritual significance to the Neolithic people who spent an estimated 0.5 million worker-hours in its construction [1]. The Cursus consisted of a pair of parallel banks (1.5 m tall) running about 82 m apart, with external ditches 1.5 m deep and 2 m wide [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:142699", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/0a8f4bd4/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/0a8f4bd4/Dorset_cursus_bank_on_bottlebush_down.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:333635", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-009-001: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/0a8f4bd4/Dorset_cursus_bank_on_bottlebush_down.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/0a8f4bd4/hero.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
