 [1]. The Cursus dates from 3300 BCE which makes it contemporary with the earthen long barrows on Cranborne Chase: many of these are found near, on, or within the Cursus and since they are still in existence they help trace the Cursus'' course in the modern landscape [1]. The relationship between the Cursus and the alignment of these barrows suggests that they had a common ritual significance to the Neolithic people who spent an estimated 0.5 million worker-hours in its construction [1]. The Cursus consisted of a pair of parallel banks (1.5 m tall) running about 82 m apart, with external ditches 1.5 m deep and 2 m wide [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:142699", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/0a8f4bd4/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/0a8f4bd4/Dorset_cursus_bank_on_bottlebush_down.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:333635", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-009-001: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/0a8f4bd4/Dorset_cursus_bank_on_bottlebush_down.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/0a8f4bd4/hero.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('0aa03558-b60a-46d3-8a77-7dcce6f7e015'::uuid, 'antiquity', '3', '1', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:0aa03558-b60a-46d3-8a77-7dcce6f7e015:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''1'' restored on Tambo Viejo', '4391802d920fdb9c0f1e02080fb03c84', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 3 from period_start 130"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33041", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0012:chunk-0001: description ''Tambo Viejo is a 44-hectare archaeological site in the Arequipa region of southern Peru [1]. The settlement was occupied from the Early Intermediate period through the Inca era until the Spanish colonial period [1]. Excavations have uncovered Early Nasca pottery, Inca structures, and Spanish colonial remains, making it a multilayered site [1].'' -> ''Tambo Viejo is an archaeological site that extends over approximately 44 hectares (110 acres) in the north of the Arequipa region of southern Peru [1]. The site presents evidence of human occupation from the Early Intermediate period, including examples of Early Nasca pottery, to structures of the Inca period and remains from the Spanish colonial period [1]. Tambo Viejo is oriented in a north–south direction [1]. The main area on its north side is a large rectangular plaza, known as Plaza Principal or Plaza 1 [1]. The southern part of Tambo Viejo is made up of a labyrinth of structures connected to a smaller, more central plaza (Plaza 2), around which there are rectangular structures of various sizes [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:92571"