vealing this and four other cists. Finds examined by the Royal Irish Academy included human remains and grave goods.'' -> \"Knockmaree Dolmen, or Knockmaree Cist, is a prehistoric site of the Neolithic period, in Phoenix Park just north of Chapelizod, near Dublin, Ireland [1]. The tomb, situated on a small ridge, is a cist, a type of burial chamber found in Ireland on the south and east coast [1]. It dates from about 3000 B.C. to 2500 B.C. A capstone, measuring 1.96 metres (6 ft 5 in) by 1.05 metres (3 ft 5 in), is supported by smaller stones [1]. The capstone is water-worn, and is thought to have come from the River Liffey nearby [1]. It was discovered in 1838, when workmen were employed to remove a tumulus of height 15 feet (4.6 m) and circumference 120 feet (37 m) [1]. In the centre of the mound were found the stones now visible; four other cists were found in the outer area [1]. The remains were examined by the Royal Irish Academy, the Academy''s report later being delivered by George Petrie [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:93235", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s002: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:93236", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s002: period_start ''-4500'' -> ''-3000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:142721", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/0ac27bb1/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/0ac27bb1/County Dublin - Knockmaree Dolmen - 20190914151453.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:320990", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-004-001: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/0ac27bb1/County Dublin - Knockmaree Dolmen - 20190914151453.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/0ac27bb1/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:324942", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-005-001: thum