lus 15 feet high and 120 feet in circumference, revealing this and four other cists. Finds examined by the Royal Irish Academy included human remains and grave goods.'' -> \"Knockmaree Dolmen, or Knockmaree Cist, is a prehistoric site of the Neolithic period, in Phoenix Park just north of Chapelizod, near Dublin, Ireland [1]. The tomb, situated on a small ridge, is a cist, a type of burial chamber found in Ireland on the south and east coast [1]. It dates from about 3000 B.C. to 2500 B.C. A capstone, measuring 1.96 metres (6 ft 5 in) by 1.05 metres (3 ft 5 in), is supported by smaller stones [1]. The capstone is water-worn, and is thought to have come from the River Liffey nearby [1]. It was discovered in 1838, when workmen were employed to remove a tumulus of height 15 feet (4.6 m) and circumference 120 feet (37 m) [1]. In the centre of the mound were found the stones now visible; four other cists were found in the outer area [1]. The remains were examined by the Royal Irish Academy, the Academy''s report later being delivered by George Petrie [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:93235", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s002: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:93236", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s002: period_start ''-4500'' -> ''-3000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:142721", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/0ac27bb1/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/0ac27bb1/County Dublin - Knockmaree Dolmen - 20190914151453.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:320990", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-004-001: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/0ac27bb1/County Dublin - Knockmaree Dolmen - 20190914151453.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/0ac27bb1/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:324942", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-005-001: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/0ac27bb1/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/0ac27bb1/Knockmaroon Tomb 01.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('0ae727b3-6355-47ad-9c47-2c5f01a3bc20'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:0ae727b3-6355-47ad-9c47-2c5f01a3bc20:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Praileaitz Cave', '5086c86426adc0711ee2a39b8b9fbd21', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Rock art'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 29 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:81754", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2042:chunk-0001: description \"Praileaitz Cave (Basque: ''Rock of the Monk'') is a Paleolithic cave site in Deba, Gipuzkoa, Basque Country. In August 2006, non-figurative cave paintings were discovered during excavation, consisting of red dot groupings dated to the Lower Magdalenian period, approximately 15,500 years ago. The cave also yielded portable art on pebbles with abstract forms resembling Venus figurines found elsewhere in Paleolithic Europe. The adjacent Sasiola Quarry threatens conservation; a 50-meter protective zone was established by the Basque Government in 2007, though deemed insufficient by the Aranzadi Science Society.\" -> ''The Praileaitz Cave (Basque for Rock of the Monk cave) is located in the municipality of Deba (Gipuzkoa, Basque Country) [1]. Early in August 2006, various paleolithic cave paintings were found during an archaeological excavation - a non-figurative iconographic grouping made up of smaller groups of red dots, either isolated or forming a series [1]. Researchers have surmised that the paintings were created around 15,500 years ago [1]. The cave is located next to the Sasiola Quarry, which presents a threat to the conservation of the paintings [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:93237", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s002: period_name ''< 4500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:93238", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s002: period_start ''-13500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:142722", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-002: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/67/Praileaitz.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/0ae727b3/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:245665", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s001: period_name None -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:245666", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s001: period_start None -> ''-10000''"}]'::jsonb),
