its date is unknown [1]. It has been thought of as a Saxon boundary ditch, but aerial photographic work has suggested that it may be part of an extensive prehistoric boundary system extending from Northamptonshire to the Humber and termed `the Jurassic spine'' [1]. This site has limestone grassland with tor-grass, cock''s foot, crested dog''s-tail and red fescue [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:113527", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s001: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:113528", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s001: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:304065", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04e_period-label-undated: period_name None -> ''Undated''"}]'::jsonb),
