 site were performed in the 18th century [1]. In the late 20th and early 21st centuries, geophysical surveys have confirmed the size of the stone circles and identified additional pits and postholes [1]. The North East Circle is 30 metres in diameter and probably consisted of 10 or more stones, of which 8 survive today [1]. The South West Circle is 43 metres in diameter, and has 12 stones surviving today [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:93272", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s002: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:93273", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s002: period_start ''-4500'' -> ''-3000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:142806", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-002: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/22/Stanton_Drew_stone_circle%2C_Somerset.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/0c259791/Landscape context of Stanton Drew stone circles.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:304792", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-001-001: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/0c259791/Landscape context of Stanton Drew stone circles.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/0c259791/Stanton_Drew_stone_circle,_Somerset.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:305993", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-001-001-rollback-rest-001: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/0c259791/Stanton_Drew_stone_circle,_Somerset.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/0c259791/Landscape context of Stanton Drew stone circles.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:306878", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-001: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/0c259791/Landscape context of Stanton Drew stone circles.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/0c259791/Stanton_Drew_stone_circle,_Somerset.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
