    ('118c6bd0-cfa1-4376-8f42-34e75bec43d3'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:118c6bd0-cfa1-4376-8f42-34e75bec43d3:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''5'' restored on Burnmoor Stone Circles', '4a21c3cee4344ca1b08d043566b2fcc5', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Stone circle'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 57 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:81906", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2046:chunk-0001: description \"The Burnmoor Stone Circles are a group of five Bronze Age stone circles in Cumbria, England[1]. Located about 1.6 km (1 mi) north of Boot village on the slopes of Boat How, the site covers roughly a square mile and is managed by the National Trust[1]. The largest circle, Brat''s Hill, features approximately 42 stones forming an irregular circle roughly 30 meters in diameter[1]. The two nearby pairs of circles are known as White Moss and Low Longrigg[1].\" -> \"The Burnmoor Stone Circles are a group of five different approximately 4,000-year-old stone circles in Cumbria [1]. They are around 1 mile north of the village of Boot, on the slopes of Boat How [1]. The largest circle is known as Brat''s Hill and there are two nearby pairs of circles known as White Moss and Low Longrigg [1]. Brat''s Hill is the largest stone circle with around 42 stones forming an irregular circle with a diameter of 30 metres [1]. There are five funerary cairns within the circle together with two further stones [1]. About 100 metres to the northwest of Brat''s Hill circle lie the two White Moss stone circles [1]. One of them measures 16 metres in diameter and has 11 stones forming the circle, while the other measures 16.5 metres in diameter and has 14 stones forming the circle [1]. Both stone circles have internal cairns [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
