rtheast of Foel-fras [1]. The summit features two large shelter cairns hollowed from a massive prehistoric burial cairn [1] [2]. Its eastern slopes drain via Afon Tafolog to Afon Roe, flowing through Rowen before joining the River Conwy [1]. The site forms the final top on Carnedd Llewelyn''s long northern spur, with Tal y Fan to the northeast [1] [3].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246099", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s002: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246100", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s002: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:346348", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s001: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:346349", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s001: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-2300''"}]'::jsonb),
