 angled toward the midwinter sunset. Dating estimates range from the middle Neolithic to the early Bronze Age, around 1500 BC. The village name derives from Welsh ''tre'' (three) and ''llech'' (stone). Local legend attributes them to King Harold''s 1063 victory, though they predate him by millennia. A possible fourth stone was destroyed in the 18th century, suggesting the row may have been part of a larger monument.\" -> \"Three Bronze Age standing stones on a northeast-southwest axis in the village of Trellech, Monmouthshire, south-east Wales [1] [2]. Local legend attributes them to King Harold''s 1063 victory [1] [3].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:307662", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/174016a6/hero.webp'' -> \"/data/images/wiki/174016a6/Harold''s_Stones.webp\""}]'::jsonb),
