inner circular enclosure; John Thomas Blight, describing it in 1865, found that these features had mostly disappeared [1]. To the north-west of the round there are thick stone walls, height up to 0.9 metres: the remains of a structure with an internal diameter of about 7.5 metres [1]. This is interpreted as a courtyard house, a type of building that developed in west Cornwall from the 2nd to 4th centuries AD [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:94373", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s003: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:94374", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s003: period_start ''-1000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:94375", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s003: site_type ''Earthwork'' -> ''Settlement''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246469", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s003: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246470", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s003: period_start None -> ''-1500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:346392", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s001: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:346393", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s001: period_start ''-1500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:354759", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-19r_fields-wd5-s001: period_name ''Undated'' -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:354760", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-19r_fields-wd5-s001: period_start None -> ''-100''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:355577", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s001: site_type ''Settlement'' -> ''City/town/settlement''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('1949a2b8-9e93-4091-b404-915fb9bd8f52'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:1949a2b8-9e93-4091-b404-915fb9bd8f52:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''7'' restored on Burroughston Broch', 'f3dc7c803c1439d633c47bbff992588a', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Minaret/tower'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 13 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29568", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0308:chunk-0001: site_type ''Minaret/tower'' -> ''Fortification''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35674", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: site_type ''Fortification'' -> ''Minaret/tower''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:82096", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2051:chunk-0001: description ''Burroughston Broch is an Iron Age broch on the northeast coast of Shapinsay in the Orkney Islands, overlooking the North Sea. Excavated by George Petrie and planned by Dryden around 1862, it remains well preserved with drystone walls up to 4 metres thick and a complete chamber intact off the entrance passage. The interior contains a 3-metre-deep well, and traces of radiating walls and compartments are still visible. Excavations revealed fragments of deer horn, ox bones, sheep remains, and stone vessels. Outside the broch, traces of outbuildings connected to the entrance extend seaward, while a wall, ditch, and rampart once encircled the structure.'' -> ''Burroughston Broch is an Iron Age broch located on the island of Shapinsay in the Orkney Islands [1]. The site overlooks the North Sea on the northeast part of Shapinsay [1]. Excavated in the mid 19th century, Burroughston Broch is still well-preserved [1]. The drystone walls are up to four metres thick in some parts and there is a complete chamber intact off the entrance passage [1]. The walls of Burroughston Broch have an external diameter of around 18 metres, and an internal diameter of around 10 metres [1]. The entrance passage is on the east side, and is about 4 metres long, 1.2 metres wide and 1.8 metres high [1]. A deep well is present in the broch floor: the upper part being dry stone masonry, the lower being cut into the rock [1]. The broch was excavated around 1862 by the estate-workers of Colonel D. Balfour under the direction of the antiquarian George Petrie [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:94377", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s003: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:94378", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s003: period_start ''-1500'' -> ''-200''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:143910", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-004: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/1949a2b8/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/1949a2b8/Burroughston Broch, entry.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
