t has been the focus of rituals dedicated to the Maya rain god, Chaac, and, in the Post-Classic period, also to his Toltec counterpart, Tlaloc [1]. The cave complex was visited by Edward Thompson and Alfred Tozzer in 1905 and has since 1932 been explored and studied by various Mexican and US scholars [1]. Small buildings and platforms surrounded the cave''s entrance; inside, stairs, walls, altars and ritual displays of ceramics and small stone implements were discovered [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:94404", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s003: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:94405", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s003: period_start ''-1000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:235504", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s002: period_name None -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:235505", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s002: period_start None -> ''-300''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('1a9c5461-e447-49c9-b574-e7118f3ab279'::uuid, 'mystery', '3', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:1a9c5461-e447-49c9-b574-e7118f3ab279:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Cosquer Cave', '408a26724ac05903c44fd696875aa1e9', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 3 from (''Cave Structures'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 113 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:91828", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2210:chunk-0001: description \"Cosquer Cave is a decorated Paleolithic cave near Cap Morgiou in Marseille, France, with its entrance submerged 37 m underwater due to postglacial sea level rise. Discovered in 1985 by diver Henri Cosquer, the cave contains approximately 500 works of parietal art from two periods: Gravettian-era hand stencils (c. 27,000 BP) and Solutrean-era animal depictions (c. 19,000 BP) including horses, ibex, seals, and great auks. Rising seas have destroyed about 80% of the cave''s art, with water levels rising roughly 3 mm annually. A full-scale replica, Cosquer Mediterranee, opened in Marseille in June 2022.\" -> ''Cosquer Cave is located in the Calanque de Morgiou in Marseille, France [1]. Nearly 500 instances of cave art remain which date back to two distinct periods during the Upper Paleolithic [1]. The first phase, from around 27,000 years ago, is represented by art consisting of 65 hand stencils [1]. Art from the more recent period dates to around 19,000 years ago and features much more complex depictions of various animals and human figures [1].''"}, {"so