    ('1ab97e76-933a-4dc5-a734-42f53c8c7667'::uuid, 'antiquity', '5', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:1ab97e76-933a-4dc5-a734-42f53c8c7667:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''8'' restored on Fir Clump Stone Circle', '8b5fb68c37f02860e8e63cb75d62df5a', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 5 from period_start None"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:82134", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2052:chunk-0001: description ''Fir Clump Stone Circle was a prehistoric stone circle located in Burderop Wood near Wroughton, Wiltshire, in South West England [1]. Built between 3300 and 900 BCE during the Late Neolithic and Early Bronze Age, it formed part of a widespread tradition of stone circle construction that spread across Britain, Ireland, and Brittany [1]. The purpose of these monuments remains unknown, though some archaeologists suggest the stones may have represented supernatural entities for their builders [1].'' -> ''Fir Clump Stone Circle was a stone circle in Burderop Wood near Wroughton, Wiltshire, in South West England [1]. The ring was part of a tradition of stone circle construction that spread throughout much of Britain, Ireland, and Brittany during the Late Neolithic and Early Bronze Age, over a period between 3300 and 900 BCE [1]. A double concentric circle consisting of sarsen megaliths, the Fir Clump stone circle was oval-shaped [1]. The outer ring measured 107 metres by 86.5 metres in diameter, and the inner ring 86.5 metres by 73.7 metres [1]. It was one of at least seven stone circles that