ing the Cambrian period when this region was covered by ancient seas, the pillars represent exposed remnants of ancient sea basins [1]. The highest concentration of pillars is found between the villages of Petrovskoye and Tit-Ary [1]. The Lena Pillars Nature Park was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 2012 [1] [2].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246487", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s003: period_name None -> ''1 - 500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246488", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s003: period_start None -> ''1''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347147", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347148", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_start ''1'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('1ac1779a-749a-4726-a671-d49a3d1b16e2'::uuid, 'antiquity', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:1ac1779a-749a-4726-a671-d49a3d1b16e2:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''4'' restored on Vescia', 'd8eec4ce53b64943b287aa5be334a8c4', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 5 from period_start None"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33193", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0016:chunk-0001: description ''Vescia was an ancient fortified city of the Ausones, part of the Auruncan Pentapolis alongside Ausona, Minturnae, Sinuessa, and Suessa. Located perhaps on the left bank of the Garigliano river in the comuni of Cellole and Sessa Aurunca, the city was destroyed by Rome in 314 BC after the Aurunci allied with the Samnites. Unlike Suessa and Minturnae, which survived as Roman colonies, Vescia disappeared entirely. Pre-Roman wall remains and subterranean aqueducts on Monte Massico may mark its location.'' -> ''Vescia was an ancient city of the Ausones, in what is now central-southern Italy, which was part of the so-called Auruncan Pentapolis and was destroyed by the Romans in 340 BC [1]. Two Latin inscriptions referring to Vescia, dating to 211-212 AD, have been found in Castelforte, Formia, in the southern province of Latina [1]. These are now in the Archaeological Museum of Minturno [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:114754", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s002: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:114755", "url": "remedi