    ('1b48aaf6-bd3f-4ec5-81d3-8e308de6e308'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:1b48aaf6-bd3f-4ec5-81d3-8e308de6e308:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''5'' restored on Dohnsen, Siddernhausen - Dolmen', 'acc97b47873d7cbc0a09308fbd8ab88e', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Dolmen'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 22 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:82156", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2053:chunk-0001: description ''The Dohnsen-Siddernhausen dolmen (German: Großsteingrab) is a Neolithic megalithic burial monument located on a low elevation between the villages of Dohnsen and Siddernhausen near the town of Bergen in the district of Celle, Lower Saxony, northern Germany [1]. Originally situated approximately 500 metres further to the north, the monument was destroyed during the 17th or 18th century, with its massive stones lying scattered across agricultural fields [1]. Several stones were sunk into the ground while others had been removed for other uses [1]. The dolmen was excavated in 1977 and subsequently reconstructed at its present location [1]. This megalithic structure dates from the Neolithic period, specifically between 3500-2800 BC, representing a fascinating example of prehistoric burial practices in northern Germany [1].'' -> ''The dolmen on a low elevation between the villages of Dohnsen and Siddernhausen near the town of Bergen in North Germany, was originally 500 m further north [1]. It was excavated in 1977 and reconstructed in its present location [1]. This dolmen from the New Stone Age is believed to have been destroyed in the 17th or 18th century, because its large stones lay scattered on the fields [1]. Several of the stones were sunk into the ground, others had been carried away [1]. Excavations revealed that the burial chamber originally comprised 20 to 24 upright stones and about 8 capstones [1]. It had a side entrance; this type of grave is known as a passage grave [1]. The open space between the uprights and the capstones was sealed in with dry stonework [1]. The dead were laid on the floor of the burial chamber and prepared for the afterlife by being buried with their traditional jewellery, earthenware jars and everyday implements [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:94414", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s003: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:94415", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s003: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:143979", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-004: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/1b48aaf6/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/1b48aaf6/Großsteingrab bei Siddernhausen Seiteneingang 20210912.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:235514", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s002: period_name None -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:235515", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s002: period_start None -> ''-3000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:307746", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/1b48aaf6/Großsteingrab bei Siddernhausen Seiteneingang 20210912.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/1b48aaf6/GroßsteingrabSidd2.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
