    ('1bda8d7d-01e8-4063-b1c1-c2f46c6b9a64'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:1bda8d7d-01e8-4063-b1c1-c2f46c6b9a64:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''5'' restored on Roman Theatre, Mainz', '20107648d8f8503de2614cdb9482ca2a', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Theatre'', ''1 - 500 AD'') is shared by 22 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43975", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0084:chunk-0001: description ''The largest Roman theatre north of the Alps, built in the 1st century AD at Mogontiacum (modern Mainz), with a 116-metre diameter, 42-metre stage width, and capacity of approximately 10,000 spectators. The city\\''s strategic position at the Rhine-Main confluence made it a major legionary base from 13/12 BC. Stage foundations were first found in 1884 during railway construction, but full identification came in 1914. Major excavations began in 1999, and the nearby train station was renamed \"Mainz - Roman Theater\" in 2006.'' -> ''The Roman Theatre in Mainz, Rhineland Palatinate was excavated in the late 1990s [1]. It is located immediately next to the Mainz Römisches Theater station and was once the largest Roman theatre north of the Alps, with a diameter of 116 metres, a stage-width of 42 metres, and a capacity of roughly ten thousand people [1]. From 9 BC, military parades were held in honour of Drusus at his cenotaph, the Drususstein, which was only 340 metres away from the theatre [1]. Foundations of the stage building were found in 1884 during the construction of the railway and were recorded before being demolished to make way for the tracks and platforms of the South Station [1]. In 1914, when further remains were discovered during the construction of a canal, the art historian Ernst Neeb identified the remains as part of a Roman theatre building [1]. An exploratory excavation in 1916 supported his case, but because of the First World War, no further excavations could be undertaken [1]. In 1998, after several years of planning, several pillars were uncovered as part of a first test trench [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:143995", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-004: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4b/B%C3%BChnentheater_Mainz.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/1bda8d7d/20230314 Roman theater Mainz.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:307770", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/1bda8d7d/20230314 Roman theater Mainz.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/1bda8d7d/Bühnentheater_Mainz.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
