    ('1d873464-bd56-415a-984e-67026294d00c'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:1d873464-bd56-415a-984e-67026294d00c:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''7'' restored on Broch of Burrian', '6a232570fe4abad0185b3cf7f5ee2173', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Minaret/tower'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 13 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33223", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0017:chunk-0001: description ''Iron Age broch on the southern tip of North Ronaldsay, Orkney, defended by four concentric ramparts on its landward side. Excavated by William Traill in 1870-71, the broch yielded a remarkable assemblage spanning centuries of occupation, including Pictish symbol-inscribed bones, an ogham cross-slab, and a Celtic ecclesiastical iron bell dating to the 5th-9th centuries. A radiocarbon-dated ox phalange bearing a crescent-and-V-rod symbol places Pictish activity at cal AD 570-655. The finds are now held in the National Museum of Scotland.'' -> ''The Broch of Burrian is an Iron Age broch located on North Ronaldsay in the Orkney Islands [1]. The broch stands on a small headland next to a rocky shoreline [1]. The broch has an external diameter of 18 metres and an internal diameter of 9.5 metres [1]. The broch is surrounded by outer defences consisting of the remains of four concentric ramparts on the landward side [1]. The Broch of Burrian was excavated by William Traill, proprietor of the island, in 1870 and 1871 [1]. A large number of artefacts, including a significant quantity of worked bone objects, were discovered [1]. In addition, a number of artefacts of early historic or Pictish date were found [1]. These include a cross-slab with ogham inscription, painted pebbles and part of an iron bell of Celtic type [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
