atre in being built on a site that is only slightly sloping (where possible Roman theatres took advantage of the natural terrain), and in its plan (although there are theatres with similar plans in Northern Gaul) [1]. The theatre was built in about 140AD [1]. Urban life continued in Verulamium into the 5th century [1]. However, by that time the theatre had fallen into disuse [1]. It was used as a rubbish dump in the 4th century [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:114818", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s002: lat ''51.7551328001168'' -> ''51.754079''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:114819", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s002: lon ''-0.3360214604358662'' -> ''-0.358312''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:144049", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-004: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a0/RomanTheatreVerulamium-PS1.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/1dd3a790/The Roman Theatre at Verulamium, St Albans (14213826514).webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:307818", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/1dd3a790/The Roman Theatre at Verulamium, St Albans (14213826514).webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/1dd3a790/RomanTheatreVerulamium-PS1.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
