raga was discovered during preventive archaeological excavations led in 1990 by the Museu Arqueològic Comarcal de Banyoles [1]. This first excavation revealed that the site was an Early Neolithic Cardial settlement likely occupied from the end of the 6th millennium BC [1]. During the older phase (5.290-5.200 cal BC) the settlement can be described as a pile dwelling site and it represents the largest known settlement in the NE of the Iberian Peninsula, while in the later phase (5.100-4.800 cal BC) large surfaces were covered by stone pavement [1]. The farmers of La Draga were among the first to practise agriculture in the north-east of the Iberian Peninsula [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:94490", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s003: lat ''42.11961149100136'' -> ''42.126667''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:94491", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s003: lon ''2.766367439024681'' -> ''2.758611''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:144065", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-004: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/1e6efd70/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/1e6efd70/La Draga 01.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
