    ('21d63980-32dc-43cc-baa1-06c9e2f15335'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:21d63980-32dc-43cc-baa1-06c9e2f15335:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''7'' restored on Silbury Hill', '8a491af32c194f10a193a0aa11bbc57a', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Mound/tumulus'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 14 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33421", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0018:chunk-0001: description ''Silbury Hill is a prehistoric artificial chalk mound near Avebury in Wiltshire. At 39.3 metres (129 ft) high, it is the second tallest prehistoric man-made mound in Europe and similar in volume to contemporary Egyptian pyramids[1]. Part of the Stonehenge, Avebury and Associated Sites UNESCO World Heritage Site, the site is in the care of English Heritage[1].'' -> ''Silbury Hill is a prehistoric artificial chalk mound near Avebury in the English county of Wiltshire [1]. At 39.3 metres high, the hill is the tallest and second largest prehistoric man-made mound in Europe and one of the largest in the world; it is similar in volume to contemporary Egyptian pyramids [1]. Silbury Hill is part of the complex of Neolithic monuments around Avebury, which includes the Avebury Ring and West Kennet Long Barrow [1]. The mound is composed mainly of chalk and clay excavated from the surrounding area and stands 39.3 metres high and covers about 2 hectares [1]. The hill was constructed in several stages between c.\\u20092400 and 2300 BC and displays immense technical skill and prolonged control over labour and resources [1]. Few prehistoric artifacts have been found on Silbury Hill: at its core there is only clay, flints, turf, moss, topsoil, gravel, freshwater shells, mistletoe, oak, hazel, sarsen stones, ox bones, and antler tines [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:144637", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-005: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f6/Prehistoric_Silbury_Hill%2C_Wiltshire.JPG'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/21d63980/Prehistoric_Silbury_Hill,_Wiltshire.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
