d possibly contained over thirty stones [1]. It was one of at least seven stone circles that are known to have been erected in the area south of Swindon in northern Wiltshire [1]. The earliest known reference to the site was made by the local writer Richard Jefferies in the 1860s [1]. When the antiquarian A. D. Passmore investigated the site during the 1890s he found nine stones, mostly buried [1]. He observed a line of five stones not far from the circle''s northern end, suggesting that these were part of a prehistoric avenue connected to the circle; these too no longer existed by the 21st century [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:95247", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s004: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:95248", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s004: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246883", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246884", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347190", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347191", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
