o features an E-Group astronomical complex dating to 900-800 BCE.'' -> ''Chiapa de Corzo is a major Mesoamerican archaeological site in Chiapas, Mexico, settled by the Zoque people, descendants of Mixe-Zoquean speakers from the Olmec tradition [1] [2] [3]. Rising to prominence c. 700-500 BCE, it became one of the earliest linearly planned towns in Mesoamerica, reaching 70 hectares [1] [2]. In 2010, archaeologists discovered a 2,700-year-old pyramidal tomb in Mound 11 -- the oldest such tomb in Mesoamerica, predating those at Tikal by 600 years [1] [2]. The site also features an E-Group astronomical complex dating to 900-800 BCE [2].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('24b5da43-0a0d-45b4-b23e-7ca3fa4cd41a'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:24b5da43-0a0d-45b4-b23e-7ca3fa4cd41a:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''6'' restored on Derventio Coritanorum', 'f68fefa9a708bbc9b5ed949dc9486da7', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Fort'', ''1 - 500 AD'') is shared by 26 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44049", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0086:chunk-0001: description ''Derventio Coritanorum was a Roman fort and small town at Little Chester on the outskirts of Derby, England. The first fort in the area was built at Strutts Park around 52-57 AD during campaigns of Aulus Didius Gallus, before a new fort was constructed on the present site around 80 AD. Excavations revealed timber barrack-blocks and gilded bronze military equipment suggesting a prestigious cavalry or legionary garrison. The Derwent Valley became a productive area for pottery manufacture, with multiple kilns at nearby Hazelwood and Holbrook producing distinctive Derbyshire Ware, while lead mines at Lutudarum (Crich) supported regional metalworking for some 200 years.'' -> ''Derventio was a small town in the Roman province of Britannia [1]. Today the area is known as Little Chester, on the outskirts of Derby, located in the English county of Derbyshire [1]. The first castra in the area was built on the opposite bank of the River Derwent at Strutts Park [1]. It was replaced about AD 80 by a fort on the present site, but this only lasted about forty years, then was decommissioned [1]. There was extensive Roman activity prompted by the fort, which was connected westward by a road to the Icknield Street, and to the east by a road to Sawley on the River Trent [1]. A fort-vicus which manufactured pottery and worked iron was founded 600m to the east on the Sawley Road [1]. The fort was later re-occupied and re-used for a further twenty five years [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:95281", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s004: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Fort''"}]'::jsonb),
