 Period [1]. The site is dominated by a palace structure, consisting of 13 rooms all with doors opening on a central courtyard [1]. Each room is likely to have served a different purpose, with evidence for ovens, grain storage and a furnace for glass-making found in separate areas [1]. A more typical house from the site is similar in its design, but smaller in area, with divided rooms averaging 3x2m [1]. In contrast with Western Europe, timber is not used in construction, with all houses being made from sun-dried mud brick and having stone foundations [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:95296", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s004: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:95297", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s004: period_start ''1'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:144720", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-005: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/25ba654d/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/25ba654d/Al-Rabadha_Jar.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246913", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_name None -> ''1 - 500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246914", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_start None -> ''1''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347208", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347209", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_start ''1'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('25cf40c9-4d5a-4818-9d6a-254bc7e097a7'::uuid, 'antiquity', '7', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:25cf40c9-4d5a-4818-9d6a-254bc7e097a7:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''8'' restored on Bluestonehenge', 'df3cadd0ae72da6190ffd927e79e5981', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 7 from period_start -3000"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:95298", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s004: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:95299", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s004: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:170072", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4wc:p4wc-4216:chunk-0001: description \"Bluestonehenge (West Amesbury Henge) is a prehistoric stone circle discovered in 2009 by Mike Parker Pearson''s Stonehenge Riverside Project, located where the Stonehenge Avenue meets the River Avon. Excavations revealed nine stone holes suggesting a circle of approximately 25 Welsh bluestones within a 25-metre ditched enclosure. The circle was likely constructed around 3000 BC, with the surrounding henge ditch dating to c.2400 BC. Parker Pearson proposed that these bluestones were later removed and incorporated into Stonehenge during its major remodeling phase around 2500 BC. The site formed part of a ceremonial route linking Durrington Walls to Stonehenge along the River Avon.\" -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:235633", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s002: period_name None -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:235634", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s002: period_start None -> ''-3000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:338481", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4wc:p4wc-4325:chunk-0001: description None -> ''Bluestonehenge, also called Bluehenge, is a prehistoric henge and stone circle monument about 1 mile south-east of Stonehenge in Wiltshire, England [1]. Tentative dating places the monument between about 3000 and 2400 BC [1]. Excavations in August 2008 and again in August 2009 revealed several stone settings thought to have been erected between 3400 and 2500 BC [1]. The stone circle may have held as many as 27 stones within a diameter of 33 feet (10 m) [1]. The henge ditch and a series of stone settings are all that survives of the site, none of it visible above ground [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
