e that the Anakeion may have lain within the vicinity of the Old Agora, perhaps to the east of the Acropolis [1]. The decoration of the Anakeion, according to Pausanias, fell to Mikon and Polygnotos [1]. The former depicted the Argonauts, the followers of Jason, with particular attention to Akastos and his horses [1]. Polygnotos depicted the Rape of the Leukippides: the forcible abduction and marriage of Phoebe and Hilaeira, daughters of Leukippos, by the Dioskouroi [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:95309", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s004: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:95310", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s004: period_start ''-1500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:144744", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-005: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/26c567ca/hero.webp'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246921", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_name None -> ''1 - 500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246922", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_start None -> ''43''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347219", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347220", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_start ''43'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('26de5254-9e5e-4611-9439-35f3086d9ab5'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:26de5254-9e5e-4611-9439-35f3086d9ab5:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Bryn Gwyn Stones', '062b087eeeaf9431409147a4a140e6f7', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (''Stone circle'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 8 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29296", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0267:chunk-0001: site_type ''Megalithic stones'' -> ''Stone circle''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44055", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0086:chunk-0001: description ''The Bryn Gwyn Stones are Neolithic standing stones in Brynsiencyn on Anglesey, Wales, dating to approximately 4100-2500 BC. Standing about 280 m southwest of the henge monument Castell Bryn Gwyn, the two surviving stones -- at 4 m and 3 m tall -- are among the tallest standing stones in Wales. In 1723 Henry Rowlands described them as part of a ruinous circle of eight stones in a ring about 16 m across. The taller stone bears notches for roof timbers, evidence of its reuse as an 18th-century cottage gable end.'' -> ''The Bryn Gwyn Stones or Bryn Gwyn Standing Stones are neolithic stones in Brynsiencyn on Anglesey [1]. The Bryn Gwyn stones stand about 280 metres to the south-west of Castell Bryn Gwyn, on the low ridge some 2 m above the valley of the Afon Braint on the Isle of Anglesey in Wales [1]. They are the tallest standing stones in Wales, nearly 4 m high [1]. In 1723 Henry Rowlands described them as part of a ruinous circle of eight stones, some 16 m across [1]. An account of 1797 says that \"ignorant country people supposing money was hid under them tore them up\" and today only two stones, one slab and one pillar, stand in a modern field bank [1]. Nothing else is visible on the ground, but a 2008 excavation found three pits of standing stones, two containing stone stumps, consistent with the record by Rowlands [1]. Further excavations in 2010 identified the pits of three more removed standing stones, making seven identified in all of the eight expected to make up the original circle [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:95311", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s004: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:95312", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s004: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246923", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246924", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:324976", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-005-001: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/26de5254/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/26de5254/Bryn Gwyn slab stone with small adult 11102009.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
