 held in the British Museum. It was the richest Anglo-Saxon burial known until the discovery of Sutton Hoo in the 1930s. The village name derives from Old English meaning \"Taeppa\\''s barrow\".'' -> ''Taplow is best known for the Taplow Barrow, a 7th-century Anglo-Saxon burial mound [1] [2]. Excavated in 1883, it contained the lavishly furnished grave of a chieftain or prince [2]. Grave goods included a sword, spears, shields, a gold buckle, drinking horns, glass beakers, now held in the British Museum [2]. It was the richest Anglo-Saxon burial known until the discovery of Sutton Hoo in the 1930s [2]. The village name derives from Old English meaning \"Taeppa\\''s barrow\" [2] [3].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246927", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246928", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_start None -> ''-1500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347221", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347222", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_start ''-1500'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
