terials used to make tools at the site included sandstone, quartzite, mudstone, andesite, siltstone, and slate; these tools included chopper-chopping tools, scrapers, sumatraliths, short-axes, disks, and flakes [1]. The Tham Lod rockshelter contained four human burials [1]. Two bodies were buried one above the other [1]. The upper skeleton, of indeterminate sex, was found in an extended position 46 cm below ground and dated to 12,100 +/- 60 years BP [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
    ('2a37333e-17ba-4a27-8084-3759d0b8501f'::uuid, 'total_power', '21', '22', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:2a37333e-17ba-4a27-8084-3759d0b8501f:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: total_power ''22'' restored on Tham Lod Rockshelter', '834543911001c6cc9b980757405fed13', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 21 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 10 + 2 + 5 + 3 + 1"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33501", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0020:chunk-0001: description ''Tham Lod Rockshelter is a prehistoric site in the limestone karst of northwestern Thailand, about 150 km from Chiang Mai, with human occupation spanning from approximately 40,000 to 10,000 BC. Excavated from 2002 to 2006 by Rasmi Shoocongdej of Silpakorn University, it yielded multiple burials including a female skeleton dated to 13,640 BP interred in a flexed position with stone tools. Over 1,700 stone artifacts indicate the site functioned as a major tool workshop and regional trading center, with chopping tools, short-axes, and hammering implements recovered. Teakwood coffins were also discovered, revealing sophisticated mortuary practices among early Southeast Asian hunter-gatherers.'' -> \"Tham Lod Rockshelter is a 30 metre deep cave near the Nam Lang stream in northern Thailand [1]. Archaeological remains of hearths, a workshop for the production of stone tools, and a set of burials have been found [1]. Ben Marwick''s study of Tham Lod found at least 1722 stone artifacts [1]. The number of finds indicates that the site served as a tool workshop [1]. Materials used to make tools at the site included sandstone, quartzite, mudstone, andesite, siltstone, and slate; these tools included chopper-chopping tools, scrapers, sumatraliths, short-axes, disks, and flakes [1]. The Tham Lod rockshelter contained four human burials [1]. Two bodies were buried one above the other [1]. The upper skeleton, of indeterminate sex, was found in an extended position 46 cm below ground and dated to 12,100 +/- 60 years BP [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
