f a tradition of stone circle construction that spread throughout much of Britain, Ireland, and Brittany during the Late Neolithic and Early Bronze Ages [1]. The Nine Ladies stands on the northern end of Stanton Moor, an area of heathland in the Peak District [1]. It is at a height of between 297 and 298 metres OD [1]. The Nine Ladies stone circle measures 10.8 m in diameter, and is located on ground which slopes downward towards the east to northeast [1]. A report from 1907 noted that there were nine stones in the circle, all but one of which stood upright [1]. The stones are made from a millstone grit sourced locally, a type of medium-grained sandstone [1]. The antiquarian Hayman Rooke noted the existence of the Nine Ladies, which he considered \"a Druid temple,\" in a 1782 article about the heritage of Stanton Moor published in the journal Archaeologia [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:114895", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s002: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:114896", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s002: period_start ''-4500'' -> ''-2000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:145451", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-006: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b7/Nine_Ladies_Stone_Circle_on_Stanton_Moor.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/2a4b62ce/Nine Ladies Stone Circle Derbyshire.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:308665", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-003: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/2a4b62ce/Nine Ladies Stone Circle Derbyshire.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/2a4b62ce/Nine_Ladies_Stone_Circle_on_Stanton_Moor.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('2a4f816f-71bb-43b5-be29-9239c432fc4f'::uuid, 'antiquity', '7', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:2a4f816f-71bb-43b5-be29-9239c432fc4f:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''8'' restored on Emblance Downs Stone Circles', '980224a2837bdc64e11f0eb8ca9702f8', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 7 from period_start -2500"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:82510", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2063:chunk-0001: description \"Emblance Downs Stone Circles are a pair of Bronze Age stone circles on Bodmin Moor, Cornwall, in the parish of St Breward. Each circle is approximately 23 metres in diameter and originally contained 18-19 stones; they stand just 3.3 metres apart. The western circle retains 6 earthfast and 4 recumbent stones, while the eastern has 4 earthfast and 4 recumbent. The name derives from 1580 records as ''Emlands,'' meaning flat-topped land. A 2017 volunteer clearance project by the Timeseekers greatly improved knowledge of these largely forgotten monuments.\" -> \"Emblance Downs stone circles are a pair of stone circles located in the parish of St. Breward on Bodmin Moor in Cornwall [1]. The two ruined stone circles are situated between King Arthur''s Downs and Emblance Downs [1]. They lie close together separated by 2.5 metres and each has a diameter of about 23 metres [1]. The circles had a very irregular spacing of stones, and each ring has an exceptionally large stone to the southeast [1]. The western circle is better preserved with six upright and two fallen stones on the perimeter, with the one in the southeast being 1.6m long [1]. Another two fallen and perhaps displaced stones are near the centre of the circle [1]. There may have been about 15 stones originally [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:96279", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s005: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:96280", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s005: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246961", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246962", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347254", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347255", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-2500''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('2a4f816f-71bb-43b5-be29-9239c432fc4f'::uuid, 'total_power', '25', '26', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:2a4f816f-71bb-43b5-be29-9239c432fc4f:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: total_power ''26'' restored on Emblance Downs Stone Circles', '980224a2837bdc64e11f0eb8ca9702f8', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 25 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 7 + 8 + 5 + 4 + 1"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:82510", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2063:chunk-0001: description \"Emblance Downs Stone Circles are a pair of Bronze Age stone circles on Bodmin Moor, Cornwall, in the parish of St Breward. Each circle is approximately 23 metres in diameter and originally contained 18-19 stones; they stand just 3.3 metres apart. The western circle retains 6 earthfast and 4 recumbent stones, while the eastern has 4 earthfast and 4 recumbent. The name derives from 1580 records as ''Emlands,'' meaning flat-topped land. A 2017 volunteer clearance project by the Timeseekers greatly improved knowledge of these largely forgotten monuments.\" -> \"Emblance Downs stone circles are a pair of stone circles located in the parish of St. Breward on Bodmin Moor in Cornwall [1]. The two ruined stone circles are situated between King Arthur''s Downs and Emblance Downs [1]. They lie close together separated by 2.5 metres and each has a diameter of about 23 metres [1]. The circles had a very irregular spacing of stones, and each ring has an exceptionally large stone to the southeast [1]. The western circle is better preserved with six upright and two fallen stones on the perimeter, with the one in the southeast being 1.6m long [1]. Another two fallen and perhaps displaced stones are near the centre of the circle [1]. There may have been about 15 stones originally [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:96279", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s005: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:96280", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s005: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246961", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246962", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347254", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347255", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-2500''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('2a956522-6ffd-4ab1-be5c-dc17990dfa79'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:2a956522-6ffd-4ab1-be5c-dc17990dfa79:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''7'' restored on Sechin Alto', 'f654e00f693e9c8e13c46dd0a7ffd27e', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Mound/tumulus'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 14 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:82516", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2063:chunk-0001: description ''Sechin Alto is a massive ceremonial complex in the Casma Valley of Peru, built around 1800-1400 BC during the Formative Period. Its main pyramid measures 300 by 250 metres and rises 35 metres high, clad in granite blocks weighing up to two tonnes, making it the largest pre-Columbian monument in Peru. The complex spans over 100 hectares and includes large rectangular plazas and smaller mounds serving ceremonial and administrative functions. It forms part of a four-site complex with Taukachi-Konkan, Cerro Sechin, and Sechin Bajo along the Sechin River, first documented by archaeologist Julio C. Tello in 1937.'' -> ''Sechin Alto is a massive architectural complex in Peru belonging to the Early Formative period (2000-1500 BC) [1]. It is located in the Casma Province, the Ancash Region, on the left bank of the Sechín River [1]. Archaeologist Julio C. Tello was the first to make a thorough description of this archaeological site during his visit to the valley of Casma in 1937 [1]. The main structure, called \"Templo\", has a pyramidal shape [1]. It measures 350m by 300m, and reaches the height of 35m [1]. It consists of raised embankments and walls with large stones alternating with smaller ones; conical adobe bricks are seen in the interior [1]. In front of the \"Templo\" extend five courts or plazas, some with sunken circular courts; this indicates the ceremonial character of the whole complex [1]. The complex is oriented to the northwest, and its length is about 2 km [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:96288", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s005: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:96289", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s005: period_start ''-3000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:96290", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s005: site_type ''Pyramid complex'' -> ''Mound/tumulus''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:145456", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-006: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Vista_de_Sechín_Alto_desde_las_faldas_del_cerro_Sechín.jpg/400px-Vista_de_Sechín_Alto_desde_las_faldas_del_cerro_Sechín.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/2a956522/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:235681", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s002: period_name None -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:235682", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s002: period_start None -> ''-2000''"}]'::jsonb),
