ities, positioned near the Porta Praetoria to facilitate crowd movement. Substantial restoration was carried out in the 1930s under archaeologist Pietro Barocelli, and the theatre continues to host performances today.'' -> ''The Roman Theatre of Aosta (Teatro Romano) is an ancient theatre in the city of Aosta in the Aosta Valley, northern Italy, built during the Julio-Claudian dynasty [1] [2] [3]. Its imposing 22-metre-high facade wall remains towering over the surrounding urban area [1] [4]. The semicircular cavea and stage were enclosed within a rectangular perimeter wall -- an unusual design among Roman cities, positioned near the Porta Praetoria to facilitate crowd movement [4] [5]. Substantial restoration was carried out in the 1930s under archaeologist Pietro Barocelli, and the theatre continues to host performances today [4] [6] [7].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246965", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_name None -> ''1 - 500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246966", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_start None -> ''43''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347256", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347257", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_start ''43'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
