e enclosed within a rectangular perimeter wall -- an unusual design among Roman cities, positioned near the Porta Praetoria to facilitate crowd movement. Substantial restoration was carried out in the 1930s under archaeologist Pietro Barocelli, and the theatre continues to host performances today.'' -> ''The Roman Theatre of Aosta (Teatro Romano) is an ancient theatre in the city of Aosta in the Aosta Valley, northern Italy, built during the Julio-Claudian dynasty [1] [2] [3]. Its imposing 22-metre-high facade wall remains towering over the surrounding urban area [1] [4]. The semicircular cavea and stage were enclosed within a rectangular perimeter wall -- an unusual design among Roman cities, positioned near the Porta Praetoria to facilitate crowd movement [4] [5]. Substantial restoration was carried out in the 1930s under archaeologist Pietro Barocelli, and the theatre continues to host performances today [4] [6] [7].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246965", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_name None -> ''1 - 500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246966", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_start None -> ''43''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347256", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347257", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_start ''43'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('2ac1dae4-0ddb-4592-ae5d-1b628466832d'::uuid, 'antiquity', '4', '3', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:2ac1dae4-0ddb-4592-ae5d-1b628466832d:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''3'' restored on Kitanaura Antik Kenti', '75c73761ecebc4c6b922b962bb86cc96', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 4 from period_start -323"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:82518", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2063:chunk-0001: description ''Kitanaura was an ancient Lycian city whose ruins lie near Saraycik village in the Kumluca district of Antalya Province, Turkey. The city was unknown to ancient authors and was identified in 1998 through coins in the Antalya Museum and a 1st-century AD Roman milestone from Patara. The acropolis features walls up to 5-6 metres high dating from the Hellenistic to Byzantine periods, with remains of multiple basilicas and a notable bath-gymnasium complex. Five churches have been identified, suggesting the city grew in importance with the spread of Christianity in Late Antiquity.'' -> \"Kitanaura was an ancient city in Lycia [1]. Its ruins are located near Saraycık, a small village in the Kumluca district of Antalya Province [1]. The history of the city is largely unknown because it was not mentioned by any ancient author [1]. The earliest phase of the city''s walls dates to the Hellenistic period, with the last phase dating to the Byzantine period [1]. Because of the five churches which have been identified the city likely became more important with the spread of Christianity in Late Antiquity [1]. T. A. B. Spratt visited the site for two days in 1842 to copy inscriptions and drew a plan [1]. A 1998 study of coins in the Antalya Museum led to the identification of the name of Kitanaura [1]. The acropolis of Kitanaura was located on a hill extending in an east–west direction and surrounded by remains of structures to its south and west [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:96293", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s005: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:96294", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s005: period_start ''1'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:145477", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-006: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/2ac1dae4/hero.webp'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246967", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_name None -> ''1 - 500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246968", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_start None -> ''1''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347258", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347259", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_start ''1'' -> ''-323''"}]'::jsonb),
