lar, square, and trapezoidal ground plans. Ceramics are distinctively decorated with white-painted angular triangles, zigzag lines, and Z- and sigma-shaped motifs. Pottery, tools, and ritual objects are housed in the Museum of Bitola.'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:235684", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s002: lat ''40.99817840746627'' -> ''40.931778''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:235685", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s002: lon ''21.372040114971085'' -> ''21.368667''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:338483", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4wc:p4wc-4325:chunk-0001: description None -> ''Veluška Tumba is an ancient settlement from Neolithic times, near the village of Porodin in the Republic of Macedonia, close to Bitola [1]. The site is an artificially raised mound shaped like a truncated cone, with an elliptical base laid out east to west [2]. The mound measures 250 m in length, 150 m in width and 0.70 to 5 m in height [2]. The excavations found a cultural layer four metres thick, filled with habitation remains spread over four horizons [2].''"}]'::jsonb),
