ed among the best preserved in Guatemala [7].\" -> \"Aguateca is a Maya archaeological site located in northern Guatemala''s Petexbatun Basin in the department of Petén [1] [2]. The center was occupied from approximately 200 B.C. until about 800 A.D., when the city was attacked and ransacked [1] [3]. Aguateca sits on top of a 90 metres (300 ft) tall limestone bluff, creating a highly defensible position [1]. An extensive system of defensive walls surrounds the city [1] [3]. The city was completely abandoned, with a 6-metre (20 ft) tall temple left unfinished and the city center destroyed by fire [1] [2]. The ruins are considered among the best preserved in Guatemala [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:308677", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-003: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/2b060651/Aguateca M7-26-1.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/2b060651/Aguateca-plaza.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:355601", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s001: site_type ''City'' -> ''City/town/settlement''"}]'::jsonb),
