escription ''The Hwangnyongsa Belt is a cluster of ancient temple ruins centered around Hwangnyongsa and Bunhwansa temples in Gyeongju, South Korea. Part of the UNESCO-listed Gyeongju Historic Areas since 2000, these Silla Kingdom (57 BC–935 AD) sites preserve the foundations of some of the largest Buddhist temples ever built in ancient Korea. [1]'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:338485", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4wc:p4wc-4325:chunk-0001: description None -> \"The Hwangnyongsa Belt is one of five subregions of the Gyeongju Historic Areas, a World Heritage Site in Gyeongju, South Korea [1]. The belt is centred on the ruins of the temple Hwangnyongsa, which the excavated foundation stones show covered 72,500 square metres [1]. The temple Hwangnyongsa was built during the Silla period under royal patronage, with construction starting in 553 under King Jinheung and not fully completed until 644 [2]. Mongolian invasions in 1238 burned the temple''s pagoda, of which only the foundation stones survive [2].\""}]'::jsonb),
