220", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4wc:p4wc-4218:chunk-0001: description ''The Hwangnyongsa Belt is a cluster of ancient temple ruins centered around Hwangnyongsa and Bunhwansa temples in Gyeongju, South Korea. Part of the UNESCO-listed Gyeongju Historic Areas since 2000, these Silla Kingdom (57 BC–935 AD) sites preserve the foundations of some of the largest Buddhist temples ever built in ancient Korea. [1]'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:338485", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4wc:p4wc-4325:chunk-0001: description None -> \"The Hwangnyongsa Belt is one of five subregions of the Gyeongju Historic Areas, a World Heritage Site in Gyeongju, South Korea [1]. The belt is centred on the ruins of the temple Hwangnyongsa, which the excavated foundation stones show covered 72,500 square metres [1]. The temple Hwangnyongsa was built during the Silla period under royal patronage, with construction starting in 553 under King Jinheung and not fully completed until 644 [2]. Mongolian invasions in 1238 burned the temple''s pagoda, of which only the foundation stones survive [2].\""}]'::jsonb),
    ('2b7bb7a9-4759-41bf-a703-8f4da42a1b5c'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:2b7bb7a9-4759-41bf-a703-8f4da42a1b5c:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Toquepala Caves', '8cdf01a0fa65595fc2dbce076f520a33', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Rock art'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 29 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33507", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0020:chunk-0001: description ''Prehistoric caves located 26 km southeast of Moquegua and near the Toquepala mine in southeastern Peru, at an elevation of 2,300 metres. The caves contain over 50 rock paintings dating to approximately 7650 BC, depicting hunting scenes where armed figures corral and kill guanacos using axes, lances, and spear throwers. The art was painted in red, yellow, white, and black pigments made from hematite. Discovered by a mine worker in the 1950s, the caves were formally studied by Jorge C. Muelle and Rogger Ravines between 1963 and 1967.'' -> ''Toquepala Caves are located near Toquepala mine, about 154 km from the city of Tacna, in the extreme southeast of Peru [1]. They are notable for a number of rock paintings [1]. The best known of them is the cave named Abrigo del Diablo [1]. The caves were seasonally occupied [1]. The caves have rock art of gatherer-hunters who are inferred to have lived in the caves about 10,000 years ago [1]. There are more than 50 rock paintings drawn by the inhabitants of the caves of the Palaeolithic age of which 30 were studied [1]. These scenes depict armed people hunting the guanaco and Andean religious symbols [1]. The scene depicted is of hunting by the humans who are corralling and killing a group of guanacos [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:145510", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-006: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/da/Toquepala_Caves_1.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/2b7bb7a9/Toquepala_Caves_1.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
