    ('2bdc9938-668f-4a33-a86f-8497b7f73e21'::uuid, 'mystery', '3', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:2bdc9938-668f-4a33-a86f-8497b7f73e21:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Caves of Gargas', 'b70a20803f8f9da30a6b94e21fca814d', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 3 from (''Cave Structures'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 113 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29126", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0243:chunk-0001: period_start ''-500'' -> ''-27000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30369", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-period-name: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33511", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0020:chunk-0001: description ''The Caves of Gargas in southwestern France contain Upper Paleolithic cave art dating to approximately 27,000 years ago, making them among the oldest known artistic sites in Europe [1]. Located in the Pyrenees near the town of Aventignan in the Hautes-Pyrénées department, these caves have yielded evidence of continuous occupation from the Mousterian period through the Middle Ages, including stone tools, bones, and portable art objects [1]. The site features numerous hand stencils and animal figures carved into the limestone walls [1].'' -> ''The Caves of Gargas in the Pyrenees region of France are known for their cave art from the Upper Paleolithic period - about 27,000 years old [1]. The caves are located near the town of Aventignan in the Hautes-Pyrénées department in south-western France [1]. The paintings have numerous negative hand stencils made by the stencil technique [1]. Many figurative engravings are also present in other parts of the caves, depicting horses, bison, aurochs, ibex and mammoth [1]. Carbon-14 dating of a bone stuck in a crack in a wall decorated with hand stencils revealed close to 27,000 years BP, indicating that the cave was frequented in the Gravettian period [1]. The two chambers of the caves began to be scientifically explored and documented at the end of the 19th century by Émile Cartailhac and Abbé Henri Breuil, but it was Felix Regnault who discovered the hand-print images in 1906 [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:145535", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-006: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/2bdc9938/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/2bdc9938/Magruta.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
