 are located near the town of Aventignan in the Hautes-Pyrénées department in south-western France [1]. The paintings have numerous negative hand stencils made by the stencil technique [1]. Many figurative engravings are also present in other parts of the caves, depicting horses, bison, aurochs, ibex and mammoth [1]. Carbon-14 dating of a bone stuck in a crack in a wall decorated with hand stencils revealed close to 27,000 years BP, indicating that the cave was frequented in the Gravettian period [1]. The two chambers of the caves began to be scientifically explored and documented at the end of the 19th century by Émile Cartailhac and Abbé Henri Breuil, but it was Felix Regnault who discovered the hand-print images in 1906 [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:145535", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-006: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/2bdc9938/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/2bdc9938/Magruta.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('2bf713bc-9782-4f70-9a9f-4b93fd1d5b15'::uuid, 'mystery', '3', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:2bf713bc-9782-4f70-9a9f-4b93fd1d5b15:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Tito Bustillo Cave', '30f03f8b06932b72e28afac10884ace8', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 3 from (''Cave Structures'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 113 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:82540", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2064:chunk-0001: description \"The Tito Bustillo Cave is a prehistoric rock shelter located in Ribadesella, Asturias, Spain [1]. Inhabited by Cro-Magnon humans before 10,000 BC, the cave''s entrance was sealed by a rock collapse thousands of years ago, which enabled exceptional preservation of artifacts, tools, and wall paintings discovered in 1968 [1]. Archaeological evidence indicates significant human presence during the Magdalenian culture of the Upper Paleolithic, though the site was likely occupied earlier [1].\" -> ''The Tito Bustillo Cave is a prehistoric rock shelter located in the small town of Ribadesella, in the autonomous community of Asturias [1]. The cave was inhabited by humans before the year 10,000 BC [1]. Due to the collapse of the rock, the original entrance to the cave was sealed thousands of years ago, which made it possible for preservation of objects, tools and wall paintings that were discovered in 1968 [1]. Based on those objects found in the cave, it is known that there was a significant human presence during the Magdalenian culture of the Upper Palaeolithic, but the cave was probably inhabited before that time [1]. The Tito Bustillo Cave has been designated a World Heritage Site, as part of the Cave of Altamira and Paleolithic Cave Art of Northern Spain, and it has 12 prehistorical paintings [1]. The oldest of these paintings shows human figures and is around 33,000 years old [1]. Apart from the paintings, some objects from the Magdalenian period were perfectly preserved [1]. The most important ones are some harpoons made of bone and a carved staghorn of a goat head [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:145536", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-006: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/60/Tito_Bustillo_entrada.JPG'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/2bf713bc/Cueva Tito Bustillo (01) Arqueoloxicu.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:308689", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-003: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/2bf713bc/Cueva Tito Bustillo (01) Arqueoloxicu.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/2bf713bc/Tito_Bustillo_entrada.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
