 the cave was probably inhabited before that time [1]. The Tito Bustillo Cave has been designated a World Heritage Site, as part of the Cave of Altamira and Paleolithic Cave Art of Northern Spain, and it has 12 prehistorical paintings [1]. The oldest of these paintings shows human figures and is around 33,000 years old [1]. Apart from the paintings, some objects from the Magdalenian period were perfectly preserved [1]. The most important ones are some harpoons made of bone and a carved staghorn of a goat head [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:145536", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-006: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/60/Tito_Bustillo_entrada.JPG'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/2bf713bc/Cueva Tito Bustillo (01) Arqueoloxicu.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:308689", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-003: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/2bf713bc/Cueva Tito Bustillo (01) Arqueoloxicu.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/2bf713bc/Tito_Bustillo_entrada.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
