 [1]. During the Middle Ages, the theatre was used as a quarry, with the stone pillaged to build the city wall and other buildings [1]. Today, only the stage area, orchestra section, seating rows, and two columns survive [1]. The Venus of Arles, a famous Roman statue made of marble, was found in pieces at the Roman theatre in 1651 [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:96315", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s005: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:96316", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s005: period_start ''1'' -> ''-40''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:145577", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-006: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e9/Arles%2C_teatro_romano%2C_02.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/2c4d2e4b/Arles (13) Rue de la Calade Théâtre gallo-romain 584.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:308707", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-003: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/2c4d2e4b/Arles (13) Rue de la Calade Théâtre gallo-romain 584.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/2c4d2e4b/Arles,_teatro_romano,_02.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
