    ('2e23ad32-b50b-4439-8260-fe854b891c5d'::uuid, 'legacy', '4', '1', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:2e23ad32-b50b-4439-8260-fe854b891c5d:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: legacy ''1'' restored on Massinissa Mausoleum', '1f1dce25d04f834318087c41120c3102', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 4 from period -500..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:114935", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s002: site_type ''Necropolis/tombs complex'' -> ''Tomb''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:114936", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s002: source_url ''https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Masinissa'' -> ''https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Souma%C3%A2_du_Khroub''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:170306", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4wc:p4wc-4219:chunk-0001: description ''The Massinissa Mausoleum is a Numidian royal necropolis dating to the 3rd–2nd century BC, located in El Khroub, Constantine Province, Algeria[1][2]. The monument houses the tomb of King Masinissa (ruled 202–148 BC)[1]. Characterized by massive cylindrical pillars and a cubic tomb chamber, the mausoleum represents the pinnacle of Numidian monumental architecture[2].'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:338491", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4wc:p4wc-4325:chunk-0001: description None -> ''The Soumaâ du Khroub, also called the royal Numidian mausoleum of El Khroub and nicknamed the Tomb of Massinissa, is a Numidian funerary monument at El Khroub in the wilaya of Constantine, Algeria [1]. The square-plan tower mausoleum rises 30 metres, and analysis of the objects found in it dates the monument to the second half of the 2nd century BC [1]. A document of the Cirta National Museum records excavations by French archaeologists between 1915 and 1916 that make a mixed Greco-Punic style of the stones likely [1]. The tomb held two superimposed compartments separated by slabs and contained three intact funerary urns, as well as fragments of a sword, a helmet, a tunic, a coat of mail and bones [1]. The finds from the tomb are exhibited in the Cirta National Museum [1]. Since 2002 the monument has stood on the UNESCO World Heritage tentative list under the Royal Mausoleums of Numidia and Mauretania [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
