omb of Massinissa, is a Numidian funerary monument at El Khroub in the wilaya of Constantine, Algeria [1]. The square-plan tower mausoleum rises 30 metres, and analysis of the objects found in it dates the monument to the second half of the 2nd century BC [1]. A document of the Cirta National Museum records excavations by French archaeologists between 1915 and 1916 that make a mixed Greco-Punic style of the stones likely [1]. The tomb held two superimposed compartments separated by slabs and contained three intact funerary urns, as well as fragments of a sword, a helmet, a tunic, a coat of mail and bones [1]. The finds from the tomb are exhibited in the Cirta National Museum [1]. Since 2002 the monument has stood on the UNESCO World Heritage tentative list under the Royal Mausoleums of Numidia and Mauretania [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('2e2617fe-2aa6-48ba-b8f1-4b3725e25e8d'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:2e2617fe-2aa6-48ba-b8f1-4b3725e25e8d:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''7'' restored on Broch of Borwick', '4e313c8e7a7dd7c1cad63b030efe9a2d', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Minaret/tower'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 13 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:80758", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2007:chunk-0001: description \"Iron Age broch perched on a cliff-top promontory 20 metres above the sea on the west coast of Mainland, Orkney. Built around 500 BC, the broch has an external diameter of 17 metres and walls up to 5 metres thick, standing to a maximum height of 2.6 metres. The eastern half and entrance passage are well preserved, but the western side has been lost to coastal erosion. Excavated by W. G. T. Watt in 1881, finds included combs, querns, stone gaming pieces, and an iron rod. Below the broch lies a cave called Hell''s Mouth, from Old Norse hellir.\" -> ''The Broch of Borwick is an Iron Age broch located on Mainland, Orkney, Scotland [1]. The broch stands on top of a cliff-top promontory 20 metres above the sea [1]. The broch has an external diameter of 17 metres and an internal diameter of 8 metres [1]. The walls which are 3.5 to 5 metres thick currently stand to a maximum height of 2.6 metres [1]. The eastern half of the broch and the entrance passage are well preserved, but the western half has been destroyed by erosion [1]. The site was excavated in 1881 by W. G. T. Watt, and the interior was cleared of debris [1]. Finds included several combs, a small whale vertebra cup, a spindle whorl, a stone gaming piece, a whetstone, hammerstones, some stone knives and choppers, an iron rod, and some querns [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:96353", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s005: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:96354", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s005: period_start ''1'' -> ''-500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:308735", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-003: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/2e2617fe/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/2e2617fe/Broch_of_Borwick_20110523.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
