    ('2e53c7cf-072e-465a-a53a-ba2128f50c08'::uuid, 'mystery', '1', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:2e53c7cf-072e-465a-a53a-ba2128f50c08:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''5'' restored on Ancient Sparta', 'e0d27cd3a1c1dfa60f3f90ae3762da45', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 1 from (''City/town/settlement'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 286 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30891", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:gap-0001:chunk-0002: site_type ''Megalithic stones'' -> ''City''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:91854", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2211:chunk-0001: description ''The ruins of the powerful Greek city-state on the Peloponnese, with earliest settlement evidence from the 10th century BC when four villages merged into one community. Sparta dominated Greek military affairs from the late 7th century BC through the Greco-Persian Wars. Surviving remains include the Temple of Artemis Orthia (9th-10th c. BC foundations), the sanctuary of Athena on the acropolis, a Roman-era stoa, and a theatre, though most visible ruins date to the Roman period.'' -> ''Ancient Sparta was built on the banks of the Eurotas, the largest river of Laconia, which provided it with a source of fresh water [1]. The Eurotas valley was a natural fortress, bounded to the west by Mt. Taygetus and to the east by Mt. Parnon [1]. Though landlocked, Sparta had a vassal harbour, Gytheio, on the Laconian Gulf [1]. The earliest certain evidence of human settlement in the region of Sparta consists of pottery dating from the Middle Neolithic period, found in the vicinity of Kouphovouno some two kilometres south-southwest of Sparta [1]. At its peak around 500 BC, Sparta had some 20,000–35,000 citizens, plus numerous helots and perioikoi [1]. In 464 BC, a violent earthquake occurred along the Sparta faultline destroying much of what was Sparta and many other city-states in ancient Greece [1]. Sparta never fully recovered from its losses at Leuctra in 371 BC and the subsequent helot revolts [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:146217", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-007: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/2e53c7cf/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/2e53c7cf/Ancient_sparta_theater.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:355605", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s001: site_type ''City'' -> ''City/town/settlement''"}]'::jsonb),
