    ('2eae8fd6-d3d4-4764-a8bd-2d3a8f88ebbe'::uuid, 'mystery', '3', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:2eae8fd6-d3d4-4764-a8bd-2d3a8f88ebbe:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Mehrgarh', '001c0ccb68f7112f27fae6fd4d5ce0f0', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 3 from (''City/town/settlement'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 105 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33545", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0021:chunk-0001: description ''Mehrgarh is a Neolithic archaeological site on the Kacchi Plain of Balochistan, Pakistan, near the Bolan Pass and west of the Indus River [1]. Discovered in 1974 by the French Archaeological Mission led by Jean-François and Catherine Jarrige, it was excavated from 1974-1986 and again from 1997-2000 [1]. The site yielded artifacts from six mounds, with approximately 32,000 artifacts collected [1].'' -> \"Mehrgarh is a Neolithic archaeological site situated on the Kacchi Plain of Balochistan in Pakistan [1]. It is located near the Bolan Pass, to the west of the Indus River and between the modern-day Pakistani cities of Quetta, Kalat and Sibi [1]. The site was discovered in 1974 by the French Archaeological Mission in the Indus Basin led by the French archaeologists Jean-François Jarrige and Catherine Jarrige [1]. Archaeological material has been found in six mounds, and about 32,000 artefacts have been collected from the site [1]. Recent radiocarbon dating on teeth done by Mutin and Zazzo has placed the dating of Mehrgarh Period I between 5250 and 4650 BCE, differently from Jarrige''s earlier dating pre-7000–5500 BCE [1]. The oldest known example of the lost-wax technique comes from a 6,000-year-old wheel-shaped copper amulet found at Mehrgarh [1]. The oldest ceramic figurines in South Asia were also found at Mehrgarh [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:146234", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-007: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/2eae8fd6/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/2eae8fd6/7000 BCE Old Well in Mehrgarh.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
