by Ptolemy around 100 AD. Metal objects and Celtic artifacts were discovered during road construction from 1838, and the Steinsburg Museum was built in 1929 on the saddle between the twin basalt peaks.'' -> ''The Oppidum Steinsburg is the remains of a major Celtic fortified settlement on the Kleiner Gleichberg in South Thuringia, Germany [1] [2]. Dating to approximately 2,500 years ago, it covered about 68 hectares [3] [4] [5]. Metal objects and Celtic artifacts were discovered during road construction from 1838, and the Steinsburg Museum was built on the saddle between the twin basalt peaks [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247347", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s005: period_name None -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247348", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s005: period_start None -> ''-500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:309342", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-004: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/2eeaa376/Steinsburg-022.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/2eeaa376/Kleiner_Gleichberg_Hildburghausen_Basaltfelder_(1_von_4).webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:357103", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-25p_fields-wd5-s002: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:357104", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-25p_fields-wd5-s002: period_start ''-500'' -> ''-1200''"}]'::jsonb),
