s and blades, some exceeding 30cm in length, representing continuous Pleistocene-era occupation when lower sea levels connected the island to the mainland [1][2].\" -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247359", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s005: period_name None -> ''1000 - 1500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247360", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s005: period_start None -> ''1000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:338493", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4wc:p4wc-4325:chunk-0001: description None -> \"Karta is the Kaurna name Karta Pintingga, meaning Island of the Dead, for the island known as Kangaroo Island in the state of South Australia [1]. Kangaroo Island was separated from mainland Australia around 10,000 years ago, when sea level rose after the last glacial period [1]. Stone tools and shell middens on the island show that Aboriginal people once lived on Kangaroo Island [1]. The island''s native population, sometimes called the Kartan people, disappeared from the archaeological record after the land became an island around 10,000 years ago [1]. The Kartan industry produced a large quantity of exceptionally large stone tools, probably more than 10,000 years ago, that were found on Karta [2].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347297", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_name ''1000 - 1500 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347298", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_start ''1000'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
