tangular enclosures with interior niches, kallancas, stairways, temples, cemeteries, and an intiwatana solar stone. Its origins trace to the Wari and Collao cultures, though most standing structures are Inca-period. Uniquely, its chullpa burial towers contain remains spanning Collao, Wari, and Inca periods, making it one of Peru\\''s few sites displaying such cultural continuity.'' -> ''Wamanmarka (Quechua \"population of eagles\") is a pre-Inca and Inca citadel at 3,600 m elevation near Santo Tomas in Cusco Region [1] [2] [3]. The complex features terraced circular plazas, rectangular enclosures with interior niches, kallancas, stairways, temples, cemeteries, and an intiwatana solar stone [2] [3] [4].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:236544", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s003: site_type None -> ''Archaeological site''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247363", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s005: period_name None -> ''1 - 500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247364", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s005: period_start None -> ''1''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347299", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347300", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_start ''1'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('3030e8d3-54a7-4014-ac0b-385aa42bed7b'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:3030e8d3-54a7-4014-ac0b-385aa42bed7b:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''5'' restored on Provincial Forum of Tarraco', '265f55a4c8c1d661467a90a12d56bfa1', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Archaeological site'', ''1 - 500 AD'') is shared by 15 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:27838", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0016:chunk-0001: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Archaeological site''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:82652", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2067:chunk-0001: description ''The Provincial Forum of Tarraco is a Roman archaeological complex in Tarragona, Catalonia, covering 18 hectares. Built from 73 AD by order of Emperor Vespasian, it served as the administrative and religious center of Hispania Tarraconensis until the 5th century. Its plaza, measuring 175m by 318m, was the largest public square ever constructed in the Roman Empire. Three sides featured a raised podium with a porticoed space resting on pilaster walls, behind which ran long vaults, several sections of which survive. The site forms part of the Archaeological Ensemble of Tarraco, inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2000 for its exceptional importance in Roman urban planning.'' -> ''The provincial forum of Tarraco is a Roman archaeological site in Tarragona, Catalonia, Spain, encompassing an area of 18 ha [1]. Together with other Roman remains in the city it makes the Archaeological Ensemble of Tarraco, which was listed in the UNESCO World Heritage Sites in 2000 [1]. It was built starting from 73 AD, by order of Emperor Vespasian, and remained in use until the 5th century [1]. The area consisted of a square dedicated to worship and a larger one for performances, in addition to the lower enclosure where the circus was located [1]. The worship enclosure was the seat of the concilium and was located in the highest part of the city, which today is occupied by the Cathedral, the Cathedral Square, and other buildings [1]. This was an impressive square measuring 153 meters by 136 meters, surrounded by a perimeter wall that was 9 meters high [1]. This wall supported the roof of a portico with columns, approximately 11 meters wide, which encircled the entire square [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:146274", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-007: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2a/Tarragona.Pretorio.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/3030e8d3/Plaza del Pallol - Arco de Entrada.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:309367", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-004: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/3030e8d3/Plaza del Pallol - Arco de Entrada.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/3030e8d3/Tarragona.Pretorio.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
