idence that Neanderthals were capable of symbolic artistic expression.'' -> ''The Cave of Maltravieso in Caceres, Extremadura, was discovered in 1951 when a quarry explosion exposed the cavity [1] [2]. It contains 71 hand stencils created by blowing pigment around hands placed on the walls, along with linear designs and animal paintings [1] [3].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247387", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s005: period_name None -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247388", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s005: period_start None -> ''-10000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:309406", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-004: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/32a2b3eb/Cueva de Maltravieso 01.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/32a2b3eb/EntradaMaltravieso.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('32e69e53-2a3c-4e9b-9622-5606d0ddefbc'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:32e69e53-2a3c-4e9b-9622-5606d0ddefbc:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''6'' restored on Ipf Mountain', 'aa1eb92ba7b4dec8027bb43773138884', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (''Fortification'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 10 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:82690", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2068:chunk-0001: description ''(1200-300 BC) A Celtic princely seat on a 668m conical hill near Bopfingen on the western edge of the Nordlinger Ries. First fortified during the Urnfield period, the Ipf reached its zenith in the late Hallstatt and early La Tene periods (6th-5th c. BC) when a 2.4 km rampart enclosed 30 hectares of slopes. Finds of Attic pottery, wine amphorae, and Mediterranean imports confirm far-reaching trade networks. Monumental burial mounds at nearby Osterholz and elite rectangular compounds provide further evidence of its role as a regional centre of power.'' -> \"The Ipf is a mostly treeless mountain (668 metres (2,192 ft) high), near Bopfingen, Ostalbkreis, Baden-Württemberg, Germany, with a prehistoric hill fort on its top [1]. The fort is situated on an isolated hill, with a flattened summit surrounded by a stone wall, ditch and large counterscarp (outer bank) [1]. The overall diameter is about 180 metres (590 ft) [1]. Extensive ramparts traverse the slopes to protect a large enclosed area and entranceway [1]. There is evidence of occupation from the Bronze Age (Urnfield culture) through the Iron Age to the early Celtic La Tene period, a span of almost a thousand years (1200 BC – 300 BC) [1]. The summit was already levelled, fortified and densely settled in the Urnfield period [1]. During the early Iron Age Hallstatt period and into the early La Tène period the Ipf was an important ''princely seat'' – a regional centre of power and aristocratic residence with long-distance trade connections, including with Greece and Italy [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:114985", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s002: site_type ''Fortress/citadel'' -> ''Fortification''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:146340", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-007: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/32e69e53/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/32e69e53/Der Ipf (Bopfingen).webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:309409", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-004: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/32e69e53/Der Ipf (Bopfingen).webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/32e69e53/Ipf_Wall.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
