ies shortly after the introduction of agriculture to Britain from continental Europe [1]. Although representing part of an architectural tradition of long barrow building widespread across Neolithic Europe, Smythe''s Megalith belonged to a localised regional variant produced in the vicinity of the River Medway, now known as the Medway Megaliths [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:96453", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s005: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:96454", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s005: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:96455", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s005: site_type ''Megalithic stones'' -> ''Tomb''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:146341", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-007: thumbnail_url \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Smythe''s_Megalith_field.jpg/400px-Smythe''s_Megalith_field.jpg\" -> \"/data/images/wiki/32f8606a/Smythe''s_Megalith_Illustration.webp\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:236558", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s003: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:236559", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s003: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}]'::jsonb),
