 10th century, the tower stands amid archaeological remains attesting to a broader Roman city. An interpretation centre now displays findings from excavations at the site.'' -> ''Torre de Almofala is a Roman monument in Figueira de Castelo Rodrigo, Portugal, originally constructed as a temple, believed to have been inspired by the Temple of Julius Caesar in the Roman Forum [1] [2]. The site is associated with the Roman settlement of Civitas Cobelcorum [3]. Later adapted as a military watchtower, the tower stands amid archaeological remains attesting to a broader Roman city [3] [4]. An interpretation centre now displays findings from excavations at the site [5].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247389", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s005: period_name None -> ''1 - 500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247390", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s005: period_start None -> ''43''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:339464", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "insert-2026-10-07-003-001: thumbnail_url None -> ''/data/images/wiki/3315cd99/Torre de Almofala (8).webp''"}]'::jsonb),
