-09-26d_fields-wd1-s005: period_start ''-500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247401", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s005: period_name None -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247402", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s005: period_start None -> ''-500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347317", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347318", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_start ''-500'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('34898f1b-9aa7-42e2-8847-4f78639c6e67'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:34898f1b-9aa7-42e2-8847-4f78639c6e67:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''6'' restored on Sohr Damb', 'd2e530396a78a74c278bad249c3d16dd', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (''Mound/tumulus'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 9 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:82746", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2070:chunk-0001: description \"Sohr Damb (''Red Mound'') is an archaeological site near Nal in central Balochistan, Pakistan, occupied from approximately 3800-2300 BC. The 4.5-hectare site gave its name to the prehistoric Amri-Nal culture and features a 13 m high mound with a cultural stratum less than 2 m deep. First discovered in 1903 and excavated by Harold Hargreaves in 1924, systematic excavations by the German Archaeological Institute since 2001 have revealed four periods of occupation. The site predates the Indus Valley civilization, featuring Togau, Kili Ghul Mohammad, and Kechi Beg pottery styles. Typical Nal pottery is buff-colored with complex geometric and figurative motifs in black, often with turquoise, yellow, and red pigments.\" -> ''Sohr Damb, c. 3800–2300 BC, is an archaeological site near Nal in central Balochistan, Pakistan that begins before the Indus Valley Civilization featuring Togau, Kili Ghul Mohammad, and Kechi Beg pottery styles [1]. It has also been known as Naal, Balochistan, and gave its name to the prehistoric Amri-Nal culture, which is attributed to the dual typesites of Amri and Nal [1]. The site extends around 4,5 hectares; the mound (mostly geologically formed) is 13 m high [1]. The excavations reveal four periods of occupation, and they could be further divided into several sub-periods [1]. The locality was first discovered in 1903 [1]. Since 2001, the site has been systematically excavated by the German Archaeological Institute and the Department of Archeology and Museums, Government of Pakistan [1]. Domesticated cattle bones are plentiful in the settlement, and bull figurines are also found [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97136", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Mound/tumulus''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:146371", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-007: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/13/Painted_Bowl_Nal_-_Sohr_Damb_BM.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/34898f1b/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:324998", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-005-001: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/34898f1b/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/34898f1b/Painted Bowl Nal - Sohr Damb BM.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('34898f1b-9aa7-42e2-8847-4f78639c6e67'::uuid, 'rarity_score', '33', '30', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:34898f1b-9aa7-42e2-8847-4f78639c6e67:rarity_score', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: rarity_score ''30'' restored on Sohr Damb', 'd2e530396a78a74c278bad249c3d16dd', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "rarity_score = 33 from mystery 7, cultural_influence 6"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:82746", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2070:chunk-0001: description \"Sohr Damb (''Red Mound'') is an archaeological site near Nal in central Balochistan, Pakistan, occupied from approximately 3800-2300 BC. The 4.5-hectare site gave its name to the prehistoric Amri-Nal culture and features a 13 m high mound with a cultural stratum less than 2 m deep. First discovered in 1903 and excavated by Harold Hargreaves in 1924, systematic excavations by the German Archaeological Institute since 2001 have revealed four periods of occupation. The site predates the Indus Valley civilization, featuring Togau, Kili Ghul Mohammad, and Kechi Beg pottery styles. Typical Nal pottery is buff-colored with complex geometric and figurative motifs in black, often with turquoise, yellow, and red pigments.\" -> ''Sohr Damb, c. 3800–2300 BC, is an archaeological site near Nal in central Balochistan, Pakistan that begins before the Indus Valley Civilization featuring Togau, Kili Ghul Mohammad, and Kechi Beg pottery styles [1]. It has also been known as Naal, Balochistan, and gave its name to the prehistoric Amri-Nal culture, which is attributed to the dual typesites of Amri and Nal [1]. The site extends around 4,5 hectares; the mound (mostly geologically formed) is 13 m high [1]. The excavations reveal four periods of occupation, and they could be further divided into several sub-periods [1]. The locality was first discovered in 1903 [1]. Since 2001, the site has been systematically excavated by the German Archaeological Institute and the Department of Archeology and Museums, Government of Pakistan [1]. Domesticated cattle bones are plentiful in the settlement, and bull figurines are also found [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97136", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Mound/tumulus''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:146371", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-007: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/13/Painted_Bowl_Nal_-_Sohr_Damb_BM.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/34898f1b/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:324998", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-005-001: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/34898f1b/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/34898f1b/Painted Bowl Nal - Sohr Damb BM.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('34898f1b-9aa7-42e2-8847-4f78639c6e67'::uuid, 'total_power', '24', '23', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:34898f1b-9aa7-42e2-8847-4f78639c6e67:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: total_power ''23'' restored on Sohr Damb', 'd2e530396a78a74c278bad249c3d16dd', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 24 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 8 + 2 + 6 + 7 + 1"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:82746", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2070:chunk-0001: description \"Sohr Damb (''Red Mound'') is an archaeological site near Nal in central Balochistan, Pakistan, occupied from approximately 3800-2300 BC. The 4.5-hectare site gave its name to the prehistoric Amri-Nal culture and features a 13 m high mound with a cultural stratum less than 2 m deep. First discovered in 1903 and excavated by Harold Hargreaves in 1924, systematic excavations by the German Archaeological Institute since 2001 have revealed four periods of occupation. The site predates the Indus Valley civilization, featuring Togau, Kili Ghul Mohammad, and Kechi Beg pottery styles. Typical Nal pottery is buff-colored with complex geometric and figurative motifs in black, often with turquoise, yellow, and red pigments.\" -> ''Sohr Damb, c. 3800–2300 BC, is an archaeological site near Nal in central Balochistan, Pakistan that begins before the Indus Valley Civilization featuring Togau, Kili Ghul Mohammad, and Kechi Beg pottery styles [1]. It has also been known as Naal, Balochistan, and gave its name to the prehistoric Amri-Nal culture, which is attributed to the dual typesites of Amri and Nal [1]. The site extends around 4,5 hectares; the mound (mostly geologically formed) is 13 m high [1]. The excavations reveal four periods of occupation, and they could be further divided into several sub-periods [1]. The locality was first discovered in 1903 [1]. Since 2001, the site has been systematically excavated by the German Archaeological Institute and the Department of Archeology and Museums, Government of Pakistan [1]. Domesticated cattle bones are plentiful in the settlement, and bull figurines are also found [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97136", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Mound/tumulus''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:146371", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-007: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/13/Painted_Bowl_Nal_-_Sohr_Damb_BM.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/34898f1b/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:324998", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-005-001: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/34898f1b/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/34898f1b/Painted Bowl Nal - Sohr Damb BM.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('34bbe345-3a0c-4b09-a81b-23ada293da19'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:34bbe345-3a0c-4b09-a81b-23ada293da19:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''5'' restored on Anta da Estria', 'f329e547b1a0298fbb111de60bb6f868', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Dolmen'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 22 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33589", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0022:chunk-0001: description ''(4500 - 3000 BC) The Anta da Estria is a megalithic dolmen situated between Belas and Queluz in the Lisbon District of Portugal. Based on radiocarbon datings of human remains, it dates to the Late Neolithic and early-Chalcolithic periods [1].'' -> ''The Anta da Estria is a megalithic dolmen situated between Belas and Queluz in the Lisbon District of Portugal [1]. Based on datings of human remains, it is believed to date back to the Late Neolithic and early-Chalcolithic eras (4000-2500 BC) [1]. The Anta da Estria, the Anta do Monte Abraão and the Anta da Pedra dos Mouros are collectively known as the Antas de Belas, and were first identified in the 1870s by Carlos Ribeiro [1]. Carlos Ribeiro, who carried out research in early 1875 and published his results in 1880, described the tomb as having a polygonal chamber, 2–5 metres wide and 2.75 metres high, approached by a corridor bordered by small slabs of limestone [1]. Ribeiro identified nine raised slabs but did not identify any stones that could have formed a roof [1]. Limited artifacts have been discovered at the site but many are thought to have been removed prior to the discovery by Ribeiro [1]. Twelve arrowheads were identified, together with two bone artifacts and three small, almost-complete pots [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97141", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97142", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: period_start ''-4500'' -> ''-3000''"}]'::jsonb),
