    ('353f7688-f230-41ef-a181-8bd9341448cf'::uuid, 'antiquity', '7', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:353f7688-f230-41ef-a181-8bd9341448cf:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''8'' restored on Brown Willy Cairns', '92fc5a1ef0ad6aeeb55713bb8606019f', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 7 from period_start -2500"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:82750", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2070:chunk-0001: description ''Brown Willy is the highest point in Cornwall at 420 m above sea level on Bodmin Moor, crowned by two prehistoric cairns on its summit. Dating studies of Bodmin Moor cairns place their construction in the early Bronze Age, with mean dates between 2162 and 1746 cal BC. The summit cairn has never been excavated, and folklore suggests an ancient Cornish king may lie entombed beneath it. Remains of around 40 hut platforms on the lower slopes show that nearly two-thirds were positioned with clear sightlines to the summit, suggesting the hilltop served as a ceremonial or communal space.'' -> ''The summit of Brown Willy is 1,378 feet above sea level, the highest point on Bodmin Moor and in the county of Cornwall [1]. Brown Willy Summit Cairn or Brown Willy North Cairn is a man made rock pile that sits alongside an Ordnance Survey triangulation station [1]. Brown Willy Summit Cairn has never been excavated and folklore suggests an ancient Cornish king may lie entombed underneath [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:116141", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-