edrock [1]. The Karahan tepe archaeological site covers almost 10 hectares, which increases by another five hectares if the quarries for the T-shaped columns are included [1]. The site contains numerous T-shaped pillars similar to those of Gobekli Tepe [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35730", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: site_type ''Archaeological site'' -> ''Megalithic stones''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:116169", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s003: site_type ''Megalithic stones'' -> ''Settlement''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:146947", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-008: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/3696e872/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/3696e872/Karahan_Tepe_head_(de-shadowed).webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:355613", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s001: site_type ''Settlement'' -> ''City/town/settlement''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('36d0cdd3-3586-46c5-bf1e-8cb4a70e1f6c'::uuid, 'mystery', '8', '9', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:36d0cdd3-3586-46c5-bf1e-8cb4a70e1f6c:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''9'' restored on Moorgate', 'cc9e70c03a3b0ac9fe26d901da02b49e', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 8 from (''Gate/archway/bridge'', ''1000 - 1500 AD'') is shared by 3 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:82778", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2071:chunk-0001: description \"Moorgate was the last gate added to London''s defensive wall, originally a small postern that became a full gate in 1415 under Lord Mayor Thomas Falconer. Unlike the city''s other six gates, it was medieval rather than Roman in origin, providing access to the marshy Moorfields north of the wall. Enlarged in 1472 and 1511, then rebuilt in 1673 to become the grandest of London''s gates, it was demolished in 1761 with its stone reused to widen London Bridge.\" -> \"Moorgate was one of the City of London''s northern gates in its defensive wall, the last to be built [1]. The gate was demolished in 1762, but gave its name to a major street, Moorgate [1]. The earliest descriptions of Moorgate date from the early 15th century, where it was described as only a postern in the London city wall [1]. Located between Bishopsgate and Cripplegate and leading to a marshy open space known as Moorfields, it was not one of the larger or more important of the city gates [1]. In 1415 an ordinance enacted that the old postern be demolished [1]. It was replaced with a newer and larger structure located farther to the west, which included a wooden gate to be shut at night [1]. This gate was enlarged again in 1472 and 1511, and then damaged in the Great Fire of London in 1666 [1]. Although the City gates had ceased to have any modern function apart from decoration, it was replaced along with Ludgate, Newgate, and Temple Bar with a stone gate in 1672 [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97184", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''1000 - 1500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97185", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: period_start ''1'' -> ''1415''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:146971", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-008: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/MoorHouse.jpg/400px-MoorHouse.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/36d0cdd3/Moorgate_Hollar.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
