    ('3807e5f9-2923-473d-930a-cc11b2b1138d'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:3807e5f9-2923-473d-930a-cc11b2b1138d:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''6'' restored on Carahunge Prehistoric Monument', 'b4e7aac86bc861b52a6f2509257ee731', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Megalithic stones'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 33 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:80790", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2008:chunk-0001: description ''Carahunge (also called Zorats Karer) is a prehistoric megalithic site near Sisian in Armenia\\''s Syunik Province, covering about 7 hectares at 1,770 metres elevation on a plateau above the Dar River canyon. The site comprises 223 basalt stones up to 3 metres tall and weighing up to 10 tons, with 84 featuring drilled holes. Its purpose is debated: radiophysicist Paris Herouni argued it is the world\\''s oldest astronomical observatory (potentially 7,500 years old), while University of Munich archaeologists identified it as a Bronze Age-to-Iron Age necropolis with stone lines representing a Hellenistic-period city wall. The name derives from Armenian \"car\" (stone) and \"hunge\" (sound), as the perforated stones produce whistling sounds in wind.'' -> \"Carahunge, also known as Zorats Karer, Dik-Dik Karer, Tsits Karer and Karenish, is a prehistoric archaeological site near the town of Sisian in the Syunik Province of Armenia [1]. The Carahunge site is at latitude 39° 34'' longitude 46° 01'' on a mountain plateau at an altitude of 1,770 metres and occupies an area of about 7 hectares on the left side of the canyon of the River Dar, a tributary of the river Vorotan [1]. The Carahunge monument consists of the central circle, the north arm, the south arm, the north–east alley, the chord across the circle, and separated standing stones [1]. The site is rich with stone settings, burial cists and standing stones [1]. In total, 223 stones have been identified [1]. The stones are basalt, eroded by time and covered with moss and lichen of many colours [1]. The heights of the stones above ground-level varies from 0.5 to 3 m, and their weights range up to 10 tons [1]. Zorats Karer was investigated in 2000 by archaeologists from the Institut für Vorderasiatische Archäologie, LMU Munich, as part of a field survey of prehistoric sites in southern Armenia [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97202", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97203", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: period_start ''1'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:141462", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-01a_fields-wd3-s001: period_name None -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:141463", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-01a_fields-wd3-s001: period_start None -> ''-2000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:147017", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-008: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/3807e5f9/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/3807e5f9/02 - 2025.8 Zorac Karer 001.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:309501", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-004: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/3807e5f9/02 - 2025.8 Zorac Karer 001.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/3807e5f9/Zorats_Karer_2008,_part_of_the_stone_circle.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
