 (AD 200-900) Maya city and became the focus of an archaeological project [1]. La Joyanca was occupied for well over 1000 years [1]. Dates from the site range from the Late Preclassic (200 BC) to the Terminal Classic/Postclassic (AD 1000) [1]. Population levels reached their height here during the Late Classic (AD 600-900); during this era the rulers of La Joyanca embarked upon an ambitious building project in two areas, dubbed the Plaza Principal and the Grupo Guacamaya [1]. The Grupo Guacamaya, a complex of vaulted rooms and corridors, may have served as their palace and thus the seat of local government [1]. The Grupo Guacamaya was briefly occupied by squatters in the 10th century, but the area was ultimately abandoned by AD 1000 [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97220", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97221", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: period_start ''-500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97222", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: site_type ''Temple complex'' -> ''City''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:147058", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-008: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/390afd0a/hero.webp'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:236623", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s003: period_name None -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:236624", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s003: period_start None -> ''-200''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:355616", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s001: site_type ''City'' -> ''City/town/settlement''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('39461539-85ee-47d9-9f64-b6a6587844ec'::uuid, 'antiquity', '5', '3', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:39461539-85ee-47d9-9f64-b6a6587844ec:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''3'' restored on Spartia Temple', 'daf6f5ffd229a7cce502959bc39b2048', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 5 from period_start None"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:82852", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2073:chunk-0001: description ''The Spartia Temple is an ancient Greek sanctuary at the foot of Spartia hill, north of Sesklo in the Magnesia region of Thessaly. A bronze omphalos phiale bearing an archaic inscription dedicated by Tilephilos to Heracles confirmed the site as the only excavated sanctuary of Heracles worship outside a city in the region of ancient Pherae. Discovered during natural gas pipeline construction in 1999, the site yielded bronze vases, weapons, figurines, black-figured roof tiles, and a Hellenistic phase built atop the earlier archaic sanctuary. Finds span from the Archaic through Byzantine periods.'' -> ''The Spartia temple is an ancient Greek cult site excavated at the foot of the hill of Spartia, north of the village of Sesklo, in the Magnesia regional unit of Greece [1]. An inscribed dedication suggests the worship there of the Greek mythological figures Heracles and Alcmene [1]. Items recovered include bronze vases, objects of lead and clay, weapons, iron tools, and figurines [1]. Some specific artifacts named are clay metopes with yellow veneer, spearheads, and part of the arm of a marble statue [1]. One important artifact is a bronze phiale in excellent condition with the inscription \"Tilephilos dedicated me to Herakles\" in the archaic Greek alphabet [1]. This indicates the presence of a Herakles cult, which is linked to Pherae and is documented in the region through Hellenistic-era inscriptions [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:116195", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s003: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:116196", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s003: period_start ''-1500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247437", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s005: period_name None -> ''1 - 500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247438", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s005: period_start None -> ''43''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348084", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348085", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_start ''43'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
