ale Moor in the North York Moors, along the route of the Lyke Wake Walk [1]. Though now used as a parish boundary marker, it is believed to date from pre-Roman times [1]. The stone is heavily weathered and bears an engraved ''E 1841'' date on its south face [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247439", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s005: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247440", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s005: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348086", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348087", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('3962491a-58ec-4e7c-a1be-3c456f9818a6'::uuid, 'antiquity', '6', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:3962491a-58ec-4e7c-a1be-3c456f9818a6:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''8'' restored on Longstone of Minchinhampton', 'd24097c68f2f6362a6bcae9a20e787e9', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 6 from period_start -1600"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33639", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0023:chunk-0001: description ''A late Neolithic or early Bronze Age standing stone on Minchinhampton Common in Gloucestershire, standing 2.3 m high and made of oolitic limestone. The stone is perforated by two large and several smaller natural holes caused by weathering, and is believed to be the closing slab from a destroyed burial chamber or portal dolmen. Local folklore held that the holes had curative powers, and children with whooping cough or rickets were passed through them. A fallen stone 11 m to the southwest, now built into a dry stone wall, may have formed a pair with the Longstone. It is a Scheduled Ancient Monument.'' -> ''The Longstone of Minchinhampton is a standing stone on Minchinhampton Common, Minchinhampton in Gloucestershire, England [1]. The Longstone is a large piece of oolitic limestone standing upright in a field, known as Longstone Field, 1 mile east of the Minchinhampton village [1]. It is 7.5 feet high and 6 feet wide, with natural holes through its 15 inches width [1]. Nearby, 12 yards to the south west, there is a fallen standing stone which has been built into a dry stone wall, and may have once formed a stone pair with the Longstone [1]. The area has numerous barrows and near the Longstone ornaments, flint and arrowheads have all been found [1]. The stone was believed to be able to cure infants of maladies such as measles and whooping cough, by passing the child through one of the natural holes [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97227", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97228", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: period_start ''-1000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247781", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s006: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247782", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s006: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:357141", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-25p_fields-wd5-s003: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:357142", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-25p_fields-wd5-s003: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-1600''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('3962491a-58ec-4e7c-a1be-3c456f9818a6'::uuid, 'total_power', '28', '30', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:3962491a-58ec-4e7c-a1be-3c456f9818a6:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: total_power ''30'' restored on Longstone of Minchinhampton', 'd24097c68f2f6362a6bcae9a20e787e9', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 28 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 6 + 8 + 8 + 5 + 1"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33639", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0023:chunk-0001: description ''A late Neolithic or early Bronze Age standing stone on Minchinhampton Common in Gloucestershire, standing 2.3 m high and made of oolitic limestone. The stone is perforated by two large and several smaller natural holes caused by weathering, and is believed to be the closing slab from a destroyed burial chamber or portal dolmen. Local folklore held that the holes had curative powers, and children with whooping cough or rickets were passed through them. A fallen stone 11 m to the southwest, now built into a dry stone wall, may have formed a pair with the Longstone. It is a Scheduled Ancient Monument.'' -> ''The Longstone of Minchinhampton is a standing stone on Minchinhampton Common, Minchinhampton in Gloucestershire, England [1]. The Longstone is a large piece of oolitic limestone standing upright in a field, known as Longstone Field, 1 mile east of the Minchinhampton village [1]. It is 7.5 feet high and 6 feet wide, with natural holes through its 15 inches width [1]. Nearby, 12 yards to the south west, there is a fallen standing stone which has been built into a dry stone wall, and may have once formed a stone pair with the Longstone [1]. The area has numerous barrows and near the Longstone ornaments, flint and arrowheads have all been found [1]. The stone was believed to be able to cure infants of maladies such as measles and whooping cough, by passing the child through one of the natural holes [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97227", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97228", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: period_start ''-1000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247781", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s006: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247782", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s006: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:357141", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-25p_fields-wd5-s003: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:357142", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-25p_fields-wd5-s003: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-1600''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('3963cd3e-5203-4506-a8a5-785aea857b71'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:3963cd3e-5203-4506-a8a5-785aea857b71:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Gamelands Stone Circle', 'b5f999d79757dac9e05693dfc6958e1c', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (''Stone circle'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 8 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:80792", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2008:chunk-0001: description \"Prehistoric embanked stone circle at the foot of Knott Scar near Orton in Cumbria, England, dating from the Late Neolithic to Early Bronze Age (c. 2900-1600 BC). The largest stone circle in Cumbria, it forms a flattened oval approximately 45 by 38 metres with around 40 recumbent stones, all of local Shap pink granite except one of limestone. A community archaeology project identified remnants of the monument''s former bank and a palaeochannel running through the circle. It is the only complete example of an embanked stone circle in Cumbria and a designated Scheduled Monument.\" -> ''Gamelands stone circle (or Orton stone circle) is a stone circle at the foot of Knott Hill in Cumbria, England [1]. Gamelands stone circle lies between the village of Orton and the hamlet of Raisbeck [1]. It is an oval enclosure of around 40 large stones together with three smaller stones [1]. The stones are set into a slight bank [1]. The stones are all below one metre in height and are arranged in an oval of 42 metres by 35 metres [1]. Around 1862 some of the stones were buried or blasted [1]. Ploughing uncovered two worked flints and a probable cist-slab [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97229", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97230", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:147069", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-008: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/3963cd3e/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/3963cd3e/Gamelands stone circle 1.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247783", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s006: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247784", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s006: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:321378", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-004-001: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/3963cd3e/Gamelands stone circle 1.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/3963cd3e/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:325002", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-005-001: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/3963cd3e/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/3963cd3e/Gamelands stone circle 2.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
