ngravings on the Monolith of Jinkiori have Amazonian origins and include abstract representations possibly linked to cosmological beliefs and ayahuasca use [1]. The carvings may have been created by ancestors of the Huachipaeri, an indigenous group still living in the Kosñipata valley [1]. Features include a small basin possibly used for preparing chicha and a distinctive key-shaped engraving [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247789", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s006: period_name None -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247790", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s006: period_start None -> ''-10000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348090", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_name ''< 4500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348091", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_start ''-10000'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('39a4d056-86ae-40f4-9f64-b78c23e4730d'::uuid, 'antiquity', '7', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:39a4d056-86ae-40f4-9f64-b78c23e4730d:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''8'' restored on Cursus Barrows', 'c4847f35c18ba17d180025912e25a2e9', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 7 from period_start -2500"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:82858", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2073:chunk-0001: description ''(3300 BC) The Cursus Barrows is a Neolithic and Bronze Age round barrow cemetery located mostly south of the western end of the Stonehenge Cursus in Wiltshire, England [1]. The cemetery contains around 18 barrows scattered along an east-to-west ridge, though some mounds are no longer visible [1]. The barrows can be seen just north of the route between the Stonehenge Visitor Centre and Stonehenge [1].'' -> ''The Cursus Barrows is the name given to a Neolithic and Bronze Age round barrow cemetery lying mostly south of the western end of the Stonehenge Cursus, in Wiltshire [1]. The cemetery contains around 18 barrows scattered along an east-to-west ridge, although some of the mounds are no longer visible [1]. The round barrow cemetery extends 1200 metres east-to-west along a ridge and measures 250 metres wide [1]. It comprises the round barrows recorded as Amesbury 43 to 56 and Winterbourne Stoke 28 to 30, plus the Fargo hengiform [1]. Many of the barrows were excavated by Richard Colt Hoare in the early 19th century [1]. Amesbury 43 and 44 are a bell barrow and a twin bell barrow forming the eastern part of the Cursus round barrow cemetery [1]. When excavated in the early 19th century Amesbury 54 revealed a primary burial with a beaker, flint spear head and polished hammer head [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97237", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97238", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247791", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s006: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247792", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s006: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:309540", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-004: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/39a4d056/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/39a4d056/Stonehenge_tumulus.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348092", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348093", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-2500''"}]'::jsonb),
