oduction centre. The site was destroyed by an earthquake around 1200 BC.'' -> ''Midea was one of the three principal Mycenaean centres in the Argolid alongside Mycenae and Tiryns, occupying the highest Mycenaean acropolis at 270m elevation [1]. The citadel is enclosed by massive cyclopean walls measuring 450m long, 5-7m wide, and up to 7m high, with two monumental gates [1] [2]. Excavations from 1939 to 2009 uncovered Linear B inscriptions, ivory inlays, and a steatite mould for gold and glass beads, confirming it as an administrative and craft production centre [1] [2] [3]. The site was destroyed around 1200 BC [2].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('3c579791-33d8-46bd-90da-1551035cc419'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:3c579791-33d8-46bd-90da-1551035cc419:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''6'' restored on Carrawburgh', '0fb11dba31083c140177066ad3fdd812', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Fort'', ''1 - 500 AD'') is shared by 26 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:83250", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2074:chunk-0001: description \"Carrawburgh (Roman name Brocolitia) is an auxiliary fort on Hadrian''s Wall built around 130 AD to fill a gap between the forts at Housesteads and Chesters. The 3.5-acre fort housed approximately 500 soldiers from auxiliary units including cohorts from Aquitania, Batavia, and Tungria. Its most notable features are three Roman sanctuaries beyond the southwest corner: a Temple of Mithras built c. 200 AD and rebuilt four times before falling into disuse around 350 AD, and Coventina''s Well, a shrine to a local water goddess where 1876 excavations recovered roughly 16,000 coins, ten altars, and sculptural fragments. The fort remains largely unexcavated, preserving significant subsurface remains for future archaeological research.\" -> \"Carrawburgh is a settlement in Northumberland [1]. In Roman times, it was the site of a 3+1⁄2-acre auxiliary fort on Hadrian''s Wall called Brocolitia, Procolita, or Brocolita [1]. Hadrian''s Wall was built from 122 AD and most of the wall forts a few years later [1]. Brocolitia was built a few years later still probably to fill a slightly longer gap between forts at Housesteads, 5.2 miles away, and Chesters, 3.5 miles away [1]. A date of 130 has been suggested from a fragmentary inscription now in the Chesters Museum [1]. The vicus (civilian settlement) was just outside the west side of the fort and covered about 4 hectares [1]. The remains of three Roman sanctuaries have been discovered in the low-lying marshy ground beyond the fort''s south-west corner [1]. Nearest to the fort, about 80 metres from its south-west corner, are the remains of an early 3rd century mithraeum, i.e. a temple of the mystery cult of the Roman god Mithras [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:116221", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s003: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Fort''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:147151", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-008: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/3c579791/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/3c579791/1015914 wall fort at Carrawburgh, Mithraeum Simonburn 20240525 0383.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:309579", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-004: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/3c579791/1015914 wall fort at Carrawburgh, Mithraeum Simonburn 20240525 0383.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/3c579791/Brocolitiamithraeum.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
