own occupied the site under the Odrysian Kingdom. After Roman annexation around 45 AD, it was renamed Augusta and became one of the most important cities in the province of Thracia. Emperor Diocletian visited in 293 AD and renamed it Diocletianopolis. In the 4th century, massive fortification walls stretching 2.3 km were built against Gothic threats, reaching up to 13 metres high -- among the best-preserved Roman walls in Eastern Europe. The city declined and was largely abandoned by the 7th century.'' -> ''Hisarya is a spa town in Plovdiv Province, Bulgaria [1]. By the 5th century BC a Thracian town occupied the site under the Odrysian Kingdom [2] [3]. After Roman annexation, it was renamed Augusta and became one of the most important cities in the province of Thracia [2] [4]. Emperor Diocletian visited in 293 AD and renamed it Diocletianopolis [1] [2]. In the 4th century, massive fortification walls stretching 2.3 km were built against Gothic threats [2]. The city declined and was largely abandoned by the 7th century [2] [5].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('3d1c5bc3-f16b-4459-afd5-0d4034875aa2'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:3d1c5bc3-f16b-4459-afd5-0d4034875aa2:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Stonehenge Cursus', '1877b96d268199e0b8874078798627df', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Earthwork'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 13 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:83278", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2075:chunk-0001: description \"Stonehenge Cursus is a large Neolithic earthwork on Salisbury Plain, Wiltshire, England, dating to 3630-3375 BCE—several centuries before Stonehenge''s earliest phase around 3000 BC[1]. It stretches roughly 3 km long and 100-150 m wide. The earthwork, along with adjacent barrows and the Lesser Cursus, forms part of the National Trust''s Stonehenge Landscape and is within the Stonehenge and Avebury World Heritage Site[1].\" -> ''The Stonehenge Cursus is a large Neolithic cursus monument on Salisbury plain, near to Stonehenge in Wiltshire, England [1]. Radiocarbon dating of a red deer antler pick discovered at the bottom of the western terminal ditch suggests that the Stonehenge Cursus was first constructed between 3630 and 3375 BCE [1]. It is just under 3 km long, and is roughly 100 m wide [1]. Because of a slight difference in the alignment of its north and south ditches, it widens to a point nearly 150 m near its western end [1]. It is roughly aligned east–west and is oriented toward the sunrise on the spring and autumn equinoxes [1]. There is a Bronze Age round barrow inside the western end of the enclosure, and a large Neolithic long barrow was constructed at its east terminal [1]. William Stukeley was the first antiquarian to identify and record the Stonehenge Cursus, although he incorrectly assumed it to be Roman in origin [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:147196", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-008: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2a/Cursus_East.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/3d1c5bc3/Cursus_East.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
