und barrow inside the western end of the enclosure, and a large Neolithic long barrow was constructed at its east terminal [1]. William Stukeley was the first antiquarian to identify and record the Stonehenge Cursus, although he incorrectly assumed it to be Roman in origin [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:147196", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-008: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2a/Cursus_East.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/3d1c5bc3/Cursus_East.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
