et in height, making this the second tallest menhir in the United Kingdom after the 25 feet tall Rudston Monolith in the East Riding of Yorkshire [1]. In 2026, scientists at Curtin University and the University of York used isotopic fingerprinting to show that the stones originated at Brimham Rocks, about 11 miles to the west [1]. The study also found that the Battle Cross at Aldborough has the same geological fingerprint as the surviving Arrows, supporting its identification as a repurposed fourth stone from the original monument [1]. The stones are part of a prehistoric complex concentrated around the River Ure [1]. The name is mentioned by the antiquary John Aubrey, who visited and drew the stones in 1687 [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97290", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97291", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: period_start ''-3500'' -> ''-2000''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('3d4fbf16-e80f-42c3-853c-4a22206dee24'::uuid, 'antiquity', '5', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:3d4fbf16-e80f-42c3-853c-4a22206dee24:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''8'' restored on Dun Troddan', '911db2bb33a398d2df3b0b8b63c58ab7', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 5 from period_start -550"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33687", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0024:chunk-0001: description \"Dun Troddan is an Iron Age Broch located about 5 km (3.1 mi) southeast of the village of Glenelg in Highland, Scotland [1]. It stands on a level rock platform north of the Abhainn a'' Ghlinne Bhig in the lower reaches of Gleann Beag [1]. One of the best-preserved Broch in Scotland, it features massive, double-walled circular construction with an inner diameter of approximately 9 meters [1]. Brochs are the most spectacular of a complex class of roundhouse buildings found throughout Atlantic Scotland [1].\" -> ''Dun Troddan (Scottish Gaelic: Dùn Trodan) is an iron-age broch located about 5 kilometres southeast of the village of Kirkton,Glenelg, Highland, in Scotland [1]. Dun Troddan stands on a level rock platform north of the Abhainn a’ Ghlinne Bhig, in the lower reaches of Gleann Beag [1]. Dun Troddan was first sketched in about 1720, when it was still an intact tower [1]. The broch was visited by Thomas Pennant in 1772, and it was still a substantial structure, although it had lost the upper gallery by this time [1]. The broch consists of a drystone tower which measures around 17.5 metres (57 ft) in diameter, and currently stands to a maximum height of 7 metres (23 ft) [1]. The external walls are 4.5 metres (15 ft) thick at the base [1]. The broch has features now missing from Dun Telve; these include a number of postholes in the floor and a hearth [1]. Built into the hearth is a broken quern-stone [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97292", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97293", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: period_start ''-2000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:147205", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-008: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/3d4fbf16/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/3d4fbf16/Dun Troddan - Intramural stairway.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247817", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s006: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247818", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s006: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:309595", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-004: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/3d4fbf16/Dun Troddan - Intramural stairway.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/3d4fbf16/Dun Troddan - geograph.org.uk - 8424.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348116", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348117", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-550''"}]'::jsonb),
