 gorge near the village of Burrington, on the north side of the Mendip Hills Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty, in North Somerset, England [1]. Burrington Combe is a gorge through the limestone hills although there is now no river running through it [1]. Various cave entrances are exposed which have been occupied by humans for over 10,000 years, with a hillfort being built beside the combe in the Iron Age [1]. Archaeological discoveries of early cemeteries demonstrate human occupation of the combe and its caves from the Bronze Age with some evidence of occupation during the Upper Palaeolithic period [1]. The combe contains the entrances to many of the caves of the Mendip Hills, including Aveline''s Hole, Sidcot Swallet and Goatchurch Cavern [1]. Above the combe on its eastern side is the site of an Iron Age univallate hill fort known as Burrington Camp [1]. It is around 100 metres by 80 metres and includes Romano-British elements [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:116248", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s003: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:116249", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s003: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:147215", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-008: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/3d8cb484/hero.webp'' -> \"/data/images/wiki/3d8cb484/Acting all ''goat-like'' - panoramio.webp\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247825", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s006: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247826", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s006: period_start None -> ''-4500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:333754", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-009-001: thumbnail_url \"/data/images/wiki/3d8cb484/Acting all ''goat-l