 in the 4th century when Metz was an important Gallo-Roman city [1]. In the 7th century, the structure was converted into a church, becoming the chapel of a Benedictine nunnery [1]. A new nave was constructed in the 11th century with further interior renovations [1]. The building became a warehouse, and remained so after being declared a historical monument in 1909 [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:147741", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-009: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/3dfa5ceb/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/3dfa5ceb/St pierre aux nonnains 1511912.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:309611", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-004: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/3dfa5ceb/St pierre aux nonnains 1511912.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/3dfa5ceb/Metz_-_Eglise_Saint-Pierre-aux-Nonnais_-_Vue_du_côté_Est.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
