.\" -> \"Rock art site dating to the 4th-1st millennia BC on Gapijiq peak in the Lesser Caucasus near Ordubad, Nakhchivan, Azerbaijan [1] [2]. Thousands of petroglyphs depict deer, goats, bulls, leopards, birds, human figures, carriages, and spiritual rituals, reflecting the religion and ideology of ancient peoples [1] [2] [3]. Formal investigation began in 1968 [1] [3]. The name Gamigaya means ''stone ship,'' linked to a local flood myth about Noah''s Ark [1] [2]. The site is on Azerbaijan''s UNESCO Tentative List [2] [4].\""}]'::jsonb),
