f side chambers and an end chamber [1]. Stoney Littleton Long Barrow was probably constructed around 3500 BC [1]. The tomb was first opened around 1760 by a local farmer to obtain stone for road building [1]. The site was excavated by John Skinner and Richard Hoare in 1816–17, along with a labourer Zebedee Weston [1]. The excavation revealed the bones (some burned) of several individuals [1]. The passage and entrance are roughly aligned towards the midwinter sunrise [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:147762", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-009: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2a/Stoney_Littleton_Long_Barrow_2015_11.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/3f06d44f/Stoney Littleton Long Barrow (35987410634).webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:309637", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-004: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/3f06d44f/Stoney Littleton Long Barrow (35987410634).webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/3f06d44f/Stoney_Littleton_Long_Barrow_2015_11.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
