fied Celtiberian settlement endured Roman sieges by Lucullus (151 BC), Lepidus (137-136 BC), and Pompey during the Sertorian wars. Cathedral square excavations revealed superimposed occupation layers from the Sertorian period through Constantine''s reign and medieval reoccupation. Finds include Roman mosaics -- notably a Medusa head now in Madrid''s National Archaeological Museum -- gold and silver hoards with native denarii, and numerous inscriptions.\" -> \"Palencia, in Castile and Leon, Spain, occupies the site of ancient Pallantia, chief town of the Vaccaei people, mentioned by Strabo and Ptolemy [1] [2]. The fortified Celtiberian settlement endured Roman sieges by Lucullus (151 BC), Lepidus (137-136 BC), and Pompey during the Sertorian wars [1] [2]. Cathedral square excavations revealed superimposed occupation layers from the Sertorian period through Constantine''s reign and medieval reoccupation [2]. Finds include Roman mosaics -- notably a Medusa head now in Madrid''s National Archaeological Museum -- gold and silver hoards with native denarii, and numerous inscriptions [1] [2] [3].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348144", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348145", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_start ''-500'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
