vaulted tombs. Heinrich Schliemann''s 1876 excavations uncovered Grave Circle A with its celebrated gold death masks. Designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1999 alongside nearby Tiryns.\" -> \"A major Bronze Age citadel in the Argolid, northeastern Peloponnese, Greece, and the center of the Mycenaean civilization that dominated the eastern Mediterranean [1] [2]. At its peak, it was a powerful palatial center controlling much of southern Greece [1] [2]. The site features the iconic Lion Gate, Cyclopean Walls, and the Treasury of Atreus, one of the largest Mycenaean vaulted tombs [2] [3]. Heinrich Schliemann''s 1876 excavations uncovered Grave Circle A with its celebrated gold death masks [4]. Designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1999 alongside nearby Tiryns [2].\""}]'::jsonb),
    ('411cacb0-6312-4465-81f2-76417d209c17'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:411cacb0-6312-4465-81f2-76417d209c17:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''6'' restored on Kit Hill', 'bfaf1daa967337d92610e9127b4c3060', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Barrow'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 34 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28893", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0211:chunk-0001: site_type ''Fortress/citadel'' -> ''Fortification''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:32763", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0003:chunk-0001: description \"Kit Hill is a Neolithic to Early Bronze Age hillfort in southeast Cornwall, England, standing 334 metres high and dominating the landscape between Callington and the River Tamar[1]. The hill''s name derives from the Old English ''kit'' meaning kite, a reference to birds of prey still visible on its slopes today[1]. One of five Marilyn hills in Cornwall, it was given to the public as Kit Hill Country Park in 1985 to mark the birth of Prince William[2].\" -> \"Kit Hill, at 334 metres high, dominates the area between Callington and the River Tamar in southeast Cornwall, England [1]. The word ''Kit'' comes from Old English for kite, a reference to birds of prey (and not specifically the red kite) [1]. Kit Hill is the highest point in the Tamar Valley Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty [1]. The hill was formed in the same way as nearby Bodmin Moor by the intrusion of magma into overlying sedimentary rocks [1]. This caused the formation of many mineral deposits that were mined extensively in the 18th and early 19th centuries [1]. East Kit Hill Mine was worked from 1855 to 1909 [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:116289", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s003: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:116290", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s003: period_start ''-4500'' -> ''-3000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:116291", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s003: site_type ''Fortification'' -> ''Barrow''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:116292", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s003: source_url ''https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kit_Hill'' -> ''https://www.megalithic.co.uk/article.php?sid=61289''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:147832", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-009: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/411cacb0/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/411cacb0/Chimney on Kit Hill (3120).webp''"}]'::jsonb),
