    ('412a1f97-7dfd-4624-b6ec-2813217f39bf'::uuid, 'mystery', '3', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:412a1f97-7dfd-4624-b6ec-2813217f39bf:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Pločnik - Archaeological Site', 'be754ab9df2698284b6f148da14c6649', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 3 from (''City/town/settlement'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 105 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:83372", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2078:chunk-0001: description ''Plocnik is a Neolithic Vinca culture settlement covering 120 hectares along the Toplica River in southern Serbia, near Prokuplje. Occupied from approximately 5500 to 4700 BC when it was destroyed by fire, the site yielded groundbreaking metallurgical evidence including a copper axe dated to 5500 BC -- pushing back the start of the Copper Age by 500 years. A tin bronze foil dated to c. 4650 BC, the oldest known in the world, was also discovered here. Excavations since 1927 have uncovered around 50 copper artifacts, 60 pottery forms, a 25-square-metre metal workshop, figurines, clay toys, and a sophisticated smelting furnace with chimney ventilation.'' -> ''Pločnik (archaeological site) is located in Pločnik, Prokuplje village in the Toplica District of Serbia [1]. A 120 hectare settlement belonging to the Neolithic Vinča culture existed on the site from 5500 BCE until it was destroyed by fire in 4700 BCE [1]. The Vinča houses at Pločnik had stoves and special holes specifically for rubbish, and the dead were buried in cemeteries [1]. People slept on woollen mats and fur and made clothes of wool, flax and leather [1]. The figurines found not only represent deities but many show the daily life of the inhabitants while crude pottery finds appear to have been made by children [1]. Women are depicted in short tops and skirt wearing jewellery [1]. In 2008, a copper axe was found at Pločnik that was dated to 5,500 BC [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:147835", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-009: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/31/%D0%9F%D0%BB%D0%BE%D1%87%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BA_01.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/412a1f97/Плочник_01.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
