aintings dating back to the Upper Paleolithic [1]. The Rouffignac cave was mentioned in 1575 by François de Belleforest [1]. Famous archeologists such as Henri Breuil, André Glory and Édouard-Alfred Martel had visited the cave in the early 20th century, but it was only in 1956 that Louis-René Nougier and Romain Robert, two prehistorians from the Pyrenees, rediscovered and confirmed the cave art [1]. The artistic representations in the cave were mainly executed as engravings or black contour drawings [1]. Reconnaissance excavations in front of the entry of the cave found traces of human occupations (several fireplaces, animal bones and stone tools) dating back to the Mesolithic and to the Neolithic [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:98006", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s007: period_name ''< 4500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:98007", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s007: period_start ''-11000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:147882", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-009: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/38/Grotte_de_Rouffignac_-_Entr%C3%A9e_-_20090924.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/424a8ef0/Grotte_de_Rouffignac_-_Entrée_-_20090924.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:236697", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s003: period_name None -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:236698", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s003: period_start None -> ''-11000''"}]'::jsonb),
