épartement, contains over 250 engravings and cave paintings dating back to the Upper Paleolithic [1]. The Rouffignac cave was mentioned in 1575 by François de Belleforest [1]. Famous archeologists such as Henri Breuil, André Glory and Édouard-Alfred Martel had visited the cave in the early 20th century, but it was only in 1956 that Louis-René Nougier and Romain Robert, two prehistorians from the Pyrenees, rediscovered and confirmed the cave art [1]. The artistic representations in the cave were mainly executed as engravings or black contour drawings [1]. Reconnaissance excavations in front of the entry of the cave found traces of human occupations (several fireplaces, animal bones and stone tools) dating back to the Mesolithic and to the Neolithic [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:98006", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s007: period_name ''< 4500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:98007", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s007: period_start ''-11000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:147882", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-009: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/38/Grotte_de_Rouffignac_-_Entr%C3%A9e_-_20090924.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/424a8ef0/Grotte_de_Rouffignac_-_Entrée_-_20090924.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:236697", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s003: period_name None -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:236698", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s003: period_start None -> ''-11000''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4297c23a-44ec-49e3-a34f-7715a16d7620'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:4297c23a-44ec-49e3-a34f-7715a16d7620:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''7'' restored on Five Wells', '42da6966a6b617719296b62e7d39401c', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Tomb'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 12 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:27968", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0038:chunk-0001: site_type ''Mound/tumulus'' -> ''Tomb''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:83398", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2079:chunk-0001: description ''Five Wells is a Neolithic chambered tomb on Taddington Moor in the Derbyshire Peak District, reputed to be the highest megalithic tomb in Britain. Built between approximately 3400-2400 BC, it originally consisted of two limestone slab chambers set back-to-back, covered by a circular mound 56 feet in diameter. Thomas Bateman first excavated the site in 1846, recovering jawbones from twelve individuals along with flint arrowheads. Later excavations in 1865 and 1899-1901 uncovered Neolithic plain ware pottery, Peterborough ware sherds, and additional skeletal remains spanning from the Early Neolithic into the Bronze Age.'' -> ''Five Wells is a Neolithic chambered tomb between the villages of Chelmorton and Taddington on Taddington Moor in the Derbyshire Peak District in England [1]. The tomb is a protected scheduled ancient monument [1]. Three stones mark the main chamber, which has been dramatically reduced; a second less well-preserved chamber is to the west [1]. The burial mound is over 20 metres in diameter and was first excavated by the local archaeologist Thomas Bateman in 1846 [1]. The chambers have paved floors [1]. Bateman discovered the remains of at least twelve human skeletons [1]. Subsequent excavations found further human remains, pottery and flint tools in the chambers and passages and a separate cist within the mound [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:147885", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-009: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/4297c23a/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/4297c23a/Five Wells is a Neolithic chambered tomb, Derbyshire - 53818056417.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:333775", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-009-001: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/4297c23a/Five Wells is a Neolithic chambered tomb, Derbyshire - 53818056417.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/4297c23a/hero.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
