ond less well-preserved chamber is to the west [1]. The burial mound is over 20 metres in diameter and was first excavated by the local archaeologist Thomas Bateman in 1846 [1]. The chambers have paved floors [1]. Bateman discovered the remains of at least twelve human skeletons [1]. Subsequent excavations found further human remains, pottery and flint tools in the chambers and passages and a separate cist within the mound [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:147885", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-009: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/4297c23a/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/4297c23a/Five Wells is a Neolithic chambered tomb, Derbyshire - 53818056417.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:333775", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-009-001: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/4297c23a/Five Wells is a Neolithic chambered tomb, Derbyshire - 53818056417.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/4297c23a/hero.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
