    ('4541e162-c69b-49d8-a4db-04e66089e8fd'::uuid, 'mystery', '3', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:4541e162-c69b-49d8-a4db-04e66089e8fd:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Solnitsata', '96398a3aaf731e4e89410b24c15ce6ef', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 3 from (''City/town/settlement'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 105 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:83434", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2080:chunk-0001: description ''Prehistoric proto-city (4700–4200 BC) near Provadia, Bulgaria, Solnitsata is the oldest salt production center in continental Europe and the first urban center in prehistoric Europe. This fortified stone settlement featured a citadel, inner and outer city, and pottery production. Massive stone walls protected salt—a commodity worth more than gold in antiquity. Despite an estimated population of only 350, archaeologist Vassil Nikolov argues it meets criteria as a prehistoric proto-city. [1][2]'' -> \"Solnitsata was a prehistoric town located in present-day Bulgaria, near the modern city of Provadia [1]. It is the oldest salt production center in continental Europe (5500‑4200 BC) [1]. The settlement was walled to protect the salt, a crucial commodity in antiquity [1]. Salt production drove Solnitsata''s economy, and the town is believed to have supplied salt throughout the Balkans [1]. Researchers suggest that some of the residential buildings in the settlement were two-story [1]. The town is believed to have been destroyed by an earthquake [1].\""}, 