yed by an earthquake [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:147949", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-009: thumbnail_url ''https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solnitsata#/media/File:Provadijska_Solnitsa_8.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/4541e162/Provadijska Solnitsa 1.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4561c396-b590-47a8-b6da-c177870361bb'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:4561c396-b590-47a8-b6da-c177870361bb:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''6'' restored on Roman Column, York', '43f6264f82f14507a1d458eeca42ba2a', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (None, ''1 - 500 AD'') is shared by 10 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:83436", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2080:chunk-0001: description \"A 1st-century Roman column standing in Minster Yard, York, originally part of the headquarters basilica of Eboracum. Built around AD 100 by Legio IX Hispana and later reused by Legion VI, it was one of sixteen columns supporting the basilica roof. The gritstone column stands approximately 9.5 metres high in Tuscan architectural order. Discovered beneath York Minster during 1969 excavations, it was re-erected in 1971 to mark the 1900th anniversary of York''s founding. It was likely inside this basilica that Constantine was proclaimed Emperor in AD 306.\" -> \"The Roman column is a surviving element of the Roman presence in York, England, located just south of York Minster [1]. Dating from the early 2nd century AD, it originally formed part of the headquarters building (principia) of Eboracum, the Roman name for York [1]. The column was uncovered during archaeological excavations beneath York Minster in the late 1960s [1]. These excavations were prompted by structural subsidence and restoration work on the cathedral [1]. To mark the 1,900th anniversary of York''s founding, the column was reassembled and erected in Minster Yard in 1972 [1]. The column is approximately 9.5 metres in height [1]. It is made up of eight cylindrical drums of Magnesian Limestone, a common building material in the region during the Roman period [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:98048", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s007: site_type ''Megalithic structures'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:148497", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-010: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/77/Roman_column%2C_York%2C_2023.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/4561c396/Roman column, York 2015.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:310333", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-005: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/4561c396/Roman column, York 2015.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/4561c396/Roman_column,_York,_2023.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
