    ('49835383-20b8-44e0-9bf1-9e06cf2ecbb1'::uuid, 'cultural_influence', '7', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:49835383-20b8-44e0-9bf1-9e06cf2ecbb1:cultural_influence', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: cultural_influence ''5'' restored on Samsø', '522f3d6376b4857deaf18238e6fa83f8', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "cultural_influence = 7 from description yes, thumbnail yes, 0 content link(s), 20 image(s), source_url yes"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28864", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0207:chunk-0001: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Natural feature''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:83532", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2083:chunk-0001: description ''Samsø is a Danish island in the Kattegat sea, approximately 15 km off the Jutland Peninsula [1]. Archaeological evidence shows the island has been continuously occupied since the end of the last Ice Age, when hunter-gatherers first settled the area [1]. Due to its central location in the Kattegat, the island served as an important meeting place during the Viking Age [1]. The island covers 114 km² and today has 3,724 inhabitants called Samsings [1]. The etymology of the name \"Samsø\" remains unknown [1].'' -> ''Samsø is a Danish island in the Kattegat 15 kilometers off the Jutland Peninsula [1]. Due to its central location, the island was used during the Viking Age as a meeting place [1]. People have lived and hunted on Samsø since the ice receded at the end of the last Ice Age [1]. Excavations at Tønnesminde and Endebjerg show evidence of human habitation from the Stone Age through the Viking Age [1]. A canal was dug across the island at its narrowest place [1]. The canal was about 500 m long and 11 m wide and could in its time be navigated by vessels with a draught of up to 1 m [1]. It was dug and clad with wooden linings in the years 726–729 AD; the last part of the Scandinavian Iron Age [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:98117", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s007: period_name ''< 4500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:98118", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s007: period_start ''-7000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:148646", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-010: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/08/%C3%98rbyIngeborgHoyJ%C3%A6stutteH1.jpg'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:248251", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s007: period_name None -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:248252", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s007: period_start None -> ''-4501''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:310390", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-005: thumbnail_url None -> ''/data/images/wiki/49835383/ØrbyIngeborgHoyJæstutteH1.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
