, "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-005: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/4d9953ff/Armenian Ancient capital Armavir - panoramio (1).webp'' -> \"/data/images/wiki/4d9953ff/Armenian_Ancient_capital_Armavir,_Armenian_godness_Anahit''s_temple_ruins_-_panoramio.webp\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:337061", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4wc:p4wc-4307:chunk-0001: description ''Ancient Armavir was a major urban center founded, according to Armenian tradition, by King Aramais around 1980 BC. The ruins lie 1 km west of modern Armavir village, with archaeological evidence showing continuous habitation since the 6th millennium BC. Excavations have yielded obsidian instruments, bronze objects, and pottery spanning multiple periods. The city served as a commercial hub and capital of ancient Armenia during the Orontid dynasty [1].'' -> ''Ancient Armavir was a major urban center founded, according to Armenian tradition, by King Aramais around 1980 BC [1]. The ruins lie 1 km west of modern Armavir village, with archaeological evidence showing habitation since the 6th millennium BC [1]. Excavations have yielded obsidian instruments, bronze objects, and pottery [1]. The city served as a commercial hub and capital of ancient Armenia during the Orontid dynasty [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:355642", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s001: site_type ''City'' -> ''City/town/settlement''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4dc60f9f-66cf-4a4a-9c47-bf24cc0d618d'::uuid, 'mystery', '9', '10', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:4dc60f9f-66cf-4a4a-9c47-bf24cc0d618d:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''10'' restored on Gate of the Sun', 'f811468a74607336606db10c10bf5f87', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 9 from (''Gate/archway/bridge'', ''500 - 1000 AD'') is shared by 2 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28945", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0217:chunk-0001: site_type ''Megalithic stones'' -> ''Gate/archway/bridge''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34455", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0028:chunk-0001: description ''The Gate of the Sun is a monumental monolithic gateway carved from a single stone slab by the Tiwanaku culture at their archaeological site near Lake Titicaca in present-day Bolivia [1]. Standing approximately 3 metres tall and 4 metres wide, with an estimated weight of 10 tons, this megalithic structure dates to between 1-500 AD [2]. The gateway features intricate carvings on its lintel, including a central figure thought to represent a deity, surrounded by 48 square panels depicting various symbolic motifs [1]. The Tiwanaku civilization flourished in the Andean highlands around Lake Titicaca from approximately 500-950 AD, leaving behind this impressive testament to their stone-working expertise [1].'' -> ''The Gate of the Sun, also known as the Gateway of the Sun, is a monolithic gateway at the site of Tiahuanaco, built by the Tiwanaku culture, an Andean civilization of Bolivia that thrived around Lake Titicaca in the Andes of western South America around AD 500–950 [1]. Tiwanaku is located near Lake Titicaca at about 12,549.2 ft above sea level near La Paz [1]. The Gate of the Sun is approximately 9.8 ft tall and 13 ft wide, and was carved from a single piece of stone [1]. Its weight is estimated to be 10 tons [1]. When rediscovered by European explorers in the mid-19th century, the megalith was lying horizontally and had a large crack through it [1]. It presently stands in the location where it was found, although it is believed that this is not its original site, which remains uncertain [1]. Although there have been various modern interpretations of the inscriptions found on the object, the carvings that decorate the gate are believed to possess astronomical or astrological significance and may have served a calendrical purpose [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:83082", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26c_fields-wd1-s001: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:83083", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26c_fields-wd1-s001: period_start ''1'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:149417", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-011: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/4dc60f9f/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/4dc60f9f/2Wiener-Tintin-Dieu Soleil.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:248279", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s007: period_name None -> ''500 - 1000 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:248280", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s007: period_start None -> ''500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:310466", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-005: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/4dc60f9f/2Wiener-Tintin-Dieu Soleil.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/4dc60f9f/Zonnepoort_tiwanaku.webp''"}]'::jsonb