culture, an Andean civilization of Bolivia that thrived around Lake Titicaca in the Andes of western South America around AD 500–950 [1]. Tiwanaku is located near Lake Titicaca at about 12,549.2 ft above sea level near La Paz [1]. The Gate of the Sun is approximately 9.8 ft tall and 13 ft wide, and was carved from a single piece of stone [1]. Its weight is estimated to be 10 tons [1]. When rediscovered by European explorers in the mid-19th century, the megalith was lying horizontally and had a large crack through it [1]. It presently stands in the location where it was found, although it is believed that this is not its original site, which remains uncertain [1]. Although there have been various modern interpretations of the inscriptions found on the object, the carvings that decorate the gate are believed to possess astronomical or astrological significance and may have served a calendrical purpose [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:83082", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26c_fields-wd1-s001: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:83083", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26c_fields-wd1-s001: period_start ''1'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:149417", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-011: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/4dc60f9f/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/4dc60f9f/2Wiener-Tintin-Dieu Soleil.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:248279", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s007: period_name None -> ''500 - 1000 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:248280", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s007: period_start None -> ''500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:310466", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-005: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/4dc60f9f/2Wiener-Tintin-Dieu Soleil.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/4dc60f9f/Zonnepoort_tiwanaku.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4dc7086c-f261-4f38-85f2-10a20eeda1bc'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:4dc7086c-f261-4f38-85f2-10a20eeda1bc:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on La Garma Cave Complex', '8d3f3f82c705d726226ce5fc1e3e7353', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Rock art'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 29 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44359", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0091:chunk-0001: description ''The La Garma Cave Complex is a parietal art-bearing paleoanthropological cave system in Cantabria, Spain, located just north of the village of Omoño in the municipality of Ribamontán al Monte [1]. The cave complex contains more than 4,000 fossils and over 500 graphical units, including 109 signs, 92 animal figures, and 40 hand stencils [2]. It is noted for having one of the best preserved floors from the Paleolithic period in Europe [1]. La Garma is part of the Cave of Altamira and Paleolithic Cave Art of Northern Spain World Heritage Site [1].'' -> ''The La Garma cave complex is a parietal art-bearing paleoanthropological cave system in Cantabria, Spain [1]. The cave complex is noted for one of the best preserved floors from the Paleolithic containing more than 4,000 fossils and more than 500 graphical units [1]. The Lower Gallery of La Garma is situated at 59 m above sea level and is around 300 m in length [1]. It was discovered in November 1995 [1]. Its original entrance was sealed during the Pleistocene by a rockslide about 16,000 years ago, preserving the cave floor in a pristine manner [1]. La Garma is notable for its rich repository of Magdalenian portable art found in The Lower Gallery [1]. The most outstanding artefact is a backward-facing ibex depiction carved onto a bovine rib spatula [1]. The peculiar remains of five Visigothic youths were found deep in the cave system [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:149418", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-011: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/4dc7086c/hero.webp'' -> \"/data/images/wiki/4dc7086c/Sepultura mesolítica de la cueva de ''El Truchiro'', en Ribamontán al Monte.webp\""}]'::jsonb),
