e Canyon are vestiges of various seasonal camping grounds in an extensive area, characterized by a concentration of archaeological material: ceramic, stone tools [1]. Most of the archaeological materials found in these grounds, are within the archaeological tradition known as Central Coast, and are particularly similar to those found by Edward Mosler and Thomas Bowen at sites in the region of Tastiota and Punta San Antonio in the southern part of the Central Coast area [1]. So far, La Pintada is the site associated with that archaeological tradition farther away from the coastline [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:98173", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s007: period_name ''500 - 1000 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:98174", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s007: period_start ''500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:149419", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-011: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/4df3dcbd/hero.webp'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:248281", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s007: period_name None -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:248282", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s007: period_start None -> ''-10000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348217", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_name ''< 4500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348218", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_start ''-10000'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4df5f7bf-8b73-4602-9d6e-dab158203820'::uuid, 'mystery', '1', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:4df5f7bf-8b73-4602-9d6e-dab158203820:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''5'' restored on Chocolá', '72320a724dc791c6a9367047ce260100', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 1 from (''City/town/settlement'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 286 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:83802", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2086:chunk-0001: description \"Chocola is a major Preclassic Maya site in the Suchitepequez Department of Guatemala, active from c. 1000 BC to 200 AD. The site spans several square kilometers with over 100 mounds, suggesting its population may have rivalled better-known Preclassic centres like Kaminaljuyu. A sophisticated underground hydraulic system of stone-lined channels runs through the site, representing one of the oldest known examples of such engineering in Mesoamerica. Cacao residues identified in Preclassic pottery support the theory that intensive cacao cultivation drove the site''s early complexity. Chocola is also one of only nine southern Mesoamerican sites to produce a ruler portrait monument.\" -> ''Chocolá is a Preclassic Southern Maya site whose developmental emphasis was from c. 1000 BC to AD 200 [1]. Chocolá is in the San Pablo Jocopilas municipality in the southern Suchitepéquez Department of Guatemala [1]. Lying on a plateau below volcanic mountain ridges to the north and east, and at a height of 500–1000 meters, the site consists of three general groupings extending over c. 6 by 2 kilometers [1]. To the north, great platform mounds consisted of elite residences, with elaborate hydraulic networks of stone-lined canals bringing water in from underground springs [1]. In the ancient central district further south, pyramidal mounds up to 25 meters held administrative structures [1]. A complex of more than 100 structures was discovered by Karl Sapper in the last years of the 19th century and a few mounds were excavated by Robert Burkitt in the 1920s [1]. The discovery by Burkitt of Monument 1, a stela carved in the \"Miraflores\" style, sparked interest among scholars, suggestive as it was that Chocolá may have been an important polity early on with distinct political connections with Kaminaljuyu [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:117516", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s004: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''City''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:149422", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-011: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/4df5f7bf/hero.webp'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:355644", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s001: site_type ''City'' -> ''City/town/settlement''"}]'::jsonb),
