sverse road linking the Eastern gate and Western gate divided the complex into two halves [1]. In the southern half there were more luxurious structures than in the northern section; these included public, private and religious buildings, as well as the Emperor''s apartments [1]. The Palace was decorated with numerous 3500-year-old granite sphinxes, originating from the site of Egyptian Pharaoh Thutmose III [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:149458", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-011: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/4e78a6c5/hero.webp'' -> \"/data/images/wiki/4e78a6c5/Diocletian''s_Palace,_Split_(11908066464).webp\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:310469", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-005: thumbnail_url \"/data/images/wiki/4e78a6c5/Diocletian''s_Palace,_Split_(11908066464).webp\" -> ''/data/images/wiki/4e78a6c5/Croatia-01239_-_The_Peristil_(9551533404).webp''"}]'::jsonb),
