    ('50d4292b-caee-4819-8aaa-64b94187fce8'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:50d4292b-caee-4819-8aaa-64b94187fce8:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''5'' restored on Serapeum of Saqqara', '7ee0ef49f3ce2fa16276777a549625ab', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Necropolis/tombs complex'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 24 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29674", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0324:chunk-0001: period_start ''-1500'' -> ''-1390''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35765", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: period_start ''-1390'' -> ''-1500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44381", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0092:chunk-0001: description ''The Serapeum of Saqqara was the sacred burial place of the Apis bulls, believed to be divine incarnations of the god Ptah, spanning approximately 1,400 years from the New Kingdom to the Ptolemaic Period. The site features an extensive underground network including the Great Gallery, a vaulted chamber 300 metres long housing around 24 granite sarcophagi, some weighing up to 62 tonnes. French archaeologist Auguste Mariette rediscovered the complex in 1850 after spotting a sphinx partially buried in desert sand. Each bull underwent elaborate 70-day embalming rituals, with the final two days devoted to funerary processions terminating at the Serapeum.'' -> ''The Serapeum of Saqqara was the ancient Egyptian burial place for sacred bulls of the Apis cult at Memphis [1]. Over a timespan of approximately 1400 years, from the New Kingdom of Egypt to the end of the Ptolemaic Period, at least sixty Apis are attested to have been interred at the Serapeum [1]. In 1850 Auguste Mariette rediscovered the Serapeum, and excavated it the following years [1]. He found two undisturbed Apis burials, as well as thousands of objects related to centuries of cult activity [1]. The most ancient burials at the Serapeum, found in isolated tombs, date back to the reign of Amenhotep III of the Eighteenth Dynasty in the 14th century BC [1]. Khaemweset, working as an administrator during the reign of his father Ramesses II (1279–1213 BC) in the Nineteenth Dynasty, ordered a tunnel with side chambers – now known as the \"Lesser Vaults\" – to be excavated, for the burial of the Apis bulls [1]. The Serapeum was abandoned at the beginning of the Roman Period, shortly after 30 BC [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:149514", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-011: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Hémicycle_Saqqarah_082005.JPG/400px-Hémicycle_Saqqarah_082005.JPG'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/50d4292b/By_ovedc_-_Serapeum_of_Saqqara_-_18.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
