Egyptian burial place for sacred bulls of the Apis cult at Memphis [1]. Over a timespan of approximately 1400 years, from the New Kingdom of Egypt to the end of the Ptolemaic Period, at least sixty Apis are attested to have been interred at the Serapeum [1]. In 1850 Auguste Mariette rediscovered the Serapeum, and excavated it the following years [1]. He found two undisturbed Apis burials, as well as thousands of objects related to centuries of cult activity [1]. The most ancient burials at the Serapeum, found in isolated tombs, date back to the reign of Amenhotep III of the Eighteenth Dynasty in the 14th century BC [1]. Khaemweset, working as an administrator during the reign of his father Ramesses II (1279–1213 BC) in the Nineteenth Dynasty, ordered a tunnel with side chambers – now known as the \"Lesser Vaults\" – to be excavated, for the burial of the Apis bulls [1]. The Serapeum was abandoned at the beginning of the Roman Period, shortly after 30 BC [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:149514", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-011: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Hémicycle_Saqqarah_082005.JPG/400px-Hémicycle_Saqqarah_082005.JPG'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/50d4292b/By_ovedc_-_Serapeum_of_Saqqara_-_18.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('50debb04-ae49-4d55-9756-bdfb8fbacf9c'::uuid, 'antiquity', '5', '3', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:50debb04-ae49-4d55-9756-bdfb8fbacf9c:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''3'' restored on Siscia', 'eb17307f1cb6bac23635b3c9a2776d88', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 5 from period_start None"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:80844", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2010:chunk-0001: description \"Siscia, modern-day Sisak in Croatia, was a major Roman city at the confluence of the Kupa, Sava, and Odra rivers. Captured by Octavian in 35 BC after a thirty-day siege, it was elevated to colonia status by Vespasian as Colonia Flavia Siscia. The city housed the state mint and treasury in the 3rd-4th centuries and served as capital of Pannonia Savia. Excavations have revealed bath buildings, walls, inscriptions, and stamped tiles reading ''SISC(ia)'', though much of ancient Siscia lies beneath the modern town.\" -> ''Siscia is described by Roman writers as a great town in the south of Upper Pannonia, on the southern bank of the Savus, on an island formed by that river and two others, the Colapis and Odra, a canal dug by Tiberius completing the island [1]. It was on the great road from Aemona to Sirmium [1]. Siscia was from the first a strongly fortified town; and after its capture by Tiberius, in the reign of Augustus, it became one of the most important places of Pannonia; for being on two navigable rivers, it not only carried on considerable commerce, but became the central point from which Augustus and Tiberius carried on their undertakings against the Pannonians and Illyrians [1]. Tiberius did much to enlarge and embellish the town, which as early as that time seems to have been made a colonia, for Pliny mentions it as such: in the time of Septimius Severus it received fresh colonists [1]. The town contained an imperial mint, which produced coins under a series of emperors between 262 and 383 AD [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:98855", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s008: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:98856", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s008: period_start ''1'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:149515", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-011: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Sisak_Fortress.jpg/400px-Sisak_Fortress.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/50debb04/Roman_ruins_in_Sisak.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:248301", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s007: period_name None -> ''1 - 500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:248302", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s007: period_start None -> ''1''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348237", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348238", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_start ''1'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
