    ('50e2e3a4-4f3e-4b1e-bd7a-8b97404f59c8'::uuid, 'mystery', '3', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:50e2e3a4-4f3e-4b1e-bd7a-8b97404f59c8:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''6'' restored on Nipisat Island', '48f7ce8acb2af588ef88fdb4fc394969', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 3 from (''City/town/settlement'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 130 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34491", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0029:chunk-0001: description ''A small uninhabited island in central-western Greenland, 15 km south of Sisimiut, containing a major Saqqaq culture archaeological site representing the earliest documented human occupation of Greenland (c. 2500-800 BC). Excavations from 1989 to 1994 uncovered over 70,000 bone fragments, 20,000 flakes, and 1,000 artefacts, with radiocarbon dating revealing nearly 1,500 years of continuous occupation. Caribou comprised 55% of mammal remains, reflecting seasonal inland hunting strategies. In 2018, the site was inscribed as part of the Aasivissuit-Nipisat UNESCO World Heritage Site, recognizing over 4,200 years of Arctic hunting traditions.'' -> ''Nipisat Island is a small, uninhabited island in the Qeqqata municipality in central-western Greenland [1]. The island is notable for its well preserved Saqqaq culture archaeological site, containing some stone artifacts that were previously unknown from the Saqqaq culture [1]. The site, named after the island, was discovered in 1989 by Finn Kramer, curator of the Sisimiut Museum [1]. It lies approximately 50 m from the present coastline, situated on raised beaches with a southeastern slope [1]. The area elevation ranges between 9 m (30 ft)and 13 m (43 ft) above mean sea level [1]. This part of the island that contains the archaeological site, did not show signs of later occupation by Dorset culture or Thule culture [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:117547", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s004: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Settlement''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:117548", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s004: source_url ''https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nipisat_Island'' -> ''https://tidsskrift.dk/meddrgroenland_man_soc/article/view/147038''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:355648", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s001: site_type ''Settlement'' -> ''City/town/settlement''"}]'::jsonb),
