: "phase4wc:p4wc-4308:chunk-0001: description ''The Bava Pyara Caves are ancient rock-cut caverns near Junagadh in Gujarat, India, forming part of the Junagadh Buddhist Cave Groups. The complex comprises 13 caves modelled on three floors, carved along a 45-metre rock face and influenced by Satavahana architecture with Graeco-Scythian stylistic elements. Dated to the 2nd-3rd century AD, the caves were initially constructed for Buddhist monks but later became a sanctuary for Jain ascetics, as evidenced by the addition of Jain auspicious symbols including the Swastika, Nandhyavarta, and Purna Ghata above door frames. The caves are located near Modhimath, outside the Uparkot fort complex, and are considered better preserved than the nearby Khapara Kodia caves.'' -> ''The Bava Pyara Caves are ancient rock-cut caverns near Junagadh in Gujarat, India, forming part of the Junagadh Buddhist Cave Groups [1] [2]. The complex comprises 13 caves modelled on three floors, carved along a 45-metre rock face [3]. The caves are located near Modhimath, outside the Uparkot fort complex, and are considered better preserved than the nearby Khapara Kodia caves [3].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('51e00207-24c0-4f44-b581-4d8584df5296'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:51e00207-24c0-4f44-b581-4d8584df5296:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''6'' restored on Roman Theatre, Cartagena', '08a0b61fa25984358602b7e3d553e0d4', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Theatre'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 28 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44387", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0092:chunk-0001: description ''Built between 5 and 1 BC in the Roman colony of Carthago Nova, this theatre was dedicated to Gaius and Lucius Caesar, grandsons of Augustus. It seated approximately 6,000 spectators across three social tiers. A 3rd-century market was built over it, later destroyed by Vandals in 425 AD. Rediscovered in 1988, it was fully excavated by 2003 under archaeologists Ramallo Asensio and Ruiz Valderas. Architect Rafael Moneo designed the museum, and the site now draws over 200,000 visitors annually.'' -> ''The Roman Theatre of Cartagena is a Roman theatre in the Roman colonia of Carthago Nova—present-day Cartagena, Spain [1]. Built between the years 5 and 1 BCE, it had a capacity of some 6,000 spectators and was used for Roman theatrical performances [1]. In Cartagena, under the rule of Augustus (31 B.C.-14 A.D.), taking advantage of the slope of the hill of La Concepción and in a position close to the port, the Theater was built so that from the sea the magnificent building could be seen [1]. The Theater, inaugurated in 1 B.C., functioned as such for two centuries, although partial restorations were necessary during that time [1]. Prior to the theatre, in the area where it is currently located, there was a Roman house built at the end of the second century B.C., which was intentionally demolished for the construction of the Roman Theater in the time of Augustus [1]. After a fire that caused a large part of its architectural framework to disappear, in the 5th century A.D. what was left standing of the Theater was dismantled and its architectural elements were used to build a market [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:149540", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-011: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/39/Teatro_romano_de_Cartagena%2C_Espa%C3%B1a%2C_2022-07-16%2C_DD_07.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/51e00207/Teatro_romano_de_Cartagena,_España,_2022-07-16,_DD_07.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
