 BC – 6th c. AD) began as a trading post on Algeria''s central coast before Rome transformed it into a strategic colonial base for conquering Mauretania [1] [2]. The site preserves a unique layered heritage—Phoenician, Roman, palaeochristian and Byzantine ruins standing together [1]. UNESCO inscribed Tipasa as a World Heritage Site in 1982, listing it as in danger from 2002–2006 before successful conservation efforts removed it from the danger list [1] [2].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:355650", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s001: site_type ''City'' -> ''City/town/settlement''"}]'::jsonb),
