n Era as a large number of late Hispano-Roman red gloss pottery testifies [1]. Excavations have been carried out since 2007 to date with a gap year in 2015 [1]. In a shelter located just few meters below the site, some samples of schematic art have been discovered [1]. The images represent human-like icons, sticks, fingers, anchor-shaped symbols, circles and indefinites [1]. Painted in red colour on quartzite, they belong to the Bronze Age with an age of more than 3000 years [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:98898", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s008: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:98899", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s008: period_start ''-1500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:149562", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-011: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/52fe4aef/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/52fe4aef/Excavación_en_el_Castillón_(2013).webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:237615", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s004: period_name None -> ''1 - 500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:237616", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s004: period_start None -> ''401''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:311125", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-006: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/52fe4aef/Excavación_en_el_Castillón_(2013).webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/52fe4aef/Cerámica_gris_estampillada.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348257", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348258", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_start ''401'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('53058d01-7e64-49c7-8dec-2b352a0e9ec6'::uuid, 'antiquity', '7', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:53058d01-7e64-49c7-8dec-2b352a0e9ec6:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''8'' restored on Grey Croft Stone Circle', '1f111a29f71c2dbdf7084ea8109230ec', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 7 from period_start -2900"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:84350", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2088:chunk-0001: description ''Grey Croft is a Bronze Age stone circle near Seascale in Cumbria, measuring approximately 30 metres in diameter with 10 surviving stones of an original 12, some reaching nearly two metres high. The stones are hard agglomerates of the Borrowdale volcanic series, with one outlier at 34 metres distance. In 1820, farmer James Fox buried all but one stone to clear his fields; in 1949, W. Fletcher and boys from Pelham House School unearthed and restored them. Excavations yielded Bronze Age flints, stone axes, a Whitby jet ring, and red granite cobbles from a burial cairn.'' -> ''Grey Croft stone circle is a restored stone circle near Seascale in Cumbria [1]. Grey Croft stone circle is a circle of 12 stones situated south of the Sellafield nuclear site [1]. Only 10 of the original 12 stones are currently standing [1]. The stones in the circle were toppled over and buried in 1820 by a tenant farmer, with the exception of one stone [1]. In 1949 the site was excavated and nine of the stones were located, and replaced as accurately as possible [1]. The excavations uncovered an oval stone cairn situated in the circle centre [1]. Trenching under the cairn revealed charcoal traces, calcined bones, a broken early Bronze Age jet or lignite ring, and flint flakes and scraper [1]. A Great Langdale Late-Neolithic stone axe was found near to the eastern stone of the circle [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:98901", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s008: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:98902", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s008: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:248327", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s007: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:248328", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s007: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348259", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348260", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-2900''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('53058d01-7e64-49c7-8dec-2b352a0e9ec6'::uuid, 'total_power', '26', '27', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:53058d01-7e64-49c7-8dec-2b352a0e9ec6:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: total_power ''27'' restored on Grey Croft Stone Circle', '1f111a29f71c2dbdf7084ea8109230ec', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 26 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 7 + 8 + 6 + 4 + 1"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:84350", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2088:chunk-0001: description ''Grey Croft is a Bronze Age stone circle near Seascale in Cumbria, measuring approximately 30 metres in diameter with 10 surviving stones of an original 12, some reaching nearly two metres high. The stones are hard agglomerates of the Borrowdale volcanic series, with one outlier at 34 metres distance. In 1820, farmer James Fox buried all but one stone to clear his fields; in 1949, W. Fletcher and boys from Pelham House School unearthed and restored them. Excavations yielded Bronze Age flints, stone axes, a Whitby jet ring, and red granite cobbles from a burial cairn.'' -> ''Grey Croft stone circle is a restored stone circle near Seascale in Cumbria [1]. Grey Croft stone circle is a circle of 12 stones situated south of the Sellafield nuclear site [1]. Only 10 of the original 12 stones are currently standing [1]. The stones in the circle were toppled over and buried in 1820 by a tenant farmer, with the exception of one stone [1]. In 1949 the site was excavated and nine of the stones were located, and replaced as accurately as possible [1]. The excavations uncovered an oval stone cairn situated in the circle centre [1]. Trenching under the cairn revealed charcoal traces, calcined bones, a broken early Bronze Age jet or lignite ring, and flint flakes and scraper [1]. A Great Langdale Late-Neolithic stone axe was found near to the eastern stone of the circle [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:98901", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s008: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:98902", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s008: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:248327", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s007: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:248328", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s007: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348259", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348260", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-2900''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('532df28e-27f6-4391-8167-bc139f303b3b'::uuid, 'antiquity', '2', '3', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:532df28e-27f6-4391-8167-bc139f303b3b:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''3'' restored on Honcopampa', '3f7ce5831c4629faaed034c90c41f5a9', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 2 from period_start 600"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:80848", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2010:chunk-0001: description \"Honcopampa is a Middle Horizon (AD 550-1000) archaeological site at 3,475m elevation on the western flank of Peru''s Cordillera Blanca. The ruins feature impressive multistoried chullpas, rectangular patio groups with elongated halls, and rare D-shaped structures, blending imported Wari architectural forms with local northern highland masonry traditions. Interpreted as a provincial administrative center, the site likely mediated power between southern Wari rulers and native northern highland peoples in the Callejon de Huaylas. Finds include grinding stones indicating food preparation in residential compounds.\" -> ''Honcopampa or Joncopampa (possibly from Ancash Quechua hunqu swamp, pampa plain) is an archaeological site in Peru [1]. It is situated in the Ancash Region, Carhuaz Province, Aco District, at a height of about 3,500 metres [1]. Hunqupampa is considered one of the most important archaeological sites of the Callejón de Huaylas [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:98903", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s008: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> None"}, {