    ('53325d71-8fe8-404a-8995-f4b2dd9d7d13'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:53325d71-8fe8-404a-8995-f4b2dd9d7d13:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''5'' restored on Menhir of Outeiro', '93356fcbed743e1e2d04b2a7872b85de', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Megalithic stones'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 17 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:84372", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2089:chunk-0001: description \"The Menhir of Outeiro, also called Penedo Comprido, is a megalithic monument in the Alentejo region of Portugal, situated midway between Outeiro and Barrada villages near Reguengos de Monsaraz, just kilometers from the Spanish border [1]. The large stone was found lying prostrate in 1969 by archaeologists Henrique Leonor Pina and José Pires Gonçalves, and re-erected around 1970 through Gonçalves''s initiative [1]. It received national monument status in 1971 [1].\" -> ''The Menhir of Outeiro, also known as the Penedo Comprido, is a megalith located midway between the villages of Outeiro and Barrada near the municipality of Reguengos de Monsaraz, in the district of Évora, in the Alentejo region of Portugal [1]. The stone was discovered on its side in 1969 by Henrique Leonor Pina and José Pires Gonçalves and, on the initiative of Gonçalves, was raised again around 1970 [1]. The menhir was classified as a Portuguese National Monument in 1971 [1]. The granite monolith is 5.6 meters high with an average diameter of one meter, and weighs an estimated 8 tonnes [1]. It is the second largest in Portugal [1]. Based on its similarity to other megalithic finds in the Évora district, the menhir is believed to date from the Late Neolithic or Chalcolithic [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:149569", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-011: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/53325d71/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/53325d71/OuteiroMenhir1.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
