h 17 stone monoliths distributed around a ceremonial platform. Carved stones depict the sun god Tata Inti with eleven solar rays, the Andean serpent (Katari), and the Andean wildcat (Titi). The complex includes a solar gateway, astronomical observatory, burial structure, and stone staircases. On June 21st, the site hosts celebrations of the Andean New Year (Pachakuti). It was declared a National Cultural Heritage of Peru.\" -> \"Intini Uyu Pata (also called Inti Uyu) is an archaeological site in Peru''s Puno Region, Yunguyo Province, between the districts of Ollaraya and Unicachi [1] [2]. The site belongs to the Lupaca society of the Aymara domain and features a temple with 17 stone monoliths distributed around a ceremonial platform [2]. Carved stones depict the sun god Tata Inti with eleven solar rays, the Andean serpent (Katari), and the Andean wildcat (Titi) [2]. The complex includes a solar gateway, astronomical observatory, burial structure, and stone staircases [3]. On June 21st, the site hosts celebrations of the Andean New Year (Pachakuti) [2]. It was declared a National Cultural Heritage of Peru [1] [2].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:349000", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s004: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:349001", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s004: period_start ''1'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
