nge_log:248704", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s008: period_start None -> ''1000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:337301", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4wc:p4wc-4310:chunk-0001: description ''Cappadocia is a historical region in central Turkey shaped by volcanic eruptions from Mount Hasan and Mount Erciyes, whose soft tuff formations enabled millennia of rock-cut architecture. Neolithic pottery attests to early habitation, while excavations at Kultepe yielded thousands of clay tablets from the 3rd millennium BC. The Phrygians began carving underground refuges around the 7th-6th century BC; Derinkuyu, the largest, reaches 18 levels and 85 metres deep. Byzantine-era rock-cut churches at Goreme, featuring frescoes from c. 1000 AD, earned UNESCO World Heritage status in 1985. The region served as home to the Cappadocian Fathers -- Basil the Great, Gregory of Nyssa, and Gregory of Nazianzus -- who shaped early Christian theology.'' -> ''Cappadocia is a historical region in central Turkey shaped by volcanic eruptions from Mount Hasan and Mount Erciyes [1]. Neolithic pottery attests to early habitation, while excavations at Kultepe yielded thousands of clay tablets [1] [2] [3]. Byzantine-era rock-cut churches at Goreme earned UNESCO World Heritage status in 1985 [1] [4] [5]. The region served as home to the Cappadocian Fathers -- Basil the Great, Gregory of Nyssa, and Gregory of Nazianzus -- who shaped early Christian theology [6].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:349004", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s004: period_name ''1000 - 1500 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:349005", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s004: period_start ''1000'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
