}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:99986", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s009: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:99987", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s009: period_start ''-3000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:99988", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s009: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Village''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:150932", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-013: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Monte_Grossu_(Biguglia,_Haute-Corse).jpg/400px-Monte_Grossu_(Biguglia,_Haute-Corse).jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/5c7bd8b5/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:248793", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s008: period_name None -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:248794", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s008: period_start None -> ''-3000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:345686", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-09a_fields-wd5-s001: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:345687", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-09a_fields-wd5-s001: period_start ''-3000'' -> ''-4000''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('5ca2f8d6-0077-4fe6-9034-18308bb4a4d1'::uuid, 'antiquity', '7', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:5ca2f8d6-0077-4fe6-9034-18308bb4a4d1:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''8'' restored on Hoarstones', '8bcc01702276841d36943eb0d8eb9abd', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 7 from period_start -2500"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34591", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0032:chunk-0001: description \"The Hoarstones is a Neolithic to Bronze Age stone circle in Chirbury with Brompton, Shropshire, measuring 23.3 by 21.1 metres in an elliptical shape. It contains 38 to 40 small dolerite stones, probably sourced locally, with a central stone of unclear original purpose. Two small round cairns lie approximately 30 metres from the circle centre. It is one of two surviving prehistoric stone circles in Shropshire alongside Mitchell''s Fold, from a group of five once recorded within two miles of each other. The Reverend C. H. Hartshorne first documented the site in 1841, and around 1897 a perforated sandstone axe-head was ploughed up nearby and donated to the Ashmolean Museum.\" -> \"The Hoarstones, or Hoar Stone Circle, is a stone circle in the civil parish of Chirbury with Brompton in the English county of Shropshire [1]. The Hoarstones are part of a tradition of stone circle construction that spread throughout much of Britain, Ireland, and Brittany during the Late Neolithic and Early Bronze Ages, over a period between 3300 and 900 BCE [1]. The Hoarstones are one of up to five stone circles known from this area, on the borders between Shropshire and Powys [1]. Of these, only the Hoarstones and Mitchell''s Fold survive [1]. The Hoarstones contains between 38 and 40 stones, arranged in an elliptical fashion [1]. Just south of the centre of the circle is a single stone, measuring around 1 metre (3 feet) high; it is unclear if this is an original prehistoric feature of the monument or a later addition [1]. Around 1897, a Mr Jarrett ploughed up a perforated sandstone axe-head from the field immediately to the south of the circle [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:99992", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s009: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:99993", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s009: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:248795", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s008: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:248796", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s008: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:349068", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s004: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:349069", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s004: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-2500''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('5ca2f8d6-0077-4fe6-9034-18308bb4a4d1'::uuid, 'total_power', '25', '26', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:5ca2f8d6-0077-4fe6-9034-18308bb4a4d1:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: total_power ''26'' restored on Hoarstones', '8bcc01702276841d36943eb0d8eb9abd', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 25 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 7 + 8 + 5 + 4 + 1"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34591", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0032:chunk-0001: description \"The Hoarstones is a Neolithic to Bronze Age stone circle in Chirbury with Brompton, Shropshire, measuring 23.3 by 21.1 metres in an elliptical shape. It contains 38 to 40 small dolerite stones, probably sourced locally, with a central stone of unclear original purpose. Two small round cairns lie approximately 30 metres from the circle centre. It is one of two surviving prehistoric stone circles in Shropshire alongside Mitchell''s Fold, from a group of five once recorded within two miles of each other. The Reverend C. H. Hartshorne first documented the site in 1841, and around 1897 a perforated sandstone axe-head was ploughed up nearby and donated to the Ashmolean Museum.\" -> \"The Hoarstones, or Hoar Stone Circle, is a stone circle in the civil parish of Chirbury with Brompton in the English county of Shropshire [1]. The Hoarstones are part of a tradition of stone circle construction that spread throughout much of Britain, Ireland, and Brittany during the Late Neolithic and Early Bronze Ages, over a period between 3300 and 900 BCE [1]. The Hoarstones are one of up to five stone circles known from this area, on the borders between Shropshire and Powys [1]. Of these, only the Hoarstones and Mitchell''s Fold survive [1]. The Hoarstones contains between 38 and 40 stones, arranged in an elliptical fashion [1]. Just south of the centre of the circle is a single stone, measuring around 1 metre (3 feet) high; it is unclear if this is an original prehistoric feature of the monument or a later addition [1]. Around 1897, a Mr Jarrett ploughed up a perforated sandstone axe-head from the field immediately to the south of the circle [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:99992", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s009: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:99993", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s009: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:248795", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s008: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:248796", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s008: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:349068", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s004: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:349069", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s004: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-2500''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('5cd47e05-c322-4510-b40f-c1f75072c147'::uuid, 'mystery', '3', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:5cd47e05-c322-4510-b40f-c1f75072c147:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Eridu', 'e0c195d77b2da5f9988bd0aeb0c63a0c', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 3 from (''City/town/settlement'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 105 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:84762", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2095:chunk-0001: description \"Eridu is the earliest known city in southern Mesopotamia at Tell Abu Shahrain. Located 24 km south-southwest of Ur, it was the southernmost of a cluster of Sumerian cities built around temples [1]. The city was dedicated to the god Enki (later Ea), believed to dwell in the Abzu—an underground aquifer considered the source of all freshwater and life [2]. Enki''s temple, E-Abzu, was central to Eridu''s religious significance, and the city''s founding is attributed to Enki himself in Sumerian tradition [2].\" -> ''Eridu was a Sumerian city located at Tell Abu Shahrain, also Abu Shahrein or Tell Abu Shahrayn, an archaeological site in Lower Mesopotamia [1]. It is located in Dhi Qar Governorate, Iraq, near the modern city of Basra [1]. Located 24 km south-southwest of the ancient site of Ur, Eridu was the southernmost of a conglomeration of Sumerian cities that grew around temples, almost in sight of one another [1]. The site was initially excavated by John George Taylor in 1855 [1]. Eridu is one of the earliest settlements in the region, founded c.\\u20095400 BC during the early Ubaid period, at that time close to the Persian Gulf near the mouth of the Euphrates, although in modern times it is about 90 miles inland [1]. Excavation has shown that the city was founded on a virgin sand dune site with no previous habitation [1]. Eighteen superimposed mudbrick temples at the site underlie the unfinished ziggurat of Amar-Sin (c. 2047–2039 BC) [1]. Occupation increased in the Early Dynastic period with a monumental 100 m × 100 m palace being constructed [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:117678", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s004: lat ''30.80732092733441'' -> ''30.81583''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:117679", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s004: lon ''45.98717126241032'' -> ''45.99611''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:150938", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-013: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/5cd47e05/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/5cd47e05/Eridu_mound4c.8.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
