930s, chalk quarrying adjacent to the long barrow had destroyed much of its southern side [1]. In 1936, local resident W. F. Rankine discovered ox bones and stone arrow-heads in the vicinity of the site [1]. An excavation was launched under the directorship of Alexander Keiller and Stuart Piggott [1]. The northern and southern sides of the tumulus were flanked with flat-bottomed, parallel ditches [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:100001", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s009: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:100002", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s009: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:150941", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-013: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/5d252b0e/hero.webp'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:237701", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s004: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:237702", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s004: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}]'::jsonb),
