    ('5d3974ac-5df3-4f32-9d62-634921bdaf5d'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:5d3974ac-5df3-4f32-9d62-634921bdaf5d:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''6'' restored on Dunkery Beacon - Dunkery Hill', '2e445628872949b5978817534eb62852', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Cairn'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 35 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:84790", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2096:chunk-0001: description ''Dunkery Beacon, at 519 metres the highest point on Exmoor and in Somerset, contains a compact group of five Bronze Age cairns dating from approximately 2350-701 BC that occupy less than 0.25 hectares at the summit. The burial mounds, including the notable Joaney How and Robin How, represent late Neolithic to late Bronze Age funerary activity. The cairns are protected as Scheduled Monument 1003026 and were surveyed by English Heritage in 2004 after sustained visitor damage, with three cairns suffering from stones being regularly rearranged. The site was donated to the National Trust and now holds designations as a Site of Special Scientific Interest and Special Area of Conservation.'' -> ''Dunkery Beacon at the summit of Dunkery Hill is the highest point on Exmoor and in Somerset, England [1]. It is also the highest point in southern England outside of Dartmoor [1]. The sandstone hill rises to 519 metres and provides views over the surrounding moorland, the Bristol Channel and hills up to 86 miles away [1]. The site has been visited by humans since the Bronze Age, and contains several burial mounds in the form of cairns and bowl barrows [1]. Dunkery Hill was part of the \"Royal Forest of Exmoor\", established by Henry II according to the late 13th-century Hundred Rolls [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:117680", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s004: source_url ''https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dunkery_Hill'' -> ''https://www.exmoorher.co.uk/Monument/MEM22803''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:150946", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-013: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/5d3974ac/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/5d3974ac/Dunkery Beacon memorial.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:311312", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-006: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/5d3974ac/Dunkery Beacon memorial.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/5d3974ac/Dunkery_Beacon_summit_cairn.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
