    ('5d8ab96d-45d0-47dd-8e05-8202baf131c1'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:5d8ab96d-45d0-47dd-8e05-8202baf131c1:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''6'' restored on Carrowmore', '93aa7ccb475adbda33c37b44e5dbea34', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (''Megalithic structures'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 10 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44423", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0093:chunk-0001: description \"Carrowmore is a large group of megalithic monuments on the Coolera Peninsula to the west of Sligo, Ireland, built in the 4th millennium BC during the Neolithic [1]. There are 30 surviving tombs with another 25 destroyed since 1800, making Carrowmore one of the largest clusters of megalithic tombs in Ireland and one of the ''big four'' along with Carrowkeel, Loughcrew and Brú na Bóinne [1]. It is Ireland''s oldest megalithic cemetery, set in a dramatic ritual landscape dominated by Knocknarea mountain to the west [1][2]. Carrowmore is a protected National Monument [1].\" -> ''Carrowmore is a large group of megalithic monuments on the Coolera Peninsula to the west of Sligo [1]. They were built in the 4th millennium BC, during the Neolithic [1]. Placed on a small plateau at an altitude of between 36.5 and 59 metres above sea level Carrowmore is the focal point of a prehistoric ritual landscape which is dominated by the mountain of Knocknarea to the west with the great cairn of Miosgán Médhbh on top [1]. Most of the sites are \"satellite tombs\" which surround the largest monument, placed on the high point of the plateau, the cairn called Listoghil [1]. Listoghil, which was erected c. 3500 BC, is 34 metres in diameter and has a distinctive box-like chamber [1]. A source critical review and 25 new radiocarbon dates demonstrated that the Carrowmore monuments are shown to have spanned the era circa 3750 BC to circa 3000 BC [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:150975", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-013: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/5d8ab96d/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/5d8ab96d/Carrowmore Megalithic Cemetery Structure close to P52 II 2015 09 08.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:311318", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-006: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/5d8ab96d/Carrowmore Megalithic Cemetery Structure close to P52 II 2015 09 08.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/5d8ab96d/Carrowmore_Megalithic_Cemetery_P7_2015_09_08.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
