nation''s capital Tbilisi in the river valley of Mashavera [1]. A series of skulls which had diverse physical traits, discovered at Dmanisi in the early 2000s, led to the hypothesis that many separate species in the genus Homo were in fact a single lineage [1]. The area around the town of Dmanisi has been settled since the Early Bronze Age [1]. Beyond a rich collection of ancient and medieval artifacts and the ruins of various buildings and structures, unique remains of prehistoric animals and humans have been unearthed [1]. The discovery of primitive stone tools in 1984 led to increasing interest to the archaeological site [1]. Early human fossils, originally named Homo georgicus and now considered Homo erectus georgicus, were found at Dmanisi between 1991 and 2005 [1]. At 1.8 million years old, they are now believed to be a subspecies of Homo erectus and not a separate species of Homo [1]. These fossils represent the earliest known human presence in the Caucasus [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:100018", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s009: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:100019", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s009: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:150979", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-013: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/5d97461b/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/5d97461b/Dmanisi_Sioni_cathedral.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:248805", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s008: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:248806", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s008: period_start None -> ''-4500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:349074", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s004: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''< 4500 B