tivity spanning from the Stone Age through the Bronze Age, Iron Age, and antiquity. In the Middle Ages, the settlement was part of Piast-ruled Poland and featured a motte-and-bailey castle. The village first appeared in written records during the 13th century. During WWII, it was the site of Polenlager 169, a camp for Polish civilians who resisted Nazi forced labor.'' -> ''Gorzyczki is a village in Gmina Gorzyce, Silesian Voivodeship, southern Poland, near the Czech border [1]. Archaeological excavations have uncovered traces of human activity spanning from the Stone Age through the Bronze Age, Iron Age, and antiquity [1] [2] [3]. In the Middle Ages, the settlement was part of Piast-ruled Poland and featured a motte-and-bailey castle [1] [2].''"}]'::jsonb),
