man-born mathematician who devoted over 50 years to studying and conserving the Nazca Lines. After her death in 1998 at age 95, the house was converted into a museum displaying her research materials, maps, blueprints, and archaeological finds. A waxwork of Reiche sits at her workspace, and she is buried on the grounds. The Nazca Lines she helped protect were designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1994.'' -> ''A waxwork of Reiche sits at her workspace, and she is buried on the grounds [1] [2]. The Nazca Lines she helped protect were designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1994 [3] [4].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:340529", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "insert-2026-10-07-005-001: thumbnail_url None -> ''/data/images/wiki/5ed144bd/Maria Reiche Nazca.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('5f10ac9c-682b-41cf-9eeb-7c2825b0ea4d'::uuid, 'antiquity', '10', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:5f10ac9c-682b-41cf-9eeb-7c2825b0ea4d:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''8'' restored on Atapuerca Mountains', '094b09aac2474843fcf59f794ef0f7b7', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 10 from period_start -1398050"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:84832", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2097:chunk-0001: description ''Karstic hill formation near Burgos, northern Spain, containing some of the richest hominin fossil deposits in the world. The Sima del Elefante cave has yielded remains dating to 1.2-1.4 million years ago, the oldest evidence of humans in Western Europe. Gran Dolina preserves Homo antecessor fossils from 800,000 BP showing the earliest known cannibalism. Sima de los Huesos has produced over 5,500 bones from 28 Homo heidelbergensis individuals. A UNESCO World Heritage Site since 2000.'' -> ''The Atapuerca Mountains is a karstic hill formation near the village of Atapuerca in the province of Burgos, northern Spain [1]. Rich fossil deposits and stone tool assemblages have been discovered which are attributed to the earliest known hominin residents in Western Europe [1]. The Atapuerca Mountains are a mid-altitude karstic range of small foothills around 1,080 m above sea level [1]. In 2008 scholars identified a new genus and species of red-toothed shrew from the Pleistocene layers of the Gran Dolina cave [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:117720", "url": "remediation_change_log"