]. The site is an important example of Inca architecture and sits at an altitude of 3,701 metres [1]. The Spanish chroniclers recorded that within the Inca''s oral history, the fortress was said to be built during the reign of Sapa Inca Pachacuti and his successors, Topa Inca Yupanqui and Huayna Capac [1]. Dry stone walls constructed of huge stones were built on the site, with the workers carefully cutting the boulders to fit them together tightly without mortar [1]. Archeological studies of surface collections of pottery at Sacsayhuamán indicate that the earliest occupation of the hilltop dates to about 900 CE [1]. During the 15th century, the Imperial Inca expanded on this settlement, building dry stone walls constructed of huge stones [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:100094", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s009: site_type ''Megalithic structures'' -> ''Fortress/citadel''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:151120", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-013: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/60/Sacsayhuam%C3%A1n%2C_Cusco%2C_Per%C3%BA%2C_2015-07-31%2C_DD_27.JPG'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/61e8d3cd/Sacsayhuamán,_Cusco,_Perú,_2015-07-31,_DD_21.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:333886", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-009-001: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/61e8d3cd/Sacsayhuamán,_Cusco,_Perú,_2015-07-31,_DD_21.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/61e8d3cd/Sacsayhuamán,_Cusco,_Perú,_2015-07-31,_DD_27.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('61f1e287-c3bc-40ad-b3fe-af956af4cb1b'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:61f1e287-c3bc-40ad-b3fe-af956af4cb1b:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''6'' restored on Tappoch Broch', '05589e0effa20ccae4a3f6c13e79f019', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (''Fortification'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 10 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28486", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0144:chunk-0001: site_type ''Minaret/tower'' -> ''Fortification''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34633", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0033:chunk-0001: description ''Tappoch Broch, also known as Torwood Broch, is an Iron Age broch situated atop Tappoch hill in Tor Wood forest near Falkirk, Scotland. One of the rare lowland brochs, it features walls up to 7 m thick with an internal courtyard spanning roughly 10.8 m by 9.8 m. First excavated in 1864 by Colonel Joseph Dundas, who removed some 200 tons of debris, the dig revealed querns, cup-and-ring marked stones, pottery, and steatite artifacts indicating trade connections with northern Scotland. Designated a scheduled monument since 1949, it underwent laser scanning and conservation work in 2014.'' -> \"Tappoch Broch, better known as Torwood Broch, is an iron-age broch located in a remote spot in dense woodland near Falkirk, Scotland [1]. Tappoch broch was first excavated in 1864 by Colonel Joseph Dundas [1]. The dry stonework is built of large, irregular stone blocks from the immediate vicinity [1]. The oval chamber measures 10.8 to 9.8 metres, is paved and has a large fire spot in the middle [1]. In the south-west of the chamber a short aisle branches off to the right and leads to an unusually well-preserved staircase in the wall [1]. Finds included saddle and rotary querns, a potential stone ''lamp'' and several sherds of coarse pottery [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:100095", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s009: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:100096", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s009: period_start ''1'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:151121", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-013: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/33/Tappoch_Broch_-_southern_side_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1024967.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/61f1e287/Torwood_2025_(83).webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:249201", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s009: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:249202", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s009: period_start None -> ''-800''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:311397", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-006: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/61f1e287/Torwood_2025_(83).webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/61f1e287/Tappoch Broch - southern side - geograph.org.uk - 1024967.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
