ssic Maya archaeological site in Cancun''s Hotel Zone, Quintana Roo, Mexico, discovered in 1923 by Raymond Merwin and Samuel Lothrop. Originally called Kin Ich Ahau Bonil (''king of the solar countenance''), the site spans the Late Preclassic through Late Post-Classic (300 BC-1500 AD). Its 47 structures served as a trade hub, royal burial ground, and astronomy centre along Caribbean maritime routes, with links to Chichen Itza and Edzna. Over 500 burials have been identified beneath residential platforms, and remains of post-Classic mural paintings survive on several structures.\" -> \"A post-Classic Maya archaeological site in Cancun''s Hotel Zone, Quintana Roo, Mexico [1] [2]. Remains of post-Classic mural paintings survive on several structures [1] [2].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:355869", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s002: site_type ''Settlement'' -> ''City/town/settlement''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('62131a06-8c8c-4c55-bf8a-c19abbca2048'::uuid, 'cultural_influence', '10', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:62131a06-8c8c-4c55-bf8a-c19abbca2048:cultural_influence', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: cultural_influence ''8'' restored on Tepoztlan', '5ac083dcc68d6fd9b51ff8420c3ea9e8', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "cultural_influence = 10 from description yes, thumbnail yes, 5 content link(s), 21 image(s), source_url yes"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29680", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0325:chunk-0001: period_start ''-3000'' -> ''-1500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30416", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-period-name: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:80930", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2013:chunk-0001: description \"Tepoztlan is home to El Tepozteco, an Aztec temple perched at 2,310 meters atop Tepozteco Hill in Morelos, Mexico. The temple was dedicated to Tepoztecatl, the god of pulque, and dates to the Postclassic period with construction beginning around 1200 CE under the Xochimilca. It consists of a 6.4-meter platform supporting a temple base with 13 steps symbolizing the 13 heavens of Mesoamerican cosmology. Inscribed stones bear the name of Aztec emperor Ahuizotl and the date 1502 CE. The site was abandoned during the Spanish conquest when Cortes''s forces burned Tepoztlan to the ground.\" -> ''Tepoztlán is derived from Nahuatl and means \"place of abundant copper\" or \"place of the broken rocks.\" This is derived from the words tepoz-tli and tlan [1]. The earliest findings of pottery and other ceramic utensils date back to approximately 1500 BCE [1]. At the top of a hill, there is a small pyramid devoted to Ometochtli-Tepoztēcatl, the supposed inventor of pulque, a beer-like drink made from the maguey plant [1]. The pyramid, called El Tepozteco, dates from the Post-Classic Period (900–1521 CE) and has inscriptions dating from 1452 and 1502 [1]. The archaeological site of El Tepozteco is centered on the 13th-century temple dedicated to Tepoztēcatl, god of pulque [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:151123", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-013: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Montaje_de_Tepoztlán.jpg/400px-Montaje_de_Tepoztlán.jpg'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:340574", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "insert-2026-10-07-005-001: thumbnail_url None -> ''/data/images/wiki/62131a06/Tepoztlan mountains2019.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
