[1]. The earliest findings of pottery and other ceramic utensils date back to approximately 1500 BCE [1]. At the top of a hill, there is a small pyramid devoted to Ometochtli-Tepoztēcatl, the supposed inventor of pulque, a beer-like drink made from the maguey plant [1]. The pyramid, called El Tepozteco, dates from the Post-Classic Period (900–1521 CE) and has inscriptions dating from 1452 and 1502 [1]. The archaeological site of El Tepozteco is centered on the 13th-century temple dedicated to Tepoztēcatl, god of pulque [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:151123", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-013: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Montaje_de_Tepoztlán.jpg/400px-Montaje_de_Tepoztlán.jpg'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:340574", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "insert-2026-10-07-005-001: thumbnail_url None -> ''/data/images/wiki/62131a06/Tepoztlan mountains2019.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
