und 3000 BC [1]. The skeletal remains of several people were uncovered in the tomb during excavation in 1867 [1]. The monument was partially restored and reconstructed after a second excavation during the early 1950s [1]. Quoyness chambered cairn is located on the Elsness peninsula [1]. The cairn sits on a wide, oval-shaped stone platform and was built with a mix of stones, earth and waste material intermixed with horizontal slabs [1]. A stone kerb surrounds the platform on the south and the west ends [1]. The cairn was excavated by James Farrer and George Petrie in 1867 [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:151710", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-014: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b3/Quoyness_cairn_%28cropped%29.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/657dbfe1/Quoyness_chambered_cairn_entrance.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:334502", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-009-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/657dbfe1/Quoyness_chambered_cairn_entrance.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/657dbfe1/Quoyness_cairn_(cropped).webp''"}]'::jsonb),
