    ('665a152b-5e67-4110-b58e-7f1bce3b739c'::uuid, 'antiquity', '5', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:665a152b-5e67-4110-b58e-7f1bce3b739c:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''8'' restored on Parque Arqueológico do Solstício', 'cd4fab1cf59be933411ee0179843b7ca', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 5 from period_start None"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:85022", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2102:chunk-0001: description ''The Parque Arqueológico do Solstício (also known as AP-CA-18) is an archaeological park in Amapá state, Brazil, near Calçoene. It features a megalithic stone circle nicknamed the \"Amazon Stonehenge,\" comprising 127 granite blocks standing up to 4 meters tall, arranged in a circle over 30 meters in diameter on a hilltop by the Rego Grande river[1]. Archaeologists believe indigenous peoples constructed it for astronomical, ceremonial, or burial purposes—likely a combination of these functions[1][2].'' -> ''The Parque Arqueológico do Solstício, referred to in academic sources as AP-CA-18, is an archaeological park located in Amapá state, Brazil, near the city of Calçoene [1]. It contains a megalithic stone circle, colloquially known as the Amazon Stonehenge, consisting of 127 blocks of granite, each up to 4 meters tall, standing upright in a circle measuring over 30 meters in diameter at the bank of the Rego Grande river on a hilltop [1]. The first reports of the Rego Grande site are from naturalist Emilio Goeldi in the late 19th century [1]. In 2005, archaeologists began exca