luc and remains closed to the public for scientific study [1].\" -> \"The Grotte de Cussac is a Paleolithic cave in France''s Dordogne valley containing over 150 engraved artworks depicting bison, horses, mammoths, rhinoceroses, ibex, birds, and female figures [1] [2]. It represents one of the few Paleolithic European sites associating parietal art with human burials, with remains of at least five individuals found alongside the engravings [1] [3]. The cave was discovered in September 2000 by amateur speleologist Marc Delluc and remains closed to the public [1] [4] [5].\""}]'::jsonb),
