rst city of Pisidia'' under Rome, reaching peak prosperity in the 2nd-3rd centuries AD. The well-preserved ruins include a 9,000-seat theatre, monumental fountains, colossal baths, and colonnaded streets, with up to 90% of original building elements recoverable during excavations. On the UNESCO Tentative List, it has been excavated since 1990 by the University of Leuven.\" -> \"An ancient city in southwestern Turkey''s Burdur Province, situated at 1,450-1,700m altitude in the Western Taurus Mountains [1] [2]. Sagalassos was conquered by Alexander the Great in 333 BC and became the ''first city of Pisidia'' under Rome, reaching peak prosperity in the 2nd-3rd centuries AD [1] [3] [4]. The well-preserved ruins include a 9,000-seat theatre, monumental fountains, colossal baths, and colonnaded streets, with up to 90% of original building elements recoverable during excavations [3] [5] [6]. On the UNESCO Tentative List, it has been excavated since 1990 [6] [7].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:355879", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s002: site_type ''City'' -> ''City/town/settlement''"}]'::jsonb),
