 333 BC and became the ''first city of Pisidia'' under Rome, reaching peak prosperity in the 2nd-3rd centuries AD. The well-preserved ruins include a 9,000-seat theatre, monumental fountains, colossal baths, and colonnaded streets, with up to 90% of original building elements recoverable during excavations. On the UNESCO Tentative List, it has been excavated since 1990 by the University of Leuven.\" -> \"An ancient city in southwestern Turkey''s Burdur Province, situated at 1,450-1,700m altitude in the Western Taurus Mountains [1] [2]. Sagalassos was conquered by Alexander the Great in 333 BC and became the ''first city of Pisidia'' under Rome, reaching peak prosperity in the 2nd-3rd centuries AD [1] [3] [4]. The well-preserved ruins include a 9,000-seat theatre, monumental fountains, colossal baths, and colonnaded streets, with up to 90% of original building elements recoverable during excavations [3] [5] [6]. On the UNESCO Tentative List, it has been excavated since 1990 [6] [7].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:355879", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s002: site_type ''City'' -> ''City/town/settlement''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('6cb8a98b-4816-4879-9de7-84095b4c0989'::uuid, 'mystery', '8', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:6cb8a98b-4816-4879-9de7-84095b4c0989:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''7'' restored on Judaculla Rock', '7a9bb3e3237ceb60087c5b209eaa1d0d', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 8 from (''Petroglyphs'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 5 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34877", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0035:chunk-0001: description ''Judaculla Rock is a curvilinear-shaped soapstone outcrop in Western North Carolina featuring ancient petroglyphs and carvings. The archaeological site occupies an 0.85-acre rectangular property owned by Jackson County, situated approximately 60 meters east of Caney Fork Creek, a major tributary of the northwestward-trending Tuckasegee River[1]. The soapstone formation in the Appalachian foothills contains multiple carved symbols and designs of unknown cultural significance. The site represents one of the most significant rock art locations in the southeastern United States.'' -> ''Judaculla Rock is a curvilinear-shaped outcrop of soapstone known for its ancient carvings and petroglyphs [1]. The petroglyph boulder occurs within a man-made bowl-shaped depression [1]. The surface of the westward-slanting main boulder is covered with petroglyphs [1]. It measures roughly 22 square meters [1]. The boulder carries scars resulting from soapstone bowl extraction [1]. The similarity between the concentric ring and cross-in-ring petroglyphs on the boulder with patterns on ceramics from the region suggests that the petroglyphs, made over the Late Archaic soapstone extraction scars, date somewhere between the Middle Woodland and late Mississippian culture periods, roughly 200 CE to 1400 CE [1]. Judaculla Rock retains a special significance for the Cherokee [1]. Judaculla Rock contains approximately 1,548 motifs [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:100938", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s010: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:100939", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s010: period_start ''1'' -> ''-2000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:152315", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-015: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/6cb8a98b/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/6cb8a98b/Judaculla Rock May 30 2021.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:312184", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-007: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/6cb8a98b/Judaculla Rock May 30 2021.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/6cb8a98b/Judaculla_Rock,_Caney_Fork,_NC_Aug_2019.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('6cb8a98b-4816-4879-9de7-84095b4c0989'::uuid, 'rarity_score', '46', '43', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:6cb8a98b-4816-4879-9de7-84095b4c0989:rarity_score', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: rarity_score ''43'' restored on Judaculla Rock', '7a9bb3e3237ceb60087c5b209eaa1d0d', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "rarity_score = 46 from mystery 8, cultural_influence 10"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34877", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0035:chunk-0001: description ''Judaculla Rock is a curvilinear-shaped soapstone outcrop in Western North Carolina featuring ancient petroglyphs and carvings. The archaeological site occupies an 0.85-acre rectangular property owned by Jackson County, situated approximately 60 meters east of Caney Fork Creek, a major tributary of the northwestward-trending Tuckasegee River[1]. The soapstone formation in the Appalachian foothills contains multiple carved symbols and designs of unknown cultural significance. The site represents one of the most significant rock art locations in the southeastern United States.'' -> ''Judaculla Rock is a curvilinear-shaped outcrop of soapstone known for its ancient carvings and petroglyphs [1]. The petroglyph boulder occurs within a man-made bowl-shaped depression [1]. The surface of the westward-slanting main boulder is covered with petroglyphs [1]. It measures roughly 22 square meters [1]. The boulder carries scars resulting from soapstone bowl extraction [1]. The similarity between the concentric ring and cross-in-ring petroglyphs on the boulder with patterns on ceramics from the region suggests that the petroglyphs, made over the Late Archaic soapstone extraction scars, date somewhere between the Middle Woodland and late Mississippian culture periods, roughly 200 CE to 1400 CE [1]. Judaculla Rock retains a special significance for the Cherokee [1]. Judaculla Rock contains approximately 1,548 motifs [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:100938", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s010: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:100939", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s010: period_start ''1'' -> ''-2000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:152315", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-015: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/6cb8a98b/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/6cb8a98b/Judaculla Rock May 30 2021.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:312184", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-007: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/6cb8a98b/Judaculla Rock May 30 2021.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/6cb8a98b/Judaculla_Rock,_Caney_Fork,_NC_Aug_2019.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
