e has since been visited and studied by rock art scholar Rainer Hostnig [1]. The site is made up of an array of deeply incised rock carvings that cover up to a height of 9 feet, a perpendicular rock face that is over 100 feet long and 75 feet high [1]. Its location is on the south shore of the Río Palatoa (designated on some maps at this location as the Porotoa, and known by others as the Palatoa Chico) [1]. The petroglyphs contain elements such as heart-shaped faces, some with double borders, spirals, zigzags, suns, \"curlicue Xs\" and others that defy verbal description [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:100940", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s010: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:100941", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s010: period_start ''1'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:152316", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-015: thumbnail_url None -> ''/data/images/wiki/6cc29702/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:238609", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s005: period_name None -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:238610", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s005: period_start None -> ''-2000''"}]'::jsonb),
