    ('6d28ceb7-10be-442f-be2e-cab319d32763'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:6d28ceb7-10be-442f-be2e-cab319d32763:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''6'' restored on Brane Barrow', '4b0d4ac683aea0c0e23d038ea0a580f7', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Barrow'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 34 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:85158", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2106:chunk-0001: description ''Brane Barrow, also known as Chapel Euny Barrow, is a Neolithic entrance grave near the hamlet of Brane in Cornwall, considered one of the smallest and best-preserved burial monuments in Britain. The circular mound of earth and stones measures up to 6.1 m in diameter, surrounded by large granite blocks, and contains a rectangular stone-lined chamber 2.3 m long, 1.2 m wide, and 0.9 m high, roofed with two surviving capstones. Entrance graves of this type, dating to c. 2500-1000 BC, are unique to western Cornwall and the Isles of Scilly. The barrow was first documented in 1863 by antiquarian William Copeland Borlase.'' -> ''Brane Barrow, or Chapel Euny Barrow, is a Neolithic entrance grave located near the hamlet of Brane, Cornwall, England [1]. It is considered to be one of the smallest and best preserved burial monuments in Britain [1]. Brane Barrow is a scheduled burial monument located 317 yards south west of Brane End Farm, and 1.2 miles southwest of the village of Sancreed, in Cornwall [1]. The monument includes an entrance grave, situated in a low-lying pasture close to the upper Lamorna River [1]. The barrow is a circular mound of earth and stones, measuring up to 20 feet in diameter [1]. The mound is surrounded and retained by a border of large granite blocks [1]. It contains a rectangular stone-lined chamber measuring 7.5 ft long by 4 ft wide and 3 ft high [1]. The chamber is roofed with two capstones: a third capstone was removed at some point [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:312193", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-007: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/6d28ceb7/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/6d28ceb7/Brane burial chamber - geograph.org.uk - 1040.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
