    ('6d2b0b49-565b-41ed-8763-aa0f35d7c627'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:6d2b0b49-565b-41ed-8763-aa0f35d7c627:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''5'' restored on Roman Theatre, Zaragoza', 'd503a73bf16eea8c9a784e62f1b3b995', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Theatre'', ''1 - 500 AD'') is shared by 22 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29433", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0288:chunk-0001: period_start ''1'' -> ''100''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35821", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: period_start ''100'' -> ''1''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44491", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0095:chunk-0001: description ''Constructed during the reigns of Tiberius (14-37 AD) and Claudius (41-54 AD) in the Roman colony of Caesaraugusta, this theatre followed the model of the Theatre of Marcellus in Rome. With a capacity of 6,000 spectators and an area of 7,000 square metres, it is considered the largest Roman theatre discovered in Spain. Active until the 3rd century, its stone was later repurposed for walls and buildings before being forgotten under new housing. Rediscovered in 1973 through archaeological excavations, it was declared a Bien de Interes Cultural in 2001 and is now preserved within the Caesaraugusta Theatre Museum.'' -> \"The Roman Theater of Zaragoza is a Roman theatre in the Roman colonia of Caesaraugusta –present-day Zaragoza, Spain–, in the Roman province of Hispania Tarraconensis [1]. The construction of the Roman theater of Caesaraugusta began in the early 1st century during the reign of the Roman Emperor Tiberius [1]. It was completed under the rule of Emperor Claudius by the mid-1st century AD [1]. Covering an area of 7,000 square meters (with a diameter of 106 meters), it ranks as one of the largest theaters in Roman Hispania [1]. The facade was adorned with ashlar blocks in the ''opus quadratum'' style, rising to an external height of three stories and twenty-two meters [1]. The theater''s decline began in the 3rd century when stones were repurposed to fortify the city walls and other constructions [1]. Over the years, the theater was buried beneath subsequent constructions [1]. It remained obscured until the 1970s when excavations brought it back into the light [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:152323", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-015: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2b/Teatro_romano_de_Caesaragusta%2C_Zaragoza_05.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/6d2b0b49/Teatro_romano_de_Caesaragusta,_Zaragoza_05.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
