y [1] [3] [4]. The site represents one of the earliest known human settlements on Cyprus, showcasing the pre-ceramic phase of the Neolithic period [1] [4] [5].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('6ebfa164-e396-45a5-82fb-2e1cb9fe841a'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:6ebfa164-e396-45a5-82fb-2e1cb9fe841a:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Jogimara Caves', '65bf263eb3b260a093dea023df96cd6c', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Cave Structures'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 19 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:85208", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2108:chunk-0001: description \"The Jogimara and Sitabenga Caves are ancient rock-cut monuments on the western slope of Ramgarh Hill in Surguja district, Chhattisgarh, dated to the 3rd-2nd century BCE. Jogimara contains India''s oldest surviving fresco paintings, executed in crimson, white, and gamboge over a plaster base, depicting human figures, animals, and mythical motifs. Brahmi inscriptions in Magadhi Prakrit mention Sutanuka, a temple dancer, and Devadinna, a sculptor. The adjacent Sitabenga cave, with its terraced benches, is interpreted by some scholars as one of India''s oldest performance theatres. Both are ASI-protected monuments.\" -> ''The Sitabenga and Jogimara Caves, sometimes referred to either as Sitabenga Cave or Jogimara Cave, are ancient cave monuments nested in the north side of Ramgarh hills in Puta village, Chhattisgarh [1]. Dated between the 3rd-century BCE to 1st-century BCE, they are notable for their non-religious inscriptions in Brahmi script and Magadhi language, and one of the oldest colored frescoes in Asia [1]. The caves are partly natural and partly sculpted [1]. The Jogimara and Sitabenga caves are in Surguja district of Chhattisgarh, about 45 kilometers southwest of Ambikapur, 180 kilometers northeast of Bilaspur city [1]. Both caves have inscriptions [1]. According to Richard Salomon – a scholar of Indian epigraphy, Sanskrit and Buddhist studies, a paleographic analysis of these inscriptions in the pure Magadhi language suggest that they are from the 3rd-century BCE [1]. The Sitabenga cave has a two line inscription in Brahmi script close to the top of cut out wall [1]. The Jogimara cave has a five line inscription, also in Brahmi script and Magadhi language [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
