 roughly oval enclosure measures 140 by 100 metres [2] [4]. One of only about 150 such hillforts in England, it may have served as a stock enclosure, redistribution centre, or settlement [2] [3]. The wider site is a National Nature Reserve, SSSI, and Special Area of Conservation renowned for its ancient pollarded beech trees [1] [5] [6].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('6f502f33-3251-4054-b334-4fa52ea7b671'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:6f502f33-3251-4054-b334-4fa52ea7b671:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Coldrum Long Barrow', '3a2f4284fc9558db2b185a2eb0cdb37e', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Barrow'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 33 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:85216", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2108:chunk-0001: description \"The Coldrum Long Barrow, also known as the Coldrum Stones and the Adscombe Stones, is a chambered long barrow located near the village of Trottiscliffe in the south-eastern English county of Kent[1]. Probably constructed in the fourth millennium BCE, during Britain''s Early Neolithic period, today it survives only in a state of ruin[1].\" -> \"The Coldrum Long Barrow, also known as the Coldrum Stones and the Adscombe Stones, is a chambered long barrow located near the village of Trottiscliffe in the south-eastern English county of Kent [1]. Probably constructed in the fourth millennium BCE, during Britain''s Early Neolithic period, today it survives only in a state of ruin [1]. Archaeologists have established that the monument was built by pastoralist communities shortly after the introduction of agriculture to Britain from continental Europe [1]. Built out of earth and around fifty local sarsen-stone megaliths, the long barrow consisted of a sub-rectangular earthen tumulus enclosed by kerb-stones [1]. Within the eastern end of the tumulus was a stone chamber, into which human remains were deposited on at least two separate occasions during the Early Neolithic [1]. Osteoarchaeological analysis of these remains has shown them to be those of at least seventeen individuals, a mixture of men, women, and children [1]. The ruin attracted the interest of antiquarians in the 19th century, while archaeological excavation took place in the early 20th [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:118936", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s005: site_type ''Megalithic stones'' -> ''Barrow''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:152390", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-015: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/6f502f33/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/6f502f33/Coldrum Long Barrow 03.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:312239", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-007: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/6f502f33/Coldrum Long Barrow 03.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/6f502f33/Chamber_of_Coldrum_Long_Barrow_from_the_East.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
