s from the Korean Three Kingdoms period[1]. The site occupies a strategic position along the Han River floodplain, likely serving defensive and administrative functions for ancient settlements in the region[1].'' -> ''Located at the edge of the Han River in present-day Songpa-gu district, this flat earthen wall forms a long oval shape extending north-south and slightly leaning eastward [1]. The massive structure encompasses a circumference of approximately 3.5 kilometers (2.2 miles), making it one of the larger known earthworks from the Korean Three Kingdoms period [1] [2] [3].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('70c0458f-b0a0-458e-9755-6b9e39153342'::uuid, 'mystery', '3', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:70c0458f-b0a0-458e-9755-6b9e39153342:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Cave of Altxerri', 'af401eb889fba69cba7b5bedd8c47fb2', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 3 from (''Cave Structures'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 113 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:85262", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2109:chunk-0001: description ''The Cave of Altxerri is located in the municipality of Aya (Gipuzkoa) in the Basque Country (Spain) [1]. The original grotto preserves rock paintings and engravings from the end of the Upper Magdalenian period within the Upper Paleolithic [1]. The paintings in the upper gallery, known as Altxerri B, were dated in a 2013 study to approximately 39,000 years old, making them the oldest stone paintings in Europe [1]. Its artistic style forms part of the Franco-Cantabrian tradition [1].'' -> ''The Cave of Altxerri is located in the municipality of Aya in the Basque Country [1]. The original grotto preserves rock paintings and engravings which have been dated from the end of the Upper Magdalenian period, within the Upper Paleolithic; the pictures situated in an upper gallery, known as Altxerri B, have been dated in a 2013 study as the oldest stone paintings in Europe, with an estimated age of 39,000 years [1]. Its artistic style forms part of the so-called Franco-Cantabrian School [1]. Altxerri houses one of the largest sets of rock engravings of the area [1]. It contains around one hundred and twenty engravings of which ninety-two are of animals [1]. The bison is the best-represented animal, with a total of fifty-three engravings [1]. Other animals present in the cave are the reindeer, with six engravings, four deer and goats, three horses and aurochs, two saiga antelope, a wolverine, a fox, a hare and a bird [1]. It was declared a World Heritage Site in 2008 as part of the group known as the Cave of Altamira and Paleolithic Cave Art of Northern Spain [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:152430", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-015: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/70c0458f/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/70c0458f/Altxerri Cave Ibex.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:323120", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-004-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/70c0458f/Altxerri Cave Ibex.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/70c0458f/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:325264", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-005-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/70c0458f/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/70c0458f/Altxerri cave - Antelope.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
