scovered in the late 1960s, although it has yet to undergo thorough archaeological investigation [1]. The barrow stands on a sandy sub-soil that is part of the Lenham Beds [1]. At its eastern end, the long barrow is 2 metres high, and a little less than that at its western end [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:101742", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s011: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:101743", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s011: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:153064", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-016: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/37/Slope_of_Shrub%27s_Wood_Long_Barrow.jpg'' -> \"/data/images/wiki/76220eb6/Slope_of_Shrub''s_Wood_Long_Barrow.webp\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:238691", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s005: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:238692", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s005: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}]'::jsonb),
