the site forms a circular earthwork, 27.47 metres across and 2.74 metres high, with a ditch on the north-west and south-east sides, on the top of a ridge, overlooking a brook which runs into the River Cary and the old Roman road of the Fosse Way [1]. In Roman times, Wimble Toot was situated at a crossroads [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:101748", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s011: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:101749", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s011: period_start ''-3000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:153067", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-016: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/29/Wimble_Toot_01.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/7667ebc2/Wimble Toot 02.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:249761", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s010: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:249762", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s010: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:323180", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-004-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/7667ebc2/Wimble Toot 02.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/7667ebc2/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:325273", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-005-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/7667ebc2/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/7667ebc2/Wimble Toot 01.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:349964", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s005: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:349965", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s005: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('766f1ffd-9ef3-4b3e-9127-cceea9f98540'::uuid, 'mystery', '2', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:766f1ffd-9ef3-4b3e-9127-cceea9f98540:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''6'' restored on Castle Folds', '42517c1ed0974e7da6403527c950941e', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 2 from (''City/town/settlement'', ''1 - 500 AD'') is shared by 180 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:85414", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2113:chunk-0001: description ''Castle Folds, also known as Castlesteads, is a Romano-British defended stone-walled settlement on Great Asby Scar, an area of limestone pavement in the parish of Asby, Cumbria. The settlement occupies a flat-topped limestone knoll, enclosed by walls originally 2.4 metres thick and thought to have stood around 3-4 metres high with a parapet. Built by native inhabitants rather than Roman occupiers, it was constructed purely for defence against a specific major threat, with its considerable proportions reflecting the seriousness of that danger. The site contains hut circles within the enclosure walls. In medieval times it was reused as a shieling for summer livestock grazing. It is now in ruins but protected as a scheduled monument.'' -> ''Castle Folds or Castlesteads was a Romano-British walled settlement on Great Asby Scar – an area of limestone pavement in the parish of Asby, Cumbria [1]. In medieval times, it was used as a shieling – a temporary summer shelter [1]. It is now in ruins but is protected as a scheduled monument [1]. The settlement covered most of a small knoll on the limestone pavement of the scar [1]. A walled enclosure surrounded most of the knoll and, on the western side, the eight-foot thick walls extended the steep escarpment of the knoll to form a significant obstacle [1]. The area enclosed is about 1.25 acres [1]. There were about a dozen stone huts built into the walls of the enclosure so that the central space was open [1]. The wall was constructed from orthostats which enclosed rubble [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:101750", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s011: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:101751", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s011: period_start ''1'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:101752", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s011: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Settlement''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:249763", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s010: period_name None -> ''1 - 500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:249764", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s010: period_start None -> ''1''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:355888", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s002: site_type ''Settlement'' -> ''City/town/settlement''"}]'::jsonb),
