ltural tools, Celtic coins such as the Nauheim Quinarius likely minted on-site, and evidence of iron and bronze working. The settlement declined in the mid-1st century BC.\" -> \"The Heidetrank Oppidum near Oberursel in the Taunus mountains is one of Europe''s most important Celtic oppida, dating to the late La Tene period [1] [2]. Enclosed by a 10 km rampart with six gates and distinctive zangentor (pincer gates), it straddled both sides of the Heidetrankthal ravine [1] [3] [4]. Archaeological finds include agricultural tools, Celtic coins such as the Nauheim Quinarius likely minted on-site [1]. The settlement declined in the mid-1st century BC [1] [3].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:355893", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s002: site_type ''Settlement'' -> ''City/town/settlement''"}]'::jsonb),
