nown in Portugal. The menhirs have a star-like appearance with an ellipsoidal cross-section and are engraved with circles and dimples. Discovered in 1992, a recovery project later restored and re-erected the stones in their original cavities.'' -> ''A group of menhirs in the parish of Alcoutim e Pereiro, Algarve, Portugal, likely erected during the second half of the 4th millennium BC [1] [2]. The largest stands over 3 metres tall and is carved from greywacke, a dark grey stone with a brown patinated surface, making it the largest example of greywacke menhirs known in Portugal [1] [2] [3]. The menhirs have an ellipsoidal cross-section and are engraved with circles and dimples [2] [4].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('77e3f3a1-163d-4925-88c5-86456fc81dd7'::uuid, 'mystery', '3', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:77e3f3a1-163d-4925-88c5-86456fc81dd7:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Vučedol', '7cf0d940477499f8cc05b60ca3fe27b4', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 3 from (''City/town/settlement'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 105 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:85454", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2114:chunk-0001: description \"Vučedol (''Wolf''s Valley''), located 5 km downstream from Vukovar on the Danube''s right bank in Croatia, is one of Europe''s most important Eneolithic sites. Its six-metre-thick cultural layer spans from the Starcevo culture (5500-4300 BC) through the eponymous Vucedol culture (3000-2200 BC), which pioneered innovations such as four-wheel carriages and copper casting. The most famous find is the Vucedol Dove, a bird-shaped vessel discovered at 4.4 m depth that has become a symbol of Croatia. Once home to approximately 3,000 inhabitants, the site held a European Heritage Label recognizing its significance.\" -> ''Vučedol became the eponym of the eneolithic Vučedol culture [1]. It is estimated that the site had once been home to about 3,000 inhabitants [1]. Some of the most important archaeological discoveries belonging to the Vučedol culture have been made at this site [1]. Archaeological research has confirmed finds of the Baden culture, Vučedol culture as well as Kostolac culture on the site of Vučedol [1]. The site is located roughly 5 kilometres downstream from the city of Vukovar [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:101789", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s011: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:101790", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s011: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:153143", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-016: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Vucedol_Culture_Museum.jpg/400px-Vucedol_Culture_Museum.jpg'' -> ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/35/ZGvucdove.jpg''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:239555", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s006: period_name None -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:239556", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s006: period_start None -> ''-6000''"}]'::jsonb),
