 is situated on a south facing promontory at the end of a 240m high ridge in the Blackdown Hills [1]. Originally a Neolithic site, an Iron Age hill fort was later built on the same site [1]. It was excavated between 1930 and 1935 by Dorothy Liddell [1]. She identified a timber framed entrance to the causewayed enclosure and an oval arrangement of postholes in the middle which she interpreted as being a building destroyed by fire before the enclosure earthworks were built [1]. During an excavation in the 1980s headed by Malcolm Todd, archaeological evidence was found on the site of Roman military occupation, suggesting a fort within the existing Iron Age site [1]. The site has given its name to some of the earliest Neolithic pottery in southern Britain after a large collection of pieces of this type of pottery were found during excavations by Dorothy Liddell [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:101798", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s011: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:101799", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s011: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:153171", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-016: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/786c39f1/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/786c39f1/Hembury Hill III.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:250123", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s011: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:250124", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s011: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:313006", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-008: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/786c39f1/Hembury Hill III.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/786c39f1/Payhembury, Hembury hillfort - geograph.org.uk - 47183.web