    ('7a9ec11e-65bd-4030-bf21-499f9cac6168'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:7a9ec11e-65bd-4030-bf21-499f9cac6168:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''6'' restored on Maeshowe', 'd9c9656271842b8a1b35558db779c2bc', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Cairn'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 35 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:85498", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2115:chunk-0001: description ''Maeshowe is a Neolithic chambered cairn and passage grave on Mainland Orkney, Scotland, constructed around 2800 BC [1]. It is the type site for the Maeshowe type of chambered cairn, found exclusively in Orkney [1]. Archaeological evidence indicates it served as a collective burial site during the Neolithic period [1].'' -> ''Maeshowe is a Neolithic chambered cairn and passage grave situated on Mainland Orkney [1]. The grass-covered mound that encloses the tomb measures about 35 metres in diameter and rises to a height of 7.3 metres [1]. Beneath the mound lies a carefully constructed stone monument composed of a passage and central chamber built from large slabs of local flagstone [1]. The monument is aligned so that sunlight enters the passage at the winter solstice and reaches the rear wall of the central chamber [1]. Maeshowe was built by Neolithic farming communities living in central Orkney during the late fourth and early third millennia BC [1]. During the twelfth century Norse visitors broke into the mound and entered the central chamber [1]. Inside the chamber the visitors carved more than thirty runic inscriptions into the stone walls [1]. These carvings form the largest single collection of runic inscriptions known from one location [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:153731", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-017: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/7a9ec11e/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/7a9ec11e/Maeshowe exterior.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:313033", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-008: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/7a9ec11e/Maeshowe exterior.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/7a9ec11e/MaesHowe.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
