    ('7ef03e67-8439-4a61-84ab-c5e92424e17a'::uuid, 'antiquity', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:7ef03e67-8439-4a61-84ab-c5e92424e17a:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''4'' restored on Saticula', '2a82e0dc581e1d8248a9e88341794142', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 5 from period_start None"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:85598", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2118:chunk-0001: description \"Saticula was an ancient Samnite (Caudini) fortified settlement near the border of Campania in southern Italy, whose remains lie beneath the modern town of Sant''Agata de'' Goti. In 343 BC during the First Samnite War, Roman forces captured the city, marking a pivotal moment in Rome''s southward expansion. It later became a Roman colony after the Second Punic War. Excavations have uncovered a Samnite necropolis with tombs and artefacts, while remnants of Roman infrastructure include ancient roads and sections of the Augustan aqueduct that supplied Capua. The medieval Ducal Castle was built directly atop ancient Samnite foundations from Saticula.\" -> \"Saticula was a Caudini city near the frontier of Campania in southern Italy [1]. In 343 BC, during the First Samnite War, the Roman consul Cornelius attacked it during the campaign against the Samnites in the Battle of Saticula [1]. Its archaeological remains are in the territory of the modern town of Sant''Agata de'' Goti [1]. Ceramic evidence from Saticula and nearby Caudium suggest that the two cities were part of a trade network along the Volturno 