    ('7f4207f8-30df-4aa9-850e-6873fcab2bb3'::uuid, 'cultural_influence', '6', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:7f4207f8-30df-4aa9-850e-6873fcab2bb3:cultural_influence', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: cultural_influence ''4'' restored on Fairy Toot', '5493d7c89cc65710d5f49e94883c2e09', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "cultural_influence = 6 from description yes, thumbnail yes, 0 content link(s), 2 image(s), source_url yes"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:85622", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2119:chunk-0001: description ''Built 4500–3000 BC, Fairy Toot is an extensive oval barrow in Nempnett Thrubwell, Somerset. It is an example of the Severn-Cotswold tomb type, a chambered long barrow featuring precisely-built trapezoid earth mounds covering burial chambers. The mound measures 60 metres long and 25 metres wide and is a Scheduled Ancient Monument [1].'' -> ''The Fairy Toot is an extensive oval barrow in the civil parish of Nempnett Thrubwell, Somerset [1]. Fairy Toot was formerly a chambered cairn which is a Scheduled Ancient Monument [1]. Located south-southwest of Howgrove Farm, it is a mound 60 m long, 25 m wide and now 2.5 m high [1]. Formerly it was considerably higher [1]. On being opened and essentially destroyed between 1787 and 1835 by the Reverend Thomas Bere of Butcombe and the Reverend John Skinner of Camerton, it was found to contain two rows of cells, running from south to north, formed by immense stones set edgeways [1]. A human skull from the barrow is now in the Bristol Museum and Art Gallery [1]. At the time it was conjectured to be a work of the Druids, but its origins are far older and probably date from the Neolithic period [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:102554", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s012: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:102555", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s012: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:153955", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-017: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/7f4207f8/hero.webp'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:250189", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s011: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:250190", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s011: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:331963", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-006-001: thumbnail_url None -> ''/data/images/wiki/7f4207f8/Fairy Toot Field - geograph.org.uk - 2655676.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
