jectured to be a work of the Druids, but its origins are far older and probably date from the Neolithic period [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:102554", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s012: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:102555", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s012: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:153955", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-017: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/7f4207f8/hero.webp'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:250189", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s011: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:250190", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s011: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:331963", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-006-001: thumbnail_url None -> ''/data/images/wiki/7f4207f8/Fairy Toot Field - geograph.org.uk - 2655676.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('7f5c1a4a-0004-410a-a275-ecd2e8368867'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:7f5c1a4a-0004-410a-a275-ecd2e8368867:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Cavernas de Marabar', 'ec187cc9bb1075437ec1dce0833f10c8', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Cave Structures'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 19 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:85624", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2119:chunk-0001: description \"A group of seven rock-cut caves in Bihar''s Jehanabad district, India, recognized as the oldest surviving rock-cut caves in the country, dating to the Maurya Empire (3rd century BCE). Four caves sit on Barabar Hill (Karan Chaupar, Lomas Rishi, Sudama, Visvakarma) and three on nearby Nagarjuni Hill. Inscriptions record that Emperor Ashoka granted the Sudama Cave to Ajivika monks in 261 BCE. The interiors display the distinctive highly polished ''Mauryan polish'' that creates remarkable acoustic effects. The Lomas Rishi Cave features the oldest chaitya arch in India, an architectural form that profoundly influenced later Buddhist rock-cut design.\" -> ''The Barabar caves greatly influenced the tradition of rock-cut architecture in the Indian subcontinent [1]. The Ashoka inscriptions in the Barabar Caves are part of Ashoka\\''s \"Minor Rock Edicts\", and appear in the three caves named Sudama, Visvakarma and Karna Chopar [1]. Dasaratha Maurya, Ashoka\\''s grandson and regnal successor, wrote dedicatory inscriptions in the three other caves, forming the Nagarjuni group of the Barabar hills [1]. The three caves were offered to the Ajivikas upon the accession to the throne of Dasaratha, confirming that these were still active around 230 BCE, and that Buddhism was not the exclusive religion of the Mauryas at that time [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:153958", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-017: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/7f5c1a4a/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/7f5c1a4a/Barabar_Caves_inside_Lomas_Rishi.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:313128", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-008: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/7f5c1a4a/Barabar_Caves_inside_Lomas_Rishi.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/7f5c1a4a/Lomas_Rishi_entrance.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
