ns in the three other caves, forming the Nagarjuni group of the Barabar hills [1]. The three caves were offered to the Ajivikas upon the accession to the throne of Dasaratha, confirming that these were still active around 230 BCE, and that Buddhism was not the exclusive religion of the Mauryas at that time [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:153958", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-017: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/7f5c1a4a/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/7f5c1a4a/Barabar_Caves_inside_Lomas_Rishi.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:313128", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-008: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/7f5c1a4a/Barabar_Caves_inside_Lomas_Rishi.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/7f5c1a4a/Lomas_Rishi_entrance.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
