he Ringwall (circular rampart) was one of the largest and most elevated in eastern Hesse [1]. It originally had three gates, only one of which — the Tor am Kälberhutstein — was passable by wagons [1]. The main fortification was a 1,300 metres long stone wall that surrounded the north, east and south sides of the hill at its base [1]. It was discovered as ancient in origin by R. Virchow (1821–1902) [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:120217", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s006: source_url ''https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milseburg'' -> ''https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oppidum_Milseburg''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:154546", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-018: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Rhoen_Milseburg.jpg/400px-Rhoen_Milseburg.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/81ee931d/Rhoen Milseburg.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
