    ('84b456ff-0b44-4d75-92a4-5cbe22026b95'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:84b456ff-0b44-4d75-92a4-5cbe22026b95:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''8'' restored on Richat Structure', 'ebeae50c8babc723321f67aa1b7407ea', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Geological interest'', ''Undated'') is shared by 14 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:81058", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2018:chunk-0001: description ''The Richat Structure, known as the Eye of Africa, is a deeply eroded geological dome approximately 40-50 km in diameter on the Adrar Plateau in Mauritania near Ouadane. Formed by a subsurface igneous intrusion during the Cretaceous Period (145-66 million years ago), its concentric rings of sedimentary and igneous rock have been exposed through erosion. The site holds archaeological significance with exceptional accumulations of Acheulean and pre-Acheulean stone tools, including hand axes indicating prehistoric hunting and manufacturing activities. First photographed by astronauts during the 1965 Gemini 4 mission, it was included among 100 IUGS geological heritage sites in 2022.'' -> ''The Richat Structure, or Guelb er Richât, often called the Eye of Africa is a prominent circular geological feature at the northwestern edge of the Taoudeni Basin, on the Adrar Plateau of the Sahara [1]. It is located near Ouadane in the Adrar Region of Mauritania [1]. The Richat Structure is a deeply eroded, slightly elliptical dome with a diameter of 40 kilometres [1]. The sedimentary rock exposed in this dome ranges in age from Late Proterozoic within the center of the dome to Ordovician sandstone around its edges [1]. The Richat Structure is the location of exceptional accumulations of Acheulean artifacts [1]. These Acheulean archaeological sites are located along wadis that occupy the outermost annular depression of this structure [1]. Pre-Acheulean stone tools also have been found in the same areas [1]. Sparse and widely scattered Neolithic spear points and other artifacts have also been found [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:120225", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s006: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:120226", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s006: period_start ''1'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:304117", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04e_period-label-undated: period_name None -> ''Undated''"}]'::jsonb),
