    ('86066b6b-c86e-48ad-90b7-9099ca1e5e58'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:86066b6b-c86e-48ad-90b7-9099ca1e5e58:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''6'' restored on Limes Germanicus', '0024f1dc74c7b37c783c4cdc2b82b535', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (''Fortification'', ''1 - 500 AD'') is shared by 9 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35109", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0041:chunk-0001: description ''The Limes Germanicus was a Roman frontier defense system separating the Empire from Germanic tribes from 83 to about 260 AD[1]. It spanned Germania Inferior, Germania Superior and Raetia, combining natural barriers like rivers with earthworks, wooden palisades and watchtowers[1]. A network of linked forts stood behind the frontier[1].'' -> \"The Limes Germanicus, or ''Germanic Limes'', is the name given in modern times to a line of frontier fortifications that bounded the ancient Roman provinces of Germania Inferior, Germania Superior and Raetia, separating the Roman Empire and the unsubdued Germanic tribes from about 83 AD to 260 AD [1]. The frontier used either a natural boundary such as a river or typically an earth bank and ditch with a wooden palisade and watchtowers at intervals [1]. The path of the limes changed over time following advances and retreats in response to external threats [1]. At its height, the Limes Germanicus stretched from the North Sea outlet of the Rhine to near Regensburg (Castra Regina) on the Danube [1]. These two major rivers afforded natural protection from mass incursions into imperial territory, with the exception of a gap stretching roughly from Mogontiacum (now Mainz) on the Rhine to Castra Regina [1]. The limes itself is a relatively simple construction [1]. The limes had a deeper ditch and a higher mound than a camp fortification [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:120250", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s006: site_type ''Fortress/citadel'' -> ''Fortification''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:154635", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-018: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/86066b6b/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/86066b6b/Hansselmann Fortsetzung Beweiss.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:313815", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-009: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/86066b6b/Hansselmann Fortsetzung Beweiss.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/86066b6b/Rätische_Mauer,_WP_12-77.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
