rigin [1]. Gunung Padang consists of a series of five artificial terraces—one rectangular and four trapezoidal—that occur, in succession, at progressively higher elevations [1]. The terraces are aligned along a central longitudinal NW–SE axis [1]. They are artificial platforms created by lowering elevated areas and filling in depressions until a flat surface was achieved [1]. The perimeters of the terraces feature retaining walls constructed from volcanic polygonal columns [1]. Pottery fragments found at the site were dated by the bureau of archaeology in the range 45 BCE–22 CE [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:120314", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s006: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:120315", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s006: period_start ''100'' -> ''-117''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:155304", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-019: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/8a66f7db/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/8a66f7db/Situs_Megalitikum_Gunung_Padang_Cianjur.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
