    ('8aa48093-66ab-456b-bbd6-b2ba884d47ed'::uuid, 'cultural_influence', '6', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:8aa48093-66ab-456b-bbd6-b2ba884d47ed:cultural_influence', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: cultural_influence ''4'' restored on Cival', 'acd54e4805718b6c29824f023598a9d6', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "cultural_influence = 6 from description yes, thumbnail yes, 0 content link(s), 2 image(s), source_url yes"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:85872", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2126:chunk-0001: description ''Cival is a major Preclassic Maya site in the Peten Basin of Guatemala, about 40 km east of Tikal, that flourished from the 6th century BC through the 1st century AD with a peak population of around 10,000. Excavations led by Francisco Estrada-Belli revealed a ceremonial center spanning half a mile, featuring a Preclassic E-Group astronomical complex dating to c. 900 BC. Two monumental 5x3-metre stucco masks flanking a pyramid stairway display Olmecoid were-jaguar features and maize deity iconography. A ritual cache contained 120 polished jade pieces, five jade axes, and spondylus shells -- among the earliest evidence of public accession rituals in the Maya lowlands. Stela 2, dating to c. 300 BC, ranks among the oldest known Maya lowland sculptures.'' -> ''Cival is an archaeological site in the Petén Basin region of the southern Maya lowlands, which was formerly a major city of the Pre-Columbian Maya civilization [1]. The site flourished from about the 6th century B.C. through the 1st century C.E., during the Preclassic Period [1]. The site is about 25 miles east of Tikal, on a ridge over the left bank of the Holmul river [1]. The site has temples on step pyramids and plazas arranged to point to astronomical events such as the equinox sunrise, and is surrounded by a defensive wall [1]. The site\\''s largest step pyramid is 27 metres high and 70 x 40 metres wide [1]. Some buildings were decorated with stucco sculptures depicting Mesoamerican deities [1]. The site was long lost in the jungle, and was discovered and partly plundered by looters around 1980 [1]. It was first mapped by explorer Ian Graham in 1984, who gave it the name Cival after a local word for \"lagoon\" [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:120325", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s006: lat ''17.391450022956363'' -> ''17.36439''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:120326", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s006: lon ''-89.25451097082332'' -> ''-89.25375''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:155312", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-019: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/8aa48093/hero.webp'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:340874", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "insert-2026-10-07-005-001: thumbnail_url None -> \"/data/images/wiki/8aa48093/Cap, Cival, museu Nacional d''Arqueologia i Etnologia de Guatemala.webp\""}]'::jsonb),
