od [1]. The site is about 25 miles east of Tikal, on a ridge over the left bank of the Holmul river [1]. The site has temples on step pyramids and plazas arranged to point to astronomical events such as the equinox sunrise, and is surrounded by a defensive wall [1]. The site\\''s largest step pyramid is 27 metres high and 70 x 40 metres wide [1]. Some buildings were decorated with stucco sculptures depicting Mesoamerican deities [1]. The site was long lost in the jungle, and was discovered and partly plundered by looters around 1980 [1]. It was first mapped by explorer Ian Graham in 1984, who gave it the name Cival after a local word for \"lagoon\" [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:120325", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s006: lat ''17.391450022956363'' -> ''17.36439''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:120326", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s006: lon ''-89.25451097082332'' -> ''-89.25375''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:155312", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-019: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/8aa48093/hero.webp'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:340874", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "insert-2026-10-07-005-001: thumbnail_url None -> \"/data/images/wiki/8aa48093/Cap, Cival, museu Nacional d''Arqueologia i Etnologia de Guatemala.webp\""}]'::jsonb),
