    ('8c624c02-31b5-4058-8551-f7cec08e6359'::uuid, 'mystery', '3', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:8c624c02-31b5-4058-8551-f7cec08e6359:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''6'' restored on Bülövqaya', '55702ba72118aa3317725639c270b43d', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 3 from (''City/town/settlement'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 105 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:32789", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0006:chunk-0001: description ''Bulovqaya (Bulovkaya) is a Chalcolithic settlement on the left bank of the Sarisu river near Goynuk village in the Babek district of Nakhchivan, Azerbaijan, dating to the 5th millennium BC. Ceramics found at the site bear close resemblance to Dalma-type pottery from northwestern Iran, and Ubaid-style pottery confirms the northward diffusion of Mesopotamian cultural elements into the South Caucasus. The settlement is considered a possible stronghold or colony of bearers of Middle Eastern cultural traditions, with obsidian sourced from the South Caucasus and Eastern Anatolia highlighting extensive interregional exchange networks.'' -> ''Bülövqaya is a Chalcolithic archaeological settlement near the village of Göynük in the Babek District of the Nakhchivan Autonomous Republic, Azerbaijan [1]. The site lies on the left bank of the Sarısu River at an elevation of about 1,430 to 1,452 m and covers approximately 1.14 ha [1]. Radiocarbon dates place the principal occupation of the site in the early to mid-fifth millennium BC [1]. Excavated material includes plain and painted pottery, obsidian and flint tools, bone artefacts, architectural remains, hearths, storage pits and pottery kilns [1]. Excavation identified two construction levels distinguished by soil colour and structure [1]. An analysis of 74 obsidian specimens found that material from the Zangezur Mountains predominated, with a smaller component attributed to Meydan Dağ in eastern Anatolia [1]. The architectural remains indicate groups of light rectangular structures arranged around working areas [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:120344", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s006: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Settlement''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:239719", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s006: lat ''39.29122731996842'' -> ''39.2834''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:239720", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s006: lon ''45.659775840734284'' -> ''45.6382''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:355914", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s002: site_type ''Settlement'' -> ''City/town/settlement''"}]'::jsonb),
