 [1]. At that time Tulamba was ruled by the Moi people, who fought Alexander''s forces [1]. The ruins of the ancient city are situated on the edge of the modern city [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:103428", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s013: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:103429", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s013: period_start ''-1500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:155371", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-019: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/54/Ruins_of_Old_Tulamba_-_panoramio.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/8dace4e6/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:250689", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s012: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:250690", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s012: period_start None -> ''-1500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:325298", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-005-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/8dace4e6/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/8dace4e6/Ruins of Old Tulamba - panoramio.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:357263", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-25p_fields-wd5-s006: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:357264", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-25p_fields-wd5-s006: period_start ''-1500'' -> ''-500''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8de309a1-1291-4878-8f63-694b2276b934'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:8de309a1-1291-4878-8f63-694b2276b934:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''5'' restored on Roman Theatre of Orange', '58e6f88f23d719f3d42ac2c2dc451807', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Theatre'', ''1 - 500 AD'') is shared by 22 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28850", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0203:chunk-0001: site_type ''Megalithic structures'' -> ''Theatre''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43561", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0076:chunk-0001: description ''One of the best-preserved Roman theatres in the world, built in the 1st century AD in Orange (ancient Colonia Arausio), Vaucluse, France. It seated approximately 9,000 spectators in a cavea built into St. Eutrope hill. The massive scaenae frons (stage wall) measures 103 m long, 37 m high, and 1.8 m thick -- called by Louis XIV \"the most beautiful wall in my kingdom\". A 3.5 m statue of Augustus dominates the central niche. UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1981. Restoration began in 1825, and the annual Choregies d\\''Orange opera festival originated from performances here in 1869.'' -> ''The Roman Theatre of Orange is a Roman theatre in Orange, Vaucluse, France [1]. It was built early in the 1st century AD [1]. The Orange theatre was created under the rule of Augustus, and is believed to be one of the first of its kind in this area of modern-day France [1]. One of the most iconic parts of this structure is the grand exterior facade, which measures 103 meters long and 37 meters high [1]. Originally, there was a wooden roof across the theatre to protect the audience from unfavorable weather conditions [1]. There is evidence on the walls that shows that, at some point, the roof was destroyed in a fire [1]. As the Western Roman Empire declined during the 4th century, by which time Christianity had become the official religion, the theatre was closed by official edict in AD 391 [1]. Reconstruction began in 1825 with the intent of returning the theatre to its original purpose: to host large, exciting performances [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:155377", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-019: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c2/Roman_Theatre_in_Orange_2008.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/8de309a1/(Orange (Vaucluse). Théâtre romain) - Fonds Trutat - 51Fi444.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:334586", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-009-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/8de309a1/(Orange (Vaucluse). Théâtre romain) - Fonds Trutat - 51Fi444.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/8de309a1/Roman_Theatre_in_Orange_2008.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8de309a1-1291-4878-8f63-694b2276b934'::uuid, 'rarity_score', '40', '37', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:8de309a1-1291-4878-8f63-694b2276b934:rarity_score', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: rarity_score ''37'' restored on Roman Theatre of Orange', '58e6f88f23d719f3d42ac2c2dc451807', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "rarity_score = 40 from mystery 6, cultural_influence 10"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28850", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0203:chunk-0001: site_type ''Megalithic structures'' -> ''Theatre''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43561", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0076:chunk-0001: description ''One of the best-preserved Roman theatres in the world, built in the 1st century AD in Orange (ancient Colonia Arausio), Vaucluse, France. It seated approximately 9,000 spectators in a cavea built into St. Eutrope hill. The massive scaenae frons (stage wall) measures 103 m long, 37 m high, and 1.8 m thick -- called by Louis XIV \"the most beautiful wall in my kingdom\". A 3.5 m statue of Augustus dominates the central niche. UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1981. Restoration began in 1825, and the annual Choregies d\\''Orange opera festival originated from performances here in 1869.'' -> ''The Roman Theatre of Orange is a Roman theatre in Orange, Vaucluse, France [1]. It was built early in the 1st century AD [1]. The Orange theatre was created under the rule of Augustus, and is believed to be one of the first of its kind in this area of modern-day France [1]. One of the most iconic parts of this structure is the grand exterior facade, which measures 103 meters long and 37 meters high [1]. Originally, there was a wooden roof across the theatre to protect the audience from unfavorable weather conditions [1]. There is evidence on the walls that shows that, at some point, the roof was destroyed in a fire [1]. As the Western Roman Empire declined during the 4th century, by which time Christianity had become the official religion, the theatre was closed by official edict in AD 391 [1]. Reconstruction began in 1825 with the intent of returning the theatre to its original purpose: to host large, exciting performances [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:155377", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-019: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c2/Roman_Theatre_in_Orange_2008.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/8de309a1/(Orange (Vaucluse). Théâtre romain) - Fonds Trutat - 51Fi444.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:334586", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-009-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/8de309a1/(Orange (Vaucluse). Théâtre romain) - Fonds Trutat - 51Fi444.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/8de309a1/Roman_Theatre_in_Orange_2008.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
