dern-day France [1]. One of the most iconic parts of this structure is the grand exterior facade, which measures 103 meters long and 37 meters high [1]. Originally, there was a wooden roof across the theatre to protect the audience from unfavorable weather conditions [1]. There is evidence on the walls that shows that, at some point, the roof was destroyed in a fire [1]. As the Western Roman Empire declined during the 4th century, by which time Christianity had become the official religion, the theatre was closed by official edict in AD 391 [1]. Reconstruction began in 1825 with the intent of returning the theatre to its original purpose: to host large, exciting performances [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:155377", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-019: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c2/Roman_Theatre_in_Orange_2008.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/8de309a1/(Orange (Vaucluse). Théâtre romain) - Fonds Trutat - 51Fi444.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:334586", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-009-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/8de309a1/(Orange (Vaucluse). Théâtre romain) - Fonds Trutat - 51Fi444.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/8de309a1/Roman_Theatre_in_Orange_2008.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
