ut the lintel over the inner end is still apparent [1]. There appear to be two guard cells on either side of the entrance passage [1]. Internally four mural cells can be seen, two of which are dumb-bell shaped with short passages between the two halves [1]. Borgarfjörð, the \"fjord of the borg\" was so named by the Norse on account of the broch [1]. The broch was visited by George Low in the 18th century, when he noted a number of cells in the base of the wall, and he stated that a hollow, galleried wall began above these [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:251063", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s013: period_name None -> ''500 - 1000 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:251064", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s013: period_start None -> ''800''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:350892", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s006: period_name ''500 - 1000 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:350893", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s006: period_start ''800'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
