    ('934ed584-2c13-46fc-bf38-eadd53b68d6f'::uuid, 'mystery', '3', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:934ed584-2c13-46fc-bf38-eadd53b68d6f:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Settlements of the Cucuteni-Trypillia Culture', '3d187e514bc361a3892fdebe840750ca', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 3 from (''City/town/settlement'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 105 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86104", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2133:chunk-0001: description \"The Cucuteni-Trypillia culture (c. 5500-2750 BC) left behind thousands of settlement ruins across modern Moldova, Romania, and Ukraine, spanning 350,000 sq km from the Carpathians to the Dnieper. During its middle phase (c. 4100-3500 BC), the culture built some of Eurasia''s largest settlements -- proto-cities of 100-400 hectares with thousands of buildings, predating Sumerian cities by over 500 years. Settlements such as Talianki may have housed up to 15,000 people across 340 hectares. The inhabitants deliberately burned their dwellings every 60-80 years before resettling, a practice requiring over 130 trees'' worth of firewood per single-story structure. They produced distinctive painted pottery, anthropomorphic figurines, and copper jewelry.\" -> ''The study of the settlements of the Cucuteni-Trypillia culture provides important insights into the early history of Europe [1]. The Cucuteni-Trypillia culture, which existed in the present-day southeastern European nations of Moldova, Romania, and Ukraine during the Neolithic Age and Copper Age, from approximately 5500 to 2750 BC, left behind thousands of settlement ruins containing a wealth of archaeological artifacts attesting to their cultural and technological characteristics [1]. Maidanetske may have contained almost 3,000 houses and a population between 12,000 and 46,000 [1]. Academicians have not designated the gigantic settlements of Cucuteni-Trypillia culture as \"cities\" due to the lack of conclusive evidence for internal social differentiation or specialization [1]. The existence of the giant settlements was discovered in the 1960s [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:156039", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-020: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Talianki_(Trypillian_city).jpg/400px-Talianki_(Trypillian_city).jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/934ed584/Maidanetske_-_Cover_Ohlrau_Dissertation_by_Susanne_Beyer.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
