    ('95981699-02b0-4ab9-9ccc-cd9efc14f97a'::uuid, 'total_power', '29', '27', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:95981699-02b0-4ab9-9ccc-cd9efc14f97a:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: total_power ''27'' restored on Flagstones Enclosure', '49ca0363e17cf0fe9d49cc3ed0b882c6', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 29 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 8 + 6 + 8 + 6 + 1"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86152", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2134:chunk-0001: description \"Flagstones Enclosure is a late Neolithic interrupted ditch enclosure located on the outskirts of Dorchester, Dorset, England [1]. It derives its name from being discovered beneath the site of the demolished Flagstones House [1]. Half of the enclosure was excavated in the 1980s during construction of the Dorchester bypass; the remaining portion survives beneath the grounds of Max Gate, Thomas Hardy''s house [1][2].\" -> \"Flagstones is a late Neolithic interrupted ditch enclosure (similar to a causewayed enclosure) on the outskirts of Dorchester, Dorset, England [1]. It derives its name from having been discovered beneath the site of the demolished Flagstones House [1]. Half of it was excavated in the 1980s when the Dorchester by-pass was built; the rest of it still exists under the grounds of Max Gate, author and architect Thomas Hardy''s former house [1]. The part of the enclosure in the grounds of Flagstones House was excavated by Wessex Archaeology [1]. The enclosure comprised a circular ring of unevenly spaced pits constructed in the late 4th millennium BC [1]. The chalk walls of some of the pit/ditch segments featured engraved designs, probably cut with flint [1]. An adult cremation and two child inhumations were found at the bottom of ditch sections, each beneath a slab of sandstone or sarsen [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
