al stones of varying heights, with the largest being approximately four metres tall [1]. No carved or painted symbols have been detected, except for a few pairs of small circular cavities, possibly representing eyes [1]. A small number of items have been found to the west of the complex, including flints from the Lower Paleolithic, ceramic fragments and other items from the Neolithic period, and ceramic fragments from the Iron Age, suggesting that the site has been reused over time [1]. The complex was not identified as a megalithic site until 1961 when it was studied by Gil Estevam Miguéis Andrade and Eduardo Prescott Vicente [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:104255", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s014: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:104256", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s014: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:156700", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-021: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/97eeca75/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/97eeca75/ConjuntoBarreira2.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:251131", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s013: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:251132", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s013: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}]'::jsonb),
