    ('99c67365-3376-46ae-9e60-0d2ec03bfff4'::uuid, 'mystery', '10', '9', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:99c67365-3376-46ae-9e60-0d2ec03bfff4:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''9'' restored on Coventina', 'ea037a86a96c36e83db5a33367e4c4ac', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 10 from (''Well'', ''1 - 500 AD'') is shared by 1 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:32793", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0006:chunk-0001: description \"Coventina was a Romano-British goddess of wells and springs venerated at a sacred well near the Roman fort of Brocolitia (Carrawburgh) on Hadrian''s Wall in Northumberland. The shrine, excavated in 1876, consisted of a rectangular stone basin surrounded by a walled enclosure approximately 12 m square over a natural spring. Excavations uncovered extraordinary votive deposits including about 16,000 coins, ten inscribed altars to Coventina and Minerva, two dedication slabs, a sculpture of three water nymphs, and clay incense burners. Evidence suggests the shrine was abruptly closed around AD 388, possibly linked to the anti-pagan edicts of Theodosius I.\" -> ''Dedications to Coventina and votive deposits were found in a walled area which had been built to contain the outflow from a spring now called \"Coventina\\''s Well\" [1]. The well itself was a spring in a rectangular basin 2.6m x 2.4m in the centre of a walled enclosure 11.6m x 12.2m within a wall 0.9m thick [1]. The contents of the well included 13,487 coins from Mark Anthony to Gratian, a relief of three water nymphs, the head of a male statue, two dedication slabs to the goddess Coventina, ten altars to Coventina and Minerva, two clay incense burners, and a wide range of votive objects [1]. Excavation of the site revealed several inscribed altars, some with depictions of Coventina in typical Roman nymph form: reclining, partially clothed and associated with water [1]. At least ten inscriptions to Coventina are recorded from Carrawburgh [1]. Several stone altars contained dedications to Coventina as did two pottery incense burners [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:121550", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s007: site_type ''Rock relief/carving'' -> ''Well''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:121551", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s007: source_url ''https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coventina'' -> ''https://keystothepast.info/search-records/results-of-search/results-of-search-2/site-details/?PRN=N7931''"}]'::jsonb),
