    ('9a219745-8e06-4d8a-8396-c5d5656990a5'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:9a219745-8e06-4d8a-8396-c5d5656990a5:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''6'' restored on Wideford Hill Chambered Cairn', 'f5e3673ec234be8ca25b53556f7461eb', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Cairn'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 35 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86276", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2137:chunk-0001: description ''Wideford Hill Chambered Cairn is a Neolithic chambered cairn on Mainland, Orkney, Scotland. The tomb dates to around 2000 BC, making it approximately 4,000 years old[1]. It is similar in design to the famous Maeshowe Chambered Cairn on Orkney[1]. The monument is situated on a steep west-facing hill northwest of Wideford Hill, overlooking the Bays of Firth and Kirkwall[1]. Historic Environment Scotland established the site as a scheduled monument in 1994, providing legal protection for this significant prehistoric heritage site[1].'' -> ''Wideford Hill chambered cairn is a Neolithic chambered cairn on Mainland, Orkney in Scotland [1]. The tomb dates to around 2000 BC, and is similar in design to the Maeshowe chambered cairn on Orkney [1]. The monument is situated on a steep west facing hill northwest of Wideford Hill and faces the Bays of Firth and Kirkwall [1]. The visible masonry shows the different construction stages of the Neolithic cairn with its three concentric walls built on an earthen platform [1]. The monument has a stepped facade, but the original shape was probably rounded or conical, with the stonework covered in clay and turf [1]. The site was excavated in the 1840s by George Petrie [1]. He did not uncover any human remains or other finds at the time and suggested that the burial monument had been filled with rubble and abandoned [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:156766", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-021: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/22/Wideford_Hill_Chambered_Cairn_showing_original_entrance.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/9a219745/Wideford Hill Chambered Cairn entrance from inside.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:314761", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-010: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/9a219745/Wideford Hill Chambered Cairn entrance from inside.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/9a219745/Wideford_Hill_Chambered_Cairn_showing_original_entrance.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
