nsive remains from the Hellenistic period [1]. Settlement at Aigeira is known from the Middle Neolithic and Final Neolithic, beginning around 5500 BCE [1]. The first settlement was situated on the acropolis, and has furnished evidence of pottery, including vessels likely used in the production of cheese [1]. Some evidence of Neolithic settlement has been found on a lower plateau, approximately 150m to the east of the acropolis [1]. Like many Aegean sites, Aigeira was abandoned at the end of the Bronze Age, following the destruction of the site at the end of LH IIIC Middle [1]. Occupation began again in the second half of the 8th century BCE, likely associated with the sanctuary at the site [1]. Some partial fortifications are known from the Archaic period, covering an area of around 3.5 hectares [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:104305", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s014: period_name ''< 4500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:104306", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s014: period_start ''-5500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:156777", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-021: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/9ab9a6fa/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/9ab9a6fa/Aigeira2.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:240690", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s007: period_name None -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:240691", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s007: period_start None -> ''-5500''"}]'::jsonb),
