    ('9ae31b41-d78d-44da-824d-43487ec611f0'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:9ae31b41-d78d-44da-824d-43487ec611f0:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Combe Hill, East Sussex', '2cbdd26e9ad0ade970c8c9d066938509', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Earthwork'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 13 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29136", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0244:chunk-0001: period_start ''-4500'' -> ''-3001''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:32421", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-23_mechanical-journal-reversal-2: period_start ''-3001'' -> ''-4500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36949", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0047:chunk-0001: description ''Combe Hill is a Neolithic causewayed enclosure on the South Downs near Eastbourne, East Sussex, dating to c. 3700–3500 BC [1]. It features an inner circuit of ditches and banks, incomplete where a steep slope interrupts it on the north side, plus remains of an outer circuit [1]. Causewayed enclosures are defined by discontinuous ditches creating gaps or causeways; their function remains unknown, though they may have served as settlements, gathering sites, or ritual locations [1].'' -> \"Combe Hill is a causewayed enclosure, near Eastbourne in East Sussex, on the northern edge of the South Downs [1]. It consists of an inner circuit of ditches and banks, incomplete where it meets a steep slope on its north side, and the remains of an outer circuit [1]. The enclosure has been excavated twice: in 1949, by Reginald Musson, and in 1962, by Veronica Seton-Williams [1]. Charcoal fragments from Musson''s dig were later dated to between 3500 and 3300 BC [1]. Musson also found a large quantity of Ebbsfleet ware pottery in one of the ditches [1]. Seton-Williams found three polished stone axes deposited in another ditch, perhaps not long after it had been dug [1]. The site is only 800 m from Butts Brow, another Neolithic enclosure, and the two locations are visible from each other; both sites may have seen Neolithic activity at the same time [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
