e on its north side, and the remains of an outer circuit [1]. The enclosure has been excavated twice: in 1949, by Reginald Musson, and in 1962, by Veronica Seton-Williams [1]. Charcoal fragments from Musson''s dig were later dated to between 3500 and 3300 BC [1]. Musson also found a large quantity of Ebbsfleet ware pottery in one of the ditches [1]. Seton-Williams found three polished stone axes deposited in another ditch, perhaps not long after it had been dug [1]. The site is only 800 m from Butts Brow, another Neolithic enclosure, and the two locations are visible from each other; both sites may have seen Neolithic activity at the same time [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
    ('9afe9a14-d2a8-495a-b0d3-ddb86a761a31'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:9afe9a14-d2a8-495a-b0d3-ddb86a761a31:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''6'' restored on Bryn Cader Faner', '57f85a00198eda0a0c94d57417b150b8', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Cairn'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 35 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28324", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0111:chunk-0001: period_start ''-3000'' -> ''-2300''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35904", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: period_start ''-2300'' -> ''-3000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86304", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2138:chunk-0001: description ''Bryn Cader Faner is a Bronze Age round cairn near Talsarnau in the Ardudwy area of Gwynedd, Wales, dating to the late 3rd millennium BC. The cairn measures 8.7 m in diameter with 18 surviving thin jagged stone pillars jutting outward from a low burial mound, originally numbering around 30 pillars each about 2 m long. Described by archaeologist Aubrey Burl as one of the wonders of prehistoric Wales, the monument contained a central cist burial. The site was damaged by 19th-century treasure hunters who dug out the centre, and further harmed before World War II when the British Army used it for gunnery practice, destroying several stones on the east side.'' -> \"The Bryn Cader Faner is a Bronze Age round cairn which lies to the east of the small hamlet of Talsarnau in the Ardudwy area of Gwynedd in Wales [1]. The diameter is 8.7 metres and there are 18 thin jagged pillars which jut upwards from the low cairn [1]. It is thought to date back to the late third millennium BC [1]. The site was disturbed by 19th-century treasure hunters, who left a hole in the centre, indicating the position of a cist or a grave [1]. Originally there may have been about 30 pillars, each some 2 metres long [1]. Bryn Cader Faner is thought to mean ''the hill with the chair with the flag'' or ''the hill of the throne with the flag'' [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:156795", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-021: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/9afe9a14/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/9afe9a14/Bryn Cader Faner 1.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
