ce for the early development of agriculture, but this is now deprecated [1]. The site is situated at an elevation of 650 m above sea level on a hillside overlooking a small stream [1]. It was excavated in the mid-1960s by Chester Gorman [1]. Human presence at the site is documented from the Upper Paleolithic to the early Neolithic, but the primary occupation layers represent Hoabinhian hunter-gatherers [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:341594", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "insert-2026-10-07-006-001: thumbnail_url None -> ''/data/images/wiki/9c64a3f6/Spirit Well Cave 1.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('9c7230d0-e962-4c5f-95c0-3b3ea8cf1b4c'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:9c7230d0-e962-4c5f-95c0-3b3ea8cf1b4c:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Offham Hill', 'd72fcf34e8404b996425abb6f3bd70f1', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Earthwork'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 13 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36959", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0047:chunk-0001: description ''Offham Hill is a causewayed enclosure near Lewes, East Sussex, England, dating to approximately 3700–3300 BC [1]. The site was identified as a possible causewayed enclosure in 1964 by a member of the Sussex Archaeological Society, and the Ordnance Survey recommended excavation in 1972 [1]. Peter Drewett conducted the excavation in 1976 [1]. The ditches are characterized by interruptions or gaps known as causeways [1]. The purpose of causewayed enclosures remains unknown; they may have functioned as settlements, meeting places, or ritual sites [1].'' -> \"Offham Hill is a causewayed enclosure near Lewes, East Sussex, England [1]. The Ordnance Survey inspected the site in 1972 and recommended an excavation, which was carried out in 1976 by Peter Drewett [1]. The majority of Drewett''s finds came from the ditches, including about 7,000 worked flints, nearly 300 sherds of pottery, a human burial, other human bone, and animal remains [1]. Most of the pottery was identified as Neolithic, and radiocarbon dating of charcoal found in one of the ditches confirmed that the enclosure dated to the Neolithic [1]. A reanalysis of the radiocarbon dates in 2011 concluded that the enclosure was constructed in the mid-fourth millennium BC [1]. The site is on the north face of Offham Hill, above the Ouse valley, on the edge of the South Downs, north of Lewes [1]. The eastern part of the site was completely destroyed by a chalk quarry during the 19th century [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:104354", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s014: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:104355", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s014: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:156896", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-021: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Malling_Brooks_near_Lewes_-_geograph.org.uk_-_108799.jpg/400px-Malling_Brooks_near_Lewes_-_geograph.org.uk_-_108799.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/9c7230d0/Plan_of_Offham_Hill_excavation_1976.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:240712", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s007: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:240713", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s007: period_start None -> ''-3500''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('9c7230d0-e962-4c5f-95c0-3b3ea8cf1b4c'::uuid, 'rarity_score', '32', '26', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:9c7230d0-e962-4c5f-95c0-3b3ea8cf1b4c:rarity_score', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: rarity_score ''26'' restored on Offham Hill', 'd72fcf34e8404b996425abb6f3bd70f1', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "rarity_score = 32 from mystery 6, cultural_influence 7"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36959", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0047:chunk-0001: description ''Offham Hill is a causewayed enclosure near Lewes, East Sussex, England, dating to approximately 3700–3300 BC [1]. The site was identified as a possible causewayed enclosure in 1964 by a member of the Sussex Archaeological Society, and the Ordnance Survey recommended excavation in 1972 [1]. Peter Drewett conducted the excavation in 1976 [1]. The ditches are characterized by interruptions or gaps known as causeways [1]. The purpose of causewayed enclosures remains unknown; they may have functioned as settlements, meeting places, or ritual sites [1].'' -> \"Offham Hill is a causewayed enclosure near Lewes, East Sussex, England [1]. The Ordnance Survey inspected the site in 1972 and recommended an excavation, which was carried out in 1976 by Peter Drewett [1]. The majority of Drewett''s finds came from the ditches, including about 7,000 worked flints, nearly 300 sherds of pottery, a human burial, other human bone, and animal remains [1]. Most of the pottery was identified as Neolithic, and radiocarbon dating of charcoal found in one of the ditches confirmed that the enclosure dated to the Neolithic [1]. A reanalysis of the radiocarbon dates in 2011 concluded that the enclosure was constructed in the mid-fourth millennium BC [1]. The site is on the north face of Offham Hill, above the Ouse valley, on the edge of the South Downs, north of Lewes [1]. The eastern part of the site was completely destroyed by a chalk quarry during the 19th century [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:104354", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s014: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:104355", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s014: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:156896", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-021: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Malling_Brooks_near_Lewes_-_geograph.org.uk_-_108799.jpg/400px-Malling_Brooks_near_Lewes_-_geograph.org.uk_-_108799.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/9c7230d0/Plan_of_Offham_Hill_excavation_1976.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:240712", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s007: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:240713", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s007: period_start None -> ''-3500''"}]'::jsonb),
