 and weights and measures. Only scant limestone foundations and rock-cut foundation trenches survive today.'' -> ''The Chalkotheke (Greek for \"bronze store\") was a structure on the Athenian Acropolis, Greece [1]. Its name and function are only known from 4th century BC inscriptions [1]. One decree orders the listing of all objects stored in the Chalkotheke and the erection of a stele inscribed with that list in front of the building [1]. Remains of a structure discovered to the east of the sanctuary of Artemis Brauronia and immediately to the southwest of the Parthenon have been suggested to be those of the Chalkotheke [1]. Only scant limestone foundations and rock-cut foundation trenches survive [1]. The building stood in front of the southern Acropolis wall and was circa 43 m long and 14 m wide [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:121585", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s007: site_type ''Archaeological site'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('9cf1ec14-dd93-4270-897f-158203e91758'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '9', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:9cf1ec14-dd93-4270-897f-158203e91758:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''9'' restored on Dun Dornaigil', '6627a255a50b8b1ad1dcd82491254da2', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (''Minaret/tower'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 7 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:81132", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2021:chunk-0001: description \"Dun Dornaigil (also Dun Dornadilla) is an Iron Age broch in Strathmore, Sutherland, probably built in the 1st century BC. Its most striking feature is a massive triangular lintel stone above the entrance, considered the best surviving example of its kind in Scotland. The surrounding wall rises to approximately 7 metres on the northeastern side, with the rest standing 2-3 metres high. The structure features the classic broch design of two concentric walls with a stone staircase spiraling between them to the top. Remarkably, Dun Dornaigil''s interior has never been archaeologically excavated, making it exceptionally rare among Scotland''s 500-plus known brochs. The broch controlled a key route from the coast into the interior of northern Sutherland and is now in the care of Historic Environment Scotland.\" -> ''Dun Dornaigil is an Iron Age broch in Sutherland in the Scottish Highlands [1]. It is in Strathmore on the eastern bank of the Strathmore River [1]. The broch has an external diameter of about 14.5 metres [1]. The walls of Dun Dornaigil generally survive from 2 to 3 metres around the circumference of the broch, but above the doorway they rise to nearly 7 metres [1]. The entrance is on the northeast side but is filled with debris [1]. There is a massive triangular lintel over the entrance which measures 1.4 metres along the base, and 0.9 metres in height [1]. The interior of the broch is still filled with collapsed rubble from the upper levels and is therefore not accessible [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:156909", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-021: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/9cf1ec14/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/9cf1ec14/Dun Dornaigil, masonry.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:314830", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-010: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/9cf1ec14/Dun Dornaigil, masonry.webp'' -> \"/data/images/wiki/9cf1ec14/Broch_Dun_Dornaigil''s_door_lintel.webp\""}]'::jsonb),
