 site near the Dead Sea in Jordan, at the entrance to a natural cave, which Catholics believed to have been the one where Lot and his daughters sought shelter after Sodom was destroyed [1]. The monastery, dating to the 5th-7th centuries, is centered around a basilical church and overlooks from a steep slope the southeastern Dead Sea and the modern town of Safi in Jordan [1]. The oldest material found inside the cave suggests a sizable Early and Middle Bronze Age presence, followed by Nabataean pottery, which is indicative of a settlement there during the first centuries BC and AD [1]. Two inscriptions on the church''s mosaic floor bear dates from the 7th century: construction in April 606, and a renovation in Xanthikos (roughly May) 691 [1]. Habitation only ceased after the Early Abbasid period [1]. The monastery had a large basilical church 18 x 17 meters long, a reservoir, a hostel complex, and access to a wide range of foods including fruits, meats, and fish from the Red Sea [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:104381", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s014: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:104382", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s014: period_start ''-3000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:157514", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-022: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/9e723832/hero.webp'' -> ''https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Lot%27s_cave_1.jpg/1280px-Lot%27s_cave_1.jpg''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:240725", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s007: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:240726", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s007: period_start None -> ''-3300''"}]'::jsonb),
