    ('9e90cd99-0d35-478e-9763-3b168e92a60d'::uuid, 'mystery', '3', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:9e90cd99-0d35-478e-9763-3b168e92a60d:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Cave of the Trois-Frères', 'f8cc14fe02bb42b1cb61dfcbc896049e', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 3 from (''Cave Structures'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 113 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36991", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0048:chunk-0001: description ''A renowned Magdalenian cave in Montesquieu-Avantes, Ariege, southwestern France, discovered in 1914 by the three sons of Count Henri Begouen. The deep chamber known as the Sanctuary contains roughly 280 superimposed engravings and paintings of bison, horses, stags, reindeer, ibex, and mammoths, dating to approximately 14,000 years ago. Its most famous image, the Sorcerer, is a small painted-and-engraved figure positioned 4 meters above the floor, combining features of several animals in human form and widely interpreted as a shamanic or spiritual entity. A separate Chapel of the Lioness features a large lioness engraving on a natural altar surrounded by votive objects.'' -> ''The Cave of the Trois-Frères is a cave in southwestern France famous for its paintings [1]. It is located in Montesquieu-Avantès, in the Ariège département [1]. The cave is named for three brothers, Max, Jacques, and Louis Begouën, who, along with their father, Henri Begouën, discovered it in 1912 [1]. The drawings in the cave were made famous in the publications of the archeologist Henri Breuil [1]. The cave art appears to date to around 15,000 years ago [1]. One of the paintings, known as \"the Sorcerer\", is the \"most famous and enigmatic human figure\", with the features of several different animals, whose exact characteristics remain a matter of debate [1]. A variety of engraved animals are found on the cave walls, including lions, owls, and bison [1]. Of particular note is a horse overlaid with claviform (club-like) symbols and an apparently speared brown bear vomiting blood [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:104385", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s014: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:104386", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s014: period_start ''-500'' -> ''-13000''"}]'::jsonb),
