er of the two stones, weighing an estimated 62 tons, and along with three others formed a four-sided cove [1]. Excavations carried out jointly by the Universities of Leicester, Newport and Southampton in 2000 revealed the socket holes for the other stones which were tightly placed close to Adam [1]. The cove had been open on its south-eastern side which faced towards the nearby South Street barrow, 130m away [1]. The other stones were removed in the post-medieval period by a local landowner [1]. Adam fell over in 1911 and was re-erected by Maud Cunnington in 1912, at the direction of the Wiltshire Archaeological Society [1]. She also found a Beaker inhumation of a middle-aged man buried close by the stone [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105056", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s015: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105057", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s015: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-2900''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:157555", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-022: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/88/Longstones%2C_Geograph.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/a06ad29e/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:314888", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-010: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/a06ad29e/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/a06ad29e/Longstones, Geograph.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('a06ad29e-06f0-450b-bf8e-cd3b7231f096'::uuid, 'total_power', '30', '31', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:a06ad29e-06f0-450b-bf8e-cd3b7231f096:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: total_power ''31'' restored on The Longstones', 'ddf3cf531a8097b94f284caa2da0f5c0', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 30 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 7 + 8 + 9 + 5 + 1"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36999", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0048:chunk-0001: description \"Two prehistoric standing stones known as ''Adam'' and ''Eve'' near Beckhampton in Avebury parish, Wiltshire. Adam, the larger stone weighing an estimated 62 tons, is the surviving component of a Neolithic cove similar to the one in Avebury''s north circle. Eve likely formed part of the Beckhampton Avenue, a curving stone avenue connecting to Avebury that was long debated until archaeological investigations at the turn of the 21st century uncovered 120 metres of avenue socket holes in double rows. The cove appears aligned to the mid-winter sunrise. In 1912, Maud Cunnington re-erected Adam after it fell in 1911 and discovered a Beaker-period burial of a middle-aged man nearby.\" -> \"The Longstones are two standing stones, one of which is the remains of a prehistoric ''cove'' of standing stones, at grid reference SU089693, close to Beckhampton in Avebury parish [1]. Two stones are visible, known as ''Adam'' and ''Eve'', although the latter is more likely to have been a stone that formed part of the Beckhampton Avenue that connected with Avebury [1]. Adam is the larger of the two stones, weighing an estimated 62 tons, and along with three others formed a four-sided cove [1]. Excavations carried out jointly by the Universities of Leicester, Newport and Southampton in 2000 revealed the socket holes for the other stones which were tightly placed close to Adam [1]. The cove had been open on its south-eastern side which faced towards the nearby South Street barrow, 130m away [1]. The other stones were removed in the post-medieval period by a local landowner [1]. Adam fell over in 1911 and was re-erected by Maud Cunnington in 1912, at the direction of the Wiltshire Archaeological Society [1]. She also found a Beaker inhumation of a middle-aged man buried close by the stone [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105056", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s015: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105057", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s015: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-2900''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:157555", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-022: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/88/Longstones%2C_Geograph.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/a06ad29e/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:314888", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-010: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/a06ad29e/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/a06ad29e/Longstones, Geograph.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('a0b08567-cd34-467a-bd6d-428196261423'::uuid, 'antiquity', '5', '2', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:a0b08567-cd34-467a-bd6d-428196261423:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''2'' restored on Kuhikugu', '169f45e626221bf55334cc7137a6f273', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 5 from period_start None"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:37003", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0048:chunk-0001: description ''Kuhikugu is a pre-Columbian archaeological complex at the headwaters of the Xingu River in the Amazon rainforest of Brazil, discovered by anthropologist Michael Heckenberger in 1993 while working with Kuikuro indigenous guides. The complex encompasses around twenty towns and villages across 20,000 km2, supporting an estimated 30,000-50,000 people from roughly 1,500 to 400 years ago. Settlements featured circular plazas, defensive ditches, palisades, and roads 10-50 meters wide connecting communities. Inhabitants practiced manioc farming and fish farming using constructed dams and enriched terra preta soils.'' -> ''Kuhikugu is an archaeological site located in Brazil, at the headwaters of the Xingu River, in the Amazon rainforest [1]. Kuhikugu was first uncovered by anthropologist Michael Heckenberger, working alongside the local Kuikuro people, who are the likely descendants of the original inhabitants of Kuhikugu [1]. Kuhikugu was likely inhabited from a period of time around 1,500 years ago to a time as recently as 400 years ago, when the people living there were likely killed by diseases brought over by Europeans [1]. Large defensive ditches and palisades were built around some of the communities at Kuhikugu [1]. Many of the communities at Kuhikugu were linked, with roads which bridged some rivers along their paths, and with canoe canals running alongside some of the roads [1]. Sites X35 and X34 are significant communities connected by two of these roads [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105066", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s015: period_name ''500 - 1000 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105067", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s015: period_start ''500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:157562", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-022: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/a0b08567/hero.webp'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:251523", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s014: period_name None -> ''500 - 1000 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:251524", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s014: period_start None -> ''500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:350987", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s006: period_name ''500 - 1000 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:350988", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s006: period_start ''500'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
