    ('a3316d5c-ee94-49c8-a428-6355708a4d64'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:a3316d5c-ee94-49c8-a428-6355708a4d64:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Pitalkhora Buddhist Caves', 'bdf896bff39c1c333e3fcdafb2e2c952', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Cave Structures'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 19 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86478", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2143:chunk-0001: description ''The Pitalkhora Caves, located in the Satmala range of the Western Ghats in Maharashtra, India, comprise 14 rock-cut Buddhist cave monuments dating to the 3rd century BCE [1]. These caves represent one of the earliest examples of rock-cut architecture in India [1]. The site lies approximately 40 kilometers from Ellora and is accessed by descending a steep flight of concrete stairs past a waterfall [1].'' -> ''The Pitalkhora Caves, in the Satmala range of the Western Ghats of Maharashtra, India, are an ancient Buddhist site consisting of 14 rock-cut cave monuments which date back to the third century BCE, making them one of the earliest examples of rock-cut architecture in India [1]. The caves are cut in a variety of basalt rock, but some of the caves have crumbled and are damaged [1]. Out of the 14, four are chaityas (one housing votive stupas, one apsidal and single-cell) and the rest are viharas [1]. All the caves belong to the Early Buddhist schools period, but the reasonably well preserved paintings are of the Mahayana period [1]. The caves are in two groups, one of 10 caves and the second of four [1]. It is believed that Pitalkhora can be identified with Ptolemy’s \"Petrigala\" as well as the \"Pitangalya\" of Mahamayuri, a Buddhist chronicle [1]. The inscriptions date from c. 250 BCE to the 3rd and 4th centuries CE [1]. The Chaitya hall, Cave 3 of Pitalkhora, represents an important marker in the chronology of the Chaitya hall design in western India [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:157656", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-022: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/61/4_Pitalkhora_overview.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/a3316d5c/043_Reconstructed_Cells_(34045149876).webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:314943", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-010: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/a3316d5c/043_Reconstructed_Cells_(34045149876).webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/a3316d5c/4_Pitalkhora_overview.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
