 complex is approximately 3,700 m, which contains a central hall of 200 m length and the Neanderthal fossil site, called the Ossuary Gallery, which is 28 m long and 12 m wide [1]. In 1994, human remains were found accidentally in the cave [1]. They were initially suspected to be from the Spanish Civil War because Republican fighters used to hide there; however, later analysis shows that the remains actually belong to Neanderthals [1]. Researchers recovered more than 2500 hominin fossil elements from the site [1]. The minimum number of individuals from Sidrón Cave is 13 [1]. The age of these remains of three men, three adolescent boys, four women, and three infants has been estimated to about 49,000 years [1]. The first sequencing of the Neanderthal Y chromosome was successfully completed from a specimen from Sidrón Cave [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:121686", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s007: site_type ''Megalithic structures'' -> ''Cave''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:157722", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-022: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Cueva_El_Sidron_(02)_Arqueoloxicu.jpg/400px-Cueva_El_Sidron_(02)_Arqueoloxicu.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/a4ac2a6f/Cueva_El_Sidron_(02)_Arqueoloxicu.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:355942", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s002: site_type ''Cave'' -> ''Cave Structures''"}]'::jsonb),
