one-axe technology, ochre use, and interaction with megafauna such as Diprotodon [1].\" -> \"Warratyi is the site of a rock shelter in the Flinders Ranges in South Australia [1]. Located around 550 kilometres north of Adelaide and about 200 kilometres inland, it has been identified as the oldest known site of human habitation in inland Australia [1]. Researchers found thousands of artefacts and bone fragments, which enabled them to date the shelter''s occupation to a number of periods between 49,000 and 10,000 years ago [1]. The finds include the earliest evidence in Australia of the development of bone and stone-axe technology, the use of ochre, and interaction with megafauna such as Diprotodon [1]. The site is located in the ancestral lands of the Adnyamathanha, an Indigenous Australian people [1]. It lies in a gorge at the southern end of the Lake Eyre basin in the northern Flinders Ranges [1]. The artefacts included pieces of gypsum pigment, hafted and stone tools, and bone needles including the oldest bone tool so far found in Australia [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105132", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s015: period_name ''< 4500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105133", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s015: period_start ''-49000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:251563", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s014: period_name None -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:251564", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s014: period_start None -> ''-500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:351760", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s007: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:351761", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s007: period_start ''-500'' -> ''-47050''"}]'::j