    ('a694497b-30ab-4283-be76-d1570d7c90ad'::uuid, 'mystery', '3', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:a694497b-30ab-4283-be76-d1570d7c90ad:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Sesklo', '0f5e9fc3570f653ed70a16012dc8443e', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 3 from (''City/town/settlement'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 105 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43639", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0078:chunk-0001: description \"Sesklo is one of the largest prehistoric settlements in Europe, located on Kastraki Hill near Volos in Thessaly, Greece. Inhabited from the mid-7th to the 4th millennium BC, the settlement gives its name to the earliest known Neolithic culture of Europe. At its peak around 5000 BC it covered roughly 100 acres with 500-800 houses, featuring mudbrick dwellings with stone foundations organized around narrow alleyways and fortification walls. The site yielded distinctive painted pottery, obsidian blades sourced from the island of Milos, and clay figurines. Notable structures include the megaron, an impressive central building, and the Potter''s House, destroyed by fire.\" -> ''During the prehistory of Southeastern Europe, Sesklo was a significant settlement of Neolithic Greece, before the advent of the Bronze Age and millennia before the Mycenaean period [1]. The settlement at Sesklo gives its name to the earliest known Neolithic culture of Europe, which inhabited Thessaly and parts of Macedonia [1]. The Neolithic settlement was discovered in the 19th century and the first excavations were made by the Greek archaeologist Christos Tsountas [1]. The oldest fragments researched at Sesklo place development of the culture as far back as c. 7510 BC — c. 6190 BC, known as proto-Sesklo and pre-Sesklo [1]. They show advanced agriculture and a very early use of pottery that rivals in age those documented in the near east [1]. The Neolithic settlement of Sesklo covered an area of approximately 20 hectares during its peak period at c. 5000 BC and comprised about 500 to 800 houses with a population estimated potentially, to be as large as 5,000 people [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:158270", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-023: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3f/Sesklo_DSC_2020a.jpg'' -> \"/data/images/wiki/a694497b/Sesklo, ''Little Megaron''.webp\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:314991", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-010: thumbnail_url \"/data/images/wiki/a694497b/Sesklo, ''Little Megaron''.webp\" -> ''/data/images/wiki/a694497b/Sesklo_DSC_2020a.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
