th the Lacedaemonians against the Athenians [1]. In 424 B.C., in the course of the Peloponnesian War, the Spartan general Brasidas managed to persuade Acanthos, an ally of Athens on the Chalcidice peninsula, to defect from their alliance [1]. Then, the Acanthians sided with the Lacaedemonians and fought against Athens [1]. From the booty of the battles, including the battle of Lyncestis, they built a treasury in the sanctuary of Apollo at Delphi, commemorating the joint victory [1]. Plutarch in his \"Life of Lysander\" mentions the dedicatory inscription: Brasidas and the Acanthians from the Athenians [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:122808", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s008: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:122809", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s008: period_start ''-424'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:122810", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s008: site_type ''Megalithic structures'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:240802", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s007: period_name None -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:240803", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s007: period_start None -> ''-422''"}]'::jsonb),
