    ('a80e7611-46f8-4fd6-a74b-0b5e29defa6e'::uuid, 'mystery', '3', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:a80e7611-46f8-4fd6-a74b-0b5e29defa6e:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Archaeological Site of Atapuerca', '6d0a1c34d3dfb7a1e8c99d8e718a2bbf', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 3 from (''Cave Structures'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 113 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86582", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2146:chunk-0001: description ''UNESCO World Heritage Site (2000) in Burgos province, northern Spain, containing the richest fossil record of early humans in Europe. The Gran Dolina cave yielded over 160 bone fragments from at least eleven individuals dated 850,000-780,000 years ago, attributed to the species Homo antecessor. More than 30% of these bones bear cut marks indicating cannibalism, the oldest known evidence of this practice. The Sima de los Huesos pit produced thousands of Middle Pleistocene hominin fossils, making Atapuerca essential to understanding human evolution in western Europe.'' -> ''The archaeological site of Atapuerca is located in the Province of Burgos in the north of Spain and is notable for its evidence of early human occupation [1]. Among numerous faunal and floral fossils, a jaw fragment was found during the 1970s and a skull fragment in 1995, which both belong to Homo heidelbergensis [1]. They date to between 600,000 and 400,000 years BP [1]. Since 1997, the excavators have located more than 5,500 human skeletal remains deposited during the Middle Pleistocene period, at least 350,000 years old, which represent 28 individuals of Homo heidelbergensis [1]. Associated finds include Ursus deningeri fossils and a hand axe called Excalibur [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105182", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s015: period_name ''< 4500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105183", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s015: period_start ''-1400000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:158318", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-023: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/a80e7611/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/a80e7611/Atapuerca, excavación G.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:302175", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04a_fields-wd4-s001: period_name None -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:302176", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04a_fields-wd4-s001: period_start None -> ''-2580000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:315622", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-011: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/a80e7611/Atapuerca, excavación G.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/a80e7611/Dolina-Pano-3.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
