    ('a9a32ee2-bd62-4fe4-8515-0ccdda65a7ee'::uuid, 'cultural_influence', '8', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:a9a32ee2-bd62-4fe4-8515-0ccdda65a7ee:cultural_influence', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: cultural_influence ''7'' restored on Bouldnor Cliff', '7cddd6b5bc5d60ff61360d52be822ab4', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "cultural_influence = 8 from description yes, thumbnail yes, 5 content link(s), 1 image(s), source_url yes"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86622", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2147:chunk-0001: description ''Bouldnor Cliff is a submerged prehistoric settlement site in the Solent, England. Discovered in 1999 when a lobster was observed discarding worked flint tools from its burrow on the seabed, the site preserves organic materials that do not normally survive on dry land, making it important to the understanding of Mesolithic Britain [1]. BBC Radio 4\\''s Making History programme described it as \"probably Europe\\''s most important Mesolithic site\" albeit concealed under water [2].'' -> ''Bouldnor Cliff is a submerged prehistoric settlement site in the Solent [1]. The site dates from the Mesolithic era and is in approximately 11 metres of water just offshore of the village of Bouldnor on the Isle of Wight in the United Kingdom [1]. Several years of fieldwork have revealed that Bouldnor was a settlement site about 8,000 years ago, at a time when lower sea levels meant that the Solent was just a river valley [1]. As sea levels rose, the Solent eventually flooded and the settlement area was swamped [1]. The rising waters deposited silt and mud onto the original land surface, covering and preserving it [1]. Archaeologists have discovered large quantities of burnt flints, mounds of timbers and pits dug into the ground [1]. Wood from this era does not normally preserve well in land environments, so the quantity of wood found at Bouldnor makes the site of international importance [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105221", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s015: site_type ''Underwater structures'' -> ''Settlement''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:158396", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-023: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/a9a32ee2/hero.webp'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:323559", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-004-002: thumbnail_url None -> ''/data/images/wiki/a9a32ee2/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:325329", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-005-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/a9a32ee2/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/a9a32ee2/Bouldnor.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:355950", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s002: site_type ''Settlement'' -> ''City/town/settlement''"}]'::jsonb),
