    ('aaa12d23-d666-4a40-9f3c-17d861f4e143'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:aaa12d23-d666-4a40-9f3c-17d861f4e143:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Gobustan Rock Art', 'be2e17280d3d40517f305f0e55149b82', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Rock art'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 29 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:37227", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0050:chunk-0001: description ''Gobustan Rock Art features thousands of petroglyphs depicting prehistoric life, including hunting scenes with lance-bearers, ritual dances, animal figures, bullfighting, camel caravans, and celestial symbols like the sun and stars [1]. These engravings date from approximately 5,000 to 20,000 years ago, spanning the Upper Paleolithic through medieval periods [1]. The rock art provides insight into the spiritual life and daily activities of early human populations in the Caucasus region [1]. Recognized as a UNESCO World Heritage Site, it forms part of the Gobustan State Historical and Cultural Reserve [1].'' -> ''The Gobustan Rock Art Cultural Landscape is a historic site in Azerbaijan, comprising a large collection of petroglyphs that reflect the flora, fauna, hunting practices, social structures, and cultural expressions of human societies from prehistoric to medieval times [1]. These engravings provide detailed depictions of anthropomorphic figures, such as lance-bearers and ritualistic dance scenes, as well as various animal species, bullfighting events, camel caravans, and celestial symbols such as the sun and other stars [1]. The Gobustan Rock Art Cultural Landscape reflects the history of human development in Eurasia from the Upper Paleolithic era through the Middle Ages [1]. It is situated at the southeastern end of the Greater Caucasus ridge, approximately 70 km southwest of central Baku, and covers an area of 537 hectares [1]. The rock art was accidentally discovered by quarry workers in the 1930s [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:158418", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-023: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/aaa12d23/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/aaa12d23/Petroglyph_of_a_aurochs_(a_large_ox)_from_that_era_(37074580146).webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:334684", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-009-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/aaa12d23/Petroglyph_of_a_aurochs_(a_large_ox)_from_that_era_(37074580146).webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/aaa12d23/hero.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
