uction of the railroad from Ancon to Pasamayo, the first large ancient tombs were discovered here [1]. In 1875, German scholars Wilhelm Reiss and Alphons Stubel visited in the area, and made several excavations in and around the area of the necropolis [1]. In the Archaic period, sedentary fishermen and collectors of seafood and shellfish lived in the Bay of Ancon; this is evidenced by the presence of large middens, specifically in the area of Las Colinas [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105923", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s016: period_name ''< 4500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105924", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s016: period_start ''-8000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105925", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s016: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Necropolis''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:158479", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-023: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/ac28b46b/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/ac28b46b/Necrópolis de Ancón Vista 3.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:241665", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s008: period_name None -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:241666", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s008: period_start None -> ''-8000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:356152", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s003: site_type ''Necropolis'' -> ''Necropolis/tombs complex''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ac2c74ff-d861-4609-8589-67a8c3eceb3c'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:ac2c74ff-d861-4609-8589-67a8c3eceb3c:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''5'' restored on Ballynoe Stone Circle', 'b10c0a4a366199292fc4ba134ab5f12d', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Stone circle'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 57 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30804", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-uk-parts: country ''Ireland'' -> ''Northern Ireland''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86678", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2149:chunk-0001: description ''A large late Neolithic to Early Bronze Age stone circle near Ballynoe, County Down, Northern Ireland, dating to around 3000 BC. The circle measures 35 m in diameter and comprises over 50 upright stones up to 1.8 m tall. Excavations in 1937-38 by Professor Albert Giffen uncovered a partially kerbed long cairn 21 m long within the circle, containing burial cists with cremated remains of at least seven individuals including a young child. The site is believed to have served ceremonial, burial, and possibly astronomical purposes.'' -> ''Ballynoe Stone Circle is a complex multi-phase site, meaning its use spans multiple periods of human history [1]. It is believed to be some 5,000 years old dating from the late Neolithic (around 3000 BC) into the Early Bronze Age [1]. The Ballynoe stone circle is located near the village of Ballynoe, County Down, in Northern Ireland [1]. The circle measures around 35 m in diameter and includes 50 or more small, upright stones, with a maximum height of 1.8 m [1]. The excavation was conducted between 20 - 27 September 1937 and 7 - 16 June 1938 by Professor Albert Giffen, a Dutch archaeologist, and Miss Mary McMurray Gaffikin, an archaeology expert from Newcastle, County Down [1]. The excavation uncovered a partially curbed long cairn 21 metres in length by 9 metres wide within the stone circle [1]. There were cists at both the east and west ends with the west end later augmented with two more chambers [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105926", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s016: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105927", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s016: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-3000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:158480", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-023: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/ac2c74ff/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/ac2c74ff/4 Ballynoe Stone Circle 1.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:315687", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-011: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/ac2c74ff/4 Ballynoe Stone Circle 1.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/ac2c74ff/Ballynoe_(27),_September_2009.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
