    ('ae28d423-c02b-44be-b78d-7d65c99f3c3b'::uuid, 'rarity_score', '33', '30', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:ae28d423-c02b-44be-b78d-7d65c99f3c3b:rarity_score', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: rarity_score ''30'' restored on Oakley Down Barrow Cemetery', '540c5302257ed5bcb1a96aa71cde6319', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "rarity_score = 33 from mystery 5, cultural_influence 8"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:81184", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2023:chunk-0001: description ''Oakley Down Barrow Cemetery is one of the finest groups of Bronze Age round barrows on Cranborne Chase, located near Sixpenny Handley in Dorset, England. The scheduled monument comprises approximately 30 barrows of varied types including bowl, bell, and rare disc barrows, with most dating to 1400-1200 BC. Excavations revealed primary and secondary inhumations and cremations in urns, with grave goods including amber, glass and faience beads, and bronze daggers suggesting social differentiation. The site survived agricultural destruction through its protection as a royal hunting ground.'' -> \"Oakley Down Barrow Cemetery is a group of Bronze Age round barrows on Cranborne Chase, about 1 mile east of the village of Sixpenny Handley in Dorset, England [1]. The presence of Wor Barrow, a Neolithic long barrow nearby to the west, may have been influential in the construction of the Oakley Down cemetery [1]. There are about 30 barrows [1]. Many of the barrows were excavated in the early 19th century by Sir Richard Colt Hoare and William Cunnington; the barrows investigated can be identified from Hoare''s numbered plan [1]. The barrows contained primary and secondary inhumations, and primary and secondary cremations in urns and cists [1]. Grave goods included beads of amber, glass and faience, and bronze daggers [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105987", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s016: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105988", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s016: period_start ''-3000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:159089", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-024: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/96/Oakley_disc_barrow_panorama.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/ae28d423/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:251967", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s015: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:251968", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s015: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:345717", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-09a_fields-wd5-s001: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:345718", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-09a_fields-wd5-s001: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-2500''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ae2ca7b1-89da-46cb-8924-f9d04dd5da2e'::uuid, 'cultural_influence', '8', '10', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:ae2ca7b1-89da-46cb-8924-f9d04dd5da2e:cultural_influence', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: cultural_influence ''10'' restored on Banias', '58bcd9ae145da529728a01a2022e2ba8', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "cultural_influence = 8 from description yes, thumbnail yes, 2 content link(s), 20 image(s), source_url yes"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86740", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2151:chunk-0001: description ''Banias is an archaeological site in the Golan Heights near a natural spring associated with the Greek god Pan [1]. The spring is the source of the Banias River, one of the main tributaries of the Jordan River [1]. Archaeologists uncovered a shrine dedicated to Pan and related deities, alongside remains of an ancient city dating from the Hellenistic and Roman periods [1]. The site was inhabited for about 2,000 years until 1967, when its Syrian population fled during the Six-Day War [1].'' -> \"Banias, also spelled Banyas, is a site in the Israeli-occupied Golan Heights, Syria near a natural spring, once associated with the Greek god Pan [1]. The spring is the source of the Banias River, one of the main tributaries of the Jordan River [1]. The spring at Banias initially originated in a large cave carved out of a sheer cliff face which was gradually lined with a series of shrines [1]. The pre-Hellenistic deity associated with the spring of Banias was variously called Ba''al-gad or Ba''al Hermon [1]. Shams al-Mulk Isma''il attacked Banias and captured it on 11 December 1132 [1]. In late spring 1140, Mu''in ad-Din Unur handed Banias to the Crusaders during the reign of King Fulk, due to their assistance against Zengi''s aggression towards Damascus [1]. On 18 May 1157, Nūr ad-Din began a siege on Banias using mangonels, a type of siege engine [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
    ('ae2ca7b1-89da-46cb-8924-f9d04dd5da2e'::uuid, 'rarity_score', '21', '25', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:ae2ca7b1-89da-46cb-8924-f9d04dd5da2e:rarity_score', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: rarity_score ''25'' restored on Banias', '58bcd9ae145da529728a01a2022e2ba8', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "rarity_score = 21 from mystery 1, cultural_influence 8"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86740", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2151:chunk-0001: description ''Banias is an archaeological site in the Golan Heights near a natural spring associated with the Greek god Pan [1]. The spring is the source of the Banias River, one of the main tributaries of the Jordan River [1]. Archaeologists uncovered a shrine dedicated to Pan and related deities, alongside remains of an ancient city dating from the Hellenistic and Roman periods [1]. The site was inhabited for about 2,000 years until 1967, when its Syrian population fled during the Six-Day War [1].'' -> \"Banias, also spelled Banyas, is a site in the Israeli-occupied Golan Heights, Syria near a natural spring, once associated with the Greek god Pan [1]. The spring is the source of the Banias River, one of the main tributaries of the Jordan River [1]. The spring at Banias initially originated in a large cave carved out of a sheer cliff face which was gradually lined with a series of shrines [1]. The pre-Hellenistic deity associated with the spring of Banias was variously called Ba''al-gad or Ba''al Hermon [1]. Shams al-Mulk Isma''il attacked Banias and captured it on 11 December 1132 [1]. In late spring 1140, Mu''in ad-Din Unur handed Banias to the Crusaders during the reign of King Fulk, due to their assistance against Zengi''s aggression towards Damascus [1]. On 18 May 1157, Nūr ad-Din began a siege on Banias using mangonels, a type of siege engine [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
    ('ae2ca7b1-89da-46cb-8924-f9d04dd5da2e'::uuid, 'total_power', '19', '21', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:ae2ca7b1-89da-46cb-8924-f9d04dd5da2e:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: total_power ''21'' restored on Banias', '58bcd9ae145da529728a01a2022e2ba8', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 19 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 4 + 5 + 8 + 1 + 1"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86740", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2151:chunk-0001: description ''Banias is an archaeological site in the Golan Heights near a natural spring associated with the Greek god Pan [1]. The spring is the source of the Banias River, one of the main tributaries of the Jordan River [1]. Archaeologists uncovered a shrine dedicated to Pan and related deities, alongside remains of an ancient city dating from the Hellenistic and Roman periods [1]. The site was inhabited for about 2,000 years until 1967, when its Syrian population fled during the Six-Day War [1].'' -> \"Banias, also spelled Banyas, is a site in the Israeli-occupied Golan Heights, Syria near a natural spring, once associated with the Greek god Pan [1]. The spring is the source of the Banias River, one of the main tributaries of the Jordan River [1]. The spring at Banias initially originated in a large cave carved out of a sheer cliff face which was gradually lined with a series of shrines [1]. The pre-Hellenistic deity associated with the spring of Banias was variously called Ba''al-gad or Ba''al Hermon [1]. Shams al-Mulk Isma''il attacked Banias and captured it on 11 December 1132 [1]. In late spring 1140, Mu''in ad-Din Unur handed Banias to the Crusaders during the reign of King Fulk, due to their assistance against Zengi''s aggression towards Damascus [1]. On 18 May 1157, Nūr ad-Din began a siege on Banias using mangonels, a type of siege engine [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
    ('ae3b5deb-3b9c-42ec-8610-445c99f5b1a8'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:ae3b5deb-3b9c-42ec-8610-445c99f5b1a8:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''8'' restored on Vespasian''s Camp', '6a39fbf72bceba9324594dcad80493ab', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 6 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86742", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2151:chunk-0001: description \"Vespasian''s Camp is an Iron Age hillfort in Wiltshire, England, located just west of Amesbury and less than 3 km from Stonehenge [1]. Built on a hill beside the Stonehenge Avenue, the fortification overlooks the River Avon to the south while the A303 road runs along its northern edge [1]. The earthworks date to between 1500 and 500 BC and are a scheduled monument lying within the Stonehenge World Heritage Site [1][2]. The site demonstrates sophisticated defensive engineering with multiple ramparts.\" -> ''Vespasian\\''s Camp is an Iron Age hillfort just west of the town of Amesbury, Wiltshire [1]. The hillfort is less than 3 kilometres from the Neolithic and Bronze Age site of Stonehenge, and was built on a hill next to the Stonehenge Avenue; it has the River Avon on its southern side and the A303 road on its northern edge [1]. The site is a scheduled monument and lies within the boundaries of the Stonehenge World Heritage Site [1]. The earthworks were named \"Vespasian\\''s Camp\" in the 16th century by William Camden, an Elizabethan antiquarian and historian, during a tour of the area [1]. Although the Roman general Vespasian, who was later Roman emperor from 69 to 79 CE, campaigned through Wessex after the Roman invasion of Britain in 43 CE, there is no evidence to suggest he came to this hillfort or had any military base here [1]. Aligned from north to south, the hillfort is 730 m long and 374 m wide at its southern end, narrowing to 100 m wide at the north [1]. It encloses an area of some 14 ha [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105989", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s016: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105990", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s016: period_start ''-1500'' -> ''-6250''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:159091", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-024: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3f/Vespasian%27s_Camp.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/ae3b5deb/hero.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
