    ('af78d851-5a24-4bd6-a1ad-1df1f29f9526'::uuid, 'mystery', '8', '9', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:af78d851-5a24-4bd6-a1ad-1df1f29f9526:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''9'' restored on Cave di Cusa', '5b22f8f5e76e06c64b0bc48f9f5bb416', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 8 from (''Mine/quarry'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 3 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:81190", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2023:chunk-0001: description ''Cave di Cusa is an ancient stone quarry in Sicily, Italy, located 3 kilometers south of Campobello di Mazara in the province of Trapani [1]. The quarry extends 1.8 kilometers along an east-west ridge [1]. Extraction began in the first half of the 6th century BC, and its stone was transported to construct the temples of the ancient Greek city of Selinunte [1]. The quarry was abandoned in 409 BC when Carthaginian forces captured Selinunte [1]. Today, it functions as an official Sicilian Archaeological Zone and attracts tourists [1][2].'' -> ''Cave di Cusa or Rocche di Cusa was an ancient stone quarry in Sicily, Italy [1]. It is located 3 kilometers south of the town Campobello di Mazara in the province of Trapani, Italy [1]. It is 1.8 kilometer long and is on a ridge that spans from east to west [1]. This site was quarried beginning in the first half of the 6th century BC and its stone was used to construct the temples in the ancient Greek city Selinunte [1]. It was abandoned in 409 BC when the city was captured by the Carthaginians [1]. The rock is in different stages of being quarried, so it is evident that the abandonment of the site occurred rather quickly [1]. There is evidence of pick marks on the rocks from various stone tools [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:159135", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-024: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/af78d851/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/af78d851/Cave di Cusa 01.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
