 Carthaginians [1]. The rock is in different stages of being quarried, so it is evident that the abandonment of the site occurred rather quickly [1]. There is evidence of pick marks on the rocks from various stone tools [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:159135", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-024: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/af78d851/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/af78d851/Cave di Cusa 01.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('af919ba9-7fa1-4da0-bc15-443767ae8b66'::uuid, 'mystery', '3', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:af919ba9-7fa1-4da0-bc15-443767ae8b66:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Pair-non-Pair Cave', '818b5e9f22a667eaaf72cdea30cc3dd8', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 3 from (''Cave Structures'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 113 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:81210", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2024:chunk-0001: description ''The Pair-non-Pair Cave is located near the village of Prignac-et-Marcamps in the Gironde department of southwestern France. Discovered in 1881, the cave contains remarkable prehistoric parietal engravings. These petroglyphs rank among the oldest known examples of Paleolithic art, dating to approximately 30,000–25,000 BP during the Aurignacian period.[1]'' -> ''The Pair-non-Pair Cave is located near the village of Prignac-et-Marcamps [1]. Only discovered in 1881 it is known for remarkable prehistoric parietal engravings - petroglyphic representations of wild animals, \"which rank among the most ancient examples of art made by prehistoric\" humans, dating back to between 30,000 and 25,000 BP [1]. The third decorated cave ever to be discovered after Altamira in Spain and the Chabot cave in Ardèche it was the first cave ever to be classified and listed as a historical monument in France on December, 20th 1901 [1]. Excavations under the leadership of archaeologist François Daleau began immediately in March 1881 lasting until 1913 [1]. Apart from the petroglyphs, wall paintings, 15000 stone tool fragments and around 6000 animal bones of 60 species were unearthed and recorded [1]. Human occupation took place over a period spanning 60,000 years from the Mousterian, the Chatelperronian, the Aurignacian and into the Gravettian cultures [1]. Most animals carved on the walls of the cave are represented in pairs [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:122895", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s008: lat ''45.016780434339054'' -> ''45.038978''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:122896", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s008: lon ''-0.48329038780693007'' -> ''-0.50178''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:122897", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s008: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:122898", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s008: period_start ''-500'' -> ''-80000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:159136", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-024: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/18/Entr%C3%A9e_de_Pair-non-Pair.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/af919ba9/hero.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
