ve curved fortifications of andesite stone, circular towers exceeding 10 metres in height, and over 300 monumental structures serving ceremonial and administrative functions. Often called the ''Machu Picchu of the North,'' it was northern Peru''s most important political and military centre before Inca conquest in the 14th century.\" -> \"Marcahuamachuco is an archeological site of Pre-Incan ruins in the La Libertad Region of Peru [1]. Construction of Marcahuamachuco began around AD 400, during the Andean Middle Horizon period and continued until approximately 800 AD [1]. Before being conquered by the Incas in the 14th century, Marcahuamachuco was known as northern Peru''s most important political, economic and military center [1]. The first formal archaeological research in Marcahuamachuco was conducted during three months in 1900 by Max Uhle and Julio C. Tello under the auspices of the University of California, Berkeley [1]. Marcahuamachuco is set atop the nexus of three mountain valleys at an altitude of more than 3,200 meters [1]. Encompassing more than three kilometres of land, the site is celebrated for its massive castillos and unique circular double-walled archaeological structures [1]. The domestic residences are multi-storied galleries which originally housed numerous individual families [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:106056", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s016: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:159204", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-024: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/b1a2bdd2/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/b1a2bdd2/Marcahuamachuco - La Portada Oeste.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:241736", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s008: site_type None -> ''Archaeological site''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:315794", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2