    ('b38b0f66-79cb-40db-9881-215e01196d2b'::uuid, 'cultural_influence', '10', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:b38b0f66-79cb-40db-9881-215e01196d2b:cultural_influence', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: cultural_influence ''8'' restored on Hengistbury Head', 'bbc4d2e00e3306a736b486942fbb3a4e', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "cultural_influence = 10 from description yes, thumbnail yes, 5 content link(s), 20 image(s), source_url yes"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:84259", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26a_fields-wd1-s001: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:84260", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26a_fields-wd1-s001: period_start ''-3000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86904", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2155:chunk-0001: description ''Hengistbury Head is a Bronze Age settlement site (c. 3000-1500 BC) on a headland jutting into the English Channel between Bournemouth and Mudeford in Dorset. It is a site of international archaeological importance and is scheduled as an Ancient Monument [1]. Declared a Local Nature Reserve in 1990, the headland and surrounding area form part of the Christchurch Harbour Site of Special Scientific Interest [1]. The site has also been designated a Special Area of Conservation and Special Protection Area [1]. Formerly called Christchurch Head, the elevated portion is known as Warren Hill.'' -> ''Hengistbury Head, formerly also called Christchurch Head, is a headland jutting into the English Channel between Bournemouth and Mudeford in the English county of Dorset [1]. It is a site of international importance in terms of its archaeology and is scheduled as an Ancient Monument [1]. Several archaeological digs have revealed that the site was occupied during the Upper Palaeolithic [1]. There is evidence of an open settlement of the Creswellian culture on the hill in the middle of the headland dating to around 14,100 years ago [1]. In Iron Age Britain around 700 BC, a settlement on the Head was established; also around this time, the headland was cut off from the mainland by the construction of two banks and ditches called the Double Dykes [1]. The advanced level of metallurgy in the area, coupled with its ease of access from the Continent, meant that Hengistbury Head became a significant Late-Iron Age port; trading worked metal of iron, silver, and bronze in return for figs, glass, tools and other goods [1]. Armorican coins and pottery uncovered here show links to the Brittany peninsula [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:159316", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-024: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/b38b0f66/hero.webp'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:241756", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s008: period_name None -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:241757", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s008: period_start None -> ''-10000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:315834", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-011: thumbnail_url None -> ''/data/images/wiki/b38b0f66/Hengistbury_Head.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
