    ('b44da255-c03d-4304-b7c8-f9e7e8093720'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:b44da255-c03d-4304-b7c8-f9e7e8093720:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''7'' restored on Klekkende Høj', 'c368900d6645c1616c1a5b398a215907', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Tomb'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 12 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29423", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0286:chunk-0001: site_type ''Necropolis/tombs complex'' -> ''Tomb''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86932", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2156:chunk-0001: description ''Klekkende Hoj is a Neolithic passage grave on the island of Mon in Denmark, dating to approximately 3300-3200 BCE. It is the only double-passage tomb found on Mon, featuring two burial chambers side by side with separate entrances, each reached by passages about 7 metres long. The tomb was excavated in 1797 by Antoine de Bosc de la Calmette, governor of the island. Fifteen men worked for a week to uncover human remains, flint weapons, clay vessels, and amber jewellery. It is one of the best preserved of over 100 burial mounds on the island.'' -> ''Klekkende Høj is a megalithic tomb on the island of Møn in Denmark [1]. The tomb is believed to date from the Neolithic Age, ca. 3500-2800 BC, and is one of the best preserved of more than 100 burial mounds on the island [1]. The tomb is a passage grave, which means that the central chamber within the mound is reached by a connecting passage [1]. Klekkende Høj is unusual in that there are two entrance passages running approximately parallel to each other, facing east [1]. Within the mound is a central space running approximately north-south, which is divided through the centre by two large stones [1]. The tomb was excavated in 1797 by Antoine de Bosc de la Calmette, who was governor of the island [1]. Inside were a considerable number of human remains, flint weapons, clay vessels and amber jewellery [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:159945", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-025: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/b44da255/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/b44da255/Klekkende_Høj_south_passage.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
