iest evidence is a stone hand axe found near Gills Lap, dated to approximately 50,000 years ago [1] [2]. The site features Bronze Age round barrows, Iron Age enclosures, prehistoric field systems, and Roman iron workings [2]. Medieval and post-medieval pillow mounds for rabbit breeding survive across the forest [1]. Notably, military kitchen mounds between Camp Hill and Nutley from 1793 are among only a handful surviving in the United Kingdom [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:106138", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s016: period_name ''< 4500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:106139", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s016: period_start ''-9000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:106140", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s016: site_type ''Barrow'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:241778", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s008: site_type None -> ''Archaeological site''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:252379", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s016: period_name None -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:252380", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s016: period_start None -> ''-4501''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:351874", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s007: period_name ''< 4500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:351875", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s007: period_start ''-4501'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('b4d3bc67-e38d-4d6e-9b03-0ca72a0d4398'::uuid, 'antiquity', '5', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:b4d3bc67-e38d-4d6e-9b03-0ca72a0d4398:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''8'' restored on Boringdon Camp', 'f4c32a1cf243bd1d0dc95ff3cebc6248', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 5 from period_start -800"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86934", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2156:chunk-0001: description ''Boringdon Camp is an Iron Age and Roman earthwork in Cann Woods, near Plympton, Plymouth, Devon [1]. The site is a scheduled ancient monument owned by South Hams District Council [2]. The site was closed to visitors on 1 April 2016 due to erosion from foot traffic and conflicts with dogs not kept on leads [3].'' -> ''Boringdon Camp is an English Iron Age and Roman earthwork in Cann Woods, near Plympton, Plymouth, Devon [1]. The site is on a hilltop at 140 metres above sea level, with views down the Plym Valley of Plymouth Sound, 11 kilometres away [1]. The fort is constructed around an earlier ring ditch, and covers an area of around 2 hectares [1]. The remains consist of a sub-circular enclosure surrounded by an 8-metre rampart and a similarly sized outer ditch [1]. This ditch can still be seen in places, though tree planting has covered some of the defences around the main entrance [1]. The site was listed as a scheduled ancient monument in 1939 [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:106144", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s016: period_name ''1500 - 5