ical site 20 kilometres north of Cancuen in Petén Department, northern Guatemala [1]. The main feature of the site is a group of three Maya stelae and an altar [1]. Tres Islas is one of only a few Maya cities that erected dated monuments in the Early Classic period (c. AD 250 - 600) [1]. The early presence of the Emblem glyph that was later associated with the Late Classic Cancuen-Machaquila kingdom indicates that Tres Islas was the Early Classic capital of the kingdom [1]. Preliminary investigations in the area of Tres Islas and Cancuen revealed evidence of inhabitation from the Late Preclassic period (c.400 BC - 250 AD) through to the Late Classic (c.600-900 AD) [1]. The arrangement of the stelae and altar precisely mimics the orientation and relationships of the E-Group astronomical complex at Uaxactun, suggesting that the monument group served a similar purpose [1]. The siting of the stela-altar complex on the edge of an east-facing scarp offers a perfect location for observing the sunrise [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:106848", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s017: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:106849", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s017: period_start ''-500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:106850", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s017: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:241794", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s008: period_name None -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:241795", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s008: period_start None -> ''-400''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:241796", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s008: site_type None -> ''City''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:356171", "url": "remediati