    ('b774f9cc-ea01-4658-b12c-c66add4453d1'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:b774f9cc-ea01-4658-b12c-c66add4453d1:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on White Barrow', 'c54285beddbb343de63d58c171ac6a86', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Barrow'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 33 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86996", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2158:chunk-0001: description ''White Barrow is a Neolithic long barrow on Copehill Down, Salisbury Plain, south of Tilshead in Wiltshire, dating to approximately 4000-3500 BC. It measures about 77.5m long and 47m wide and was the first ancient monument purchased by the National Trust in 1909, acquired for 60 pounds to protect it from War Office land purchases. A 1997 geophysical survey by English Heritage revealed a possible burial chamber or mortuary cairn inside, along with post pits at its eastern end. In 1998, badgers that had burrowed into the barrow were relocated and the monument was covered with chain-link fencing for protection.'' -> ''White Barrow is a large Neolithic long barrow just below the crest of Copehill Down on Salisbury Plain, just south of the village of Tilshead in Wiltshire [1]. It is a scheduled monument, and the first ancient monument to be purchased by the National Trust [1]. White Barrow is 77.5 m long and approximately 47 m wide (including the surrounding ditch) [1]. It has never been fully excavated, but dating of materials found in and around it suggests that it dates from 3500 to 4000 BC, making it contemporary with other long barrows on Salisbury Plain [1]. A geophysical survey by English Heritage in 1997 found evidence of a possible burial chamber or mortuary cairn inside the barrow, and an arc of post pits at its eastern end [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:160010", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-025: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d3/Whitebarrow.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/b774f9cc/hero.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
