ed by R. P. Ross Williamson and E. Cecil Curwen in 1929 in response to a plan to lay out football pitches on the site [1]. In 2011, the Gathering Time project published an analysis of radiocarbon dates from almost forty British causewayed enclosures, including several from Whitehawk Camp [1]. The conclusion was that the Neolithic part of the site was probably constructed between 3650 and 3500 BC, and probably went out of use some time between 3500 and 3400 BC [1]. Most of the prehistoric pottery found at Whitehawk Camp is of the plain bowl or decorated bowl types; these are the earliest forms of Neolithic pottery found in England [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:160784", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-026: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cb/Signboard_at_Whitehawk_Neolithic_Camp%2C_Whitehawk_Hill%2C_Brighton_%28November_2020%29_%2810%29.JPG'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/bfa3495a/Whitehawk_camp_aerial_view_1930_Williamson.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:334738", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-009-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/bfa3495a/Whitehawk_camp_aerial_view_1930_Williamson.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/bfa3495a/Signboard_at_Whitehawk_Neolithic_Camp,_Whitehawk_Hill,_Brighton_(November_2020)_(10).webp''"}]'::jsonb),
