    ('c2984c15-07c8-4109-ad99-0ecc2e2e2d82'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:c2984c15-07c8-4109-ad99-0ecc2e2e2d82:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''5'' restored on The Hurlers Stone Circles', 'b2915d812480841f6afe87dbec93a7ba', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Stone circle'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 57 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:87266", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2166:chunk-0001: description ''A unique group of three Late Neolithic or Early Bronze Age stone circles near Minions on Bodmin Moor, Cornwall. This close grouping of three regular circles is unparalleled in England. The central circle (41.8 m diameter) is the best preserved with 14 stones, the northern circle (34.7 m) has 15 stones, and the southern circle (32.9 m) retains nine. Excavations in the 1930s revealed that all stones had been deliberately hammered smooth, with chippings deposited nearby. A granite paving strip once connected the northern and central circles. Legend holds the stones are men petrified for playing hurling on a Sunday.'' -> ''The Hurlers (Cornish: An Hurlysi) is a group of three stone circles in the civil parish of St Cleer, Cornwall, England [1]. The site is half-a-mile (0.8 km) west of the village of Minions on the eastern flank of Bodmin Moor, and approximately four miles (6 km) north of Liskeard [1]. Just to the west of the circles are two standing stones known as the Pipers [1]. The Hurlers comprises three stone circles that lie on a line from SSW to NNE, and have diameters of 35 metres, 42 metres and 33 metres [1]. The middle circle, the largest is slightly elliptical [1]. In the middle circle 14 stones survive out of 28 [1]. The stones show clear traces of being hammered smooth [1]. Ralegh Radford excavated the site in the 1930s, and partly restored the two northern circles by re-erecting some stones and placing marker stones in the positions of those missing [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:107936", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s018: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:107937", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s018: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:161383", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-027: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8c/Hurlers2.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/c2984c15/Hurlers 01.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:242743", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s009: period_name None -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:242744", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s009: period_start None -> ''-2500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:316695", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-012: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/c2984c15/Hurlers 01.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/c2984c15/Hurlers2.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
