r Witham in the vicinity of Washingborough and Fiskerton in Lincolnshire, England in 1826 [1]. The shield is now in the British Museum [1]. The bronze facings show evidence of having been reworked [1]. Originally a leather silhouette of a long-legged wild boar would have been riveted to the shield around the central dome, as indicated by small rivet holes and staining of the shield [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:124172", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s009: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:161484", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-027: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6f/Witham_Shield_cutout.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/c5c5f2da/hero.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c5d05e95-f6a3-454f-85ba-6ebb4736b7bb'::uuid, 'cultural_influence', '8', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:c5d05e95-f6a3-454f-85ba-6ebb4736b7bb:cultural_influence', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: cultural_influence ''6'' restored on Arch of Augustus', '50744ad24461ca1da2c8e4c866a996fa', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "cultural_influence = 8 from description yes, thumbnail yes, 4 content link(s), 1 image(s), source_url yes"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:37497", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0057:chunk-0001: description ''The Arch of Augustus (Arco di Augusto) is a Roman triumphal arch built in 27 BC in Rimini, Emilia-Romagna, Italy [1]. The arch is one of the oldest surviving Roman arches [1].'' -> ''The Arch of Augustus is a monument in the city of Aosta, northern Italy [1]. It was erected in 25 BC on the occasion of the Roman victory over the Salassi and was the work of Aulus Terentius Varro Murena [1]. It is located at the end of the decumanus maximus [1]. Constructed from conglomerate, the arch has a single vault, with a height to the keystone of 11.4 metres [1]. Its span is a barrel vault, constituting an extension in width of a round arch [1]. In the monument, various styles can be recognised: The ten engaged columns which decorate its facade and its sides culminate in Corinthian capitals, while the entablature, adorned with metopes and triglyphs, is of the Doric order [1]. During the 12th century, the arch contained the home of a local noble family and in 1318 a small fortification was built inside it, designed for a corps of crossbowmen [1]. In 1716, because of the numerous leaks that were compromising the integrity of the monument, the attic that previously crowned the arch was replaced with a slate roof [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:342329", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "insert-2026-10-07-007-001: thumbnail_url None -> ''/data/images/wiki/c5d05e95/Arco di augusto di sera.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
