    ('c8d2c13e-fd9a-466c-9fdc-fc26ee798ded'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:c8d2c13e-fd9a-466c-9fdc-fc26ee798ded:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''7'' restored on Baltic Sea Anomaly', '072ca9a87590028cf338e79869d7476f', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Natural feature'', ''Undated'') is shared by 11 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:91892", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2212:chunk-0001: description ''Discovered in June 2011 by Swedish treasure hunters Peter Lindberg and Dennis Asberg of the OceanX team, the Baltic Sea anomaly is an underwater feature located on the seabed at the center of the Gulf of Bothnia in the northern Baltic Sea [1]. While treasure hunting, the team captured an indistinct sonar image of an object with unusual features [1]. The sonar reading prompted speculation that the object might be a UFO, but subsequent analysis determined the feature to be a glacial deposit [1][2].'' -> ''The Baltic Sea anomaly is a feature visible on an indistinct sonar image taken by Peter Lindberg, Dennis Åsberg and their Swedish OceanX diving team while treasure hunting on the floor of the northern Baltic Sea at the center of the Gulf of Bothnia in June 2011 [1]. A consensus of experts and scientists say that the image most likely shows a natural geological formation [1]. The group revisited the site the following year to get more detailed sonar scans [1]. Samples of stone recovered at the site by OceanX were given to Volker Brüchert, an associate professor of geology at Stockholm University [1]. The single sonar image provided by OceanX has drawn criticism from a number of sources [1]. Hanumant Singh of the Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution has said that it cannot be trusted because several distortions render it \"virtually useless for identifying an undersea formation\" [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:124221", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s009: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:124222", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s009: period_start ''1'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:124223", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s009: site_type ''Underwater structures'' -> ''Natural feature''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:304139", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04e_period-label-undated: period_name None -> ''Undated''"}]'::jsonb),
