    ('ca28f10c-b6b6-41bb-bf64-980908425ab7'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:ca28f10c-b6b6-41bb-bf64-980908425ab7:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''7'' restored on Chabukauri Basilica', 'f96a665abc50a518848e93f6ea42bf36', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Church/cathedral'', ''1 - 500 AD'') is shared by 12 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:27272", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-21_mechanical-country: country ''Georgia (country)'' -> ''Georgia''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:45249", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0107:chunk-0001: description \"Chabukauri Basilica is an early Christian three-aisle basilica in eastern Georgia''s Kakheti region, situated within the historical settlement of Nekresi approximately 1.5 km northwest of the Nekresi monastic complex [1]. Dated to the 4th to 5th century AD, it ranks among the earliest Christian church constructions in Georgia [1]. The church was excavated in 1998 and remains in a ruinous condition today [1]. It is inscribed on the list of Immovable Cultural Monuments of National Significance of Georgia [1].\" -> ''Chabukauri basilica is an early Christian church—now in a ruinous state—in the eastern Georgian region of Kakheti, in the territory of the historical settlement of Nekresi, about 1.5 km northwest of the Nekresi monastic complex [1]. It is a large three-aisle basilica, dated to the 4th to 5th century, making it one of the earliest Christian church buildings in Georgia [1]. The Chabukauri basilica was unearthed in an eponymous plot in 1998 and was dated to the 4th or 5th century [1]. Nodar Bakhtadze, who excavated Chabukauri, identifies the basilica with a church known from the early medieval Georgian chronicles to have been founded at Nekresi by King Trdat of Kartli (r.\\u2009c. 394 – 406) [1]. Chabukauri is a three-nave basilica, measuring 33.4 x 15 m [1]. It was built of large limestone blocks, sledged stones, and gravel [1]. The naos is divided into three naves by five pairs of rectangular-shaped pillars [1]. The building had three entrances, on the south, west, and north [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:317466", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-013: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/ca28f10c/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/ca28f10c/Chabukauri_basilica._Ruined_columns.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
