    ('cbc9996e-862d-44dd-b6be-c2967bc3f58c'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:cbc9996e-862d-44dd-b6be-c2967bc3f58c:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''7'' restored on Cathedral of Saint Domnius', '6ce4b1b418a452e9460d206c49ff4c34', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Church/cathedral'', ''1 - 500 AD'') is shared by 12 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:88834", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2507:chunk-0001: description \"Catholic cathedral in Split, Croatia, originally built c. 305 AD as the mausoleum of Roman Emperor Diocletian within his palace complex. The octagonal structure stands 24 metres high with walls 2.75 metres thick, reinforced with 28 granite and marble columns brought from Greek and Egyptian temples. In the 7th century, after refugees from destroyed Salona settled in the abandoned palace, Diocletian''s sarcophagus and pagan statues were removed and the building was converted into a Christian cathedral by Bishop John of Ravenna. It retains its original Roman brick dome. Regarded as the oldest Catholic cathedral in the world still in use in its original structure. A Romanesque bell tower was added beginning in the 13th century.\" -> \"The Cathedral of Saint Domnius, known locally as the Sveti Dujam or colloquially Sveti Duje, is the Catholic cathedral in Split, Croatia [1]. The Cathedral of St. Domnius is a complex of a church, formed from an Imperial Roman mausoleum, with a bell tower; strictly the church is dedicated to the Virgin Mary [1]. When the Avars and Slavs destroyed Salona in the 7th century, part of the population fled to the nearby islands [1]. After some time, a portion of these refugees returned to the mainland and settled within the abandoned imperial palace of Diocletian''s Palace [1]. They converted the emperor’s mausoleum into a Christian church [1]. The interior of the church is circular in plan, covered by a dome and articulated by square and semicircular niches that once housed statues of gods and emperors [1]. Of particular interest are two ribbon-framed medallions, which archaeologists and local tradition in Split identify as portraits of Emperor Diocletian and his wife Prisca [1]. One of the cathedral’s most celebrated features is its wooden doors, carved in walnut in 1214 by the Split native Andrija Buvina [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:162193", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-028: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/cbc9996e/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/cbc9996e/Cathedral_of_Saint_Domnius_-_Bell_tower_02.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
