rs and Slavs destroyed Salona in the 7th century, part of the population fled to the nearby islands [1]. After some time, a portion of these refugees returned to the mainland and settled within the abandoned imperial palace of Diocletian''s Palace [1]. They converted the emperor’s mausoleum into a Christian church [1]. The interior of the church is circular in plan, covered by a dome and articulated by square and semicircular niches that once housed statues of gods and emperors [1]. Of particular interest are two ribbon-framed medallions, which archaeologists and local tradition in Split identify as portraits of Emperor Diocletian and his wife Prisca [1]. One of the cathedral’s most celebrated features is its wooden doors, carved in walnut in 1214 by the Split native Andrija Buvina [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:162193", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-028: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/cbc9996e/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/cbc9996e/Cathedral_of_Saint_Domnius_-_Bell_tower_02.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
