 is dedicated to the Virgin Mary [1]. When the Avars and Slavs destroyed Salona in the 7th century, part of the population fled to the nearby islands [1]. After some time, a portion of these refugees returned to the mainland and settled within the abandoned imperial palace of Diocletian''s Palace [1]. They converted the emperor’s mausoleum into a Christian church [1]. The interior of the church is circular in plan, covered by a dome and articulated by square and semicircular niches that once housed statues of gods and emperors [1]. Of particular interest are two ribbon-framed medallions, which archaeologists and local tradition in Split identify as portraits of Emperor Diocletian and his wife Prisca [1]. One of the cathedral’s most celebrated features is its wooden doors, carved in walnut in 1214 by the Split native Andrija Buvina [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:162193", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-028: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/cbc9996e/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/cbc9996e/Cathedral_of_Saint_Domnius_-_Bell_tower_02.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cbfd3d2a-70f5-41f4-a3db-298b56040fea'::uuid, 'cultural_influence', '8', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:cbfd3d2a-70f5-41f4-a3db-298b56040fea:cultural_influence', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: cultural_influence ''6'' restored on Vindomora', 'f5cefdcbe856128cc650e6ed8c77fa87', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "cultural_influence = 8 from description yes, thumbnail yes, 3 content link(s), 2 image(s), source_url yes"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:87496", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2173:chunk-0001: description ''Vindomora was a Roman auxiliary fort on Dere Street in the province of Lower Britain. Its ruins, known as Ebchester Roman Fort, lie at Ebchester in County Durham, 12 miles (19 km) west-south-west of Newcastle upon Tyne [1]. The fort was strategically positioned between Corstopitum (Corbridge) to the north-west and Longovicium (Lanchester) to the south on this major Roman road [1].'' -> ''Vindomora was an auxiliary fort on Dere Street, in the province of Lower Britain (Britannia Inferior) [1]. Its ruins, now known as Ebchester Roman Fort, are situated at Ebchester in the English county of Durham [1]. Vindomora is situated in between the forts of Corstopitum (Corbridge) and Bywell to the north/west north, and Longovicium (Lanchester) to the south [1]. Legionary stones recovered identify a Fifth Cohort as building the fort, but unfortunately give no clue as to the parent legion [1]. The stones also identify the names of Centurions responsible for the construction work, and later restoration work done by auxiliary regiments at the time of the Scottish campaigns by emperor Severus [1]. The first unit proven to garrison Ebchester is the Cohors Quartae Breucorum Antoninianae, identified from an early 3rd century altar [1]. They were a nominally 500-strong infantry regiment, although usually the number was around 460 or lower [1]. This Auxilia unit was originally levied from amongst the Breuci tribe of Pannonia Inferior [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:108775", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s019: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:108776", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s019: period_start ''1'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:162201", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-028: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/B6309_Staggered_Crossroads_at_Ebchester_-_geograph.org.uk_-_2187419.jpg/400px-B6309_Staggered_Crossroads_at_Ebchester_-_geograph.org.uk_-_2187419.jpg'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:242836", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s009: period_name None -> ''1 - 500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:242837", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s009: period_start None -> ''69''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:342389", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "insert-2026-10-07-007-001: thumbnail_url None -> ''/data/images/wiki/cbfd3d2a/Befundskizze Kastell Ebchester.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
