    ('cdd84f55-f2c9-41a9-8f15-72e6e3f4e69d'::uuid, 'cultural_influence', '8', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:cdd84f55-f2c9-41a9-8f15-72e6e3f4e69d:cultural_influence', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: cultural_influence ''7'' restored on Land of Tema', 'ffcc4a3bbefd8c9414afbaf543a0e323', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "cultural_influence = 8 from description yes, thumbnail yes, 5 content link(s), 1 image(s), source_url yes"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:87546", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2174:chunk-0001: description \"The Land of Tema (Te''-ma, Tema or Thaiman) is a biblical location where the descendants of Ishmael''s son Tema dwelt. It has been identified with modern Teima'', an oasis in northern Saudi Arabia lying about 320 km (200 mi) north of Medina and 64 km (40 mi) south of Dumat (Dumah), now known as el-Jauf[1]. The settlement sat on an ancient caravan road connecting the Persian Gulf with the Gulf of Aqaba, facilitating trade wealth[1]. Archaeologist Charles Doughty documented ruins of a city wall 4.8 km (3 mi) in circuit[1]. A recently discovered 6th-century BC Aramaic stele demonstrates Assyrian cultural influence[1]. References in Job 6:19 and cuneiform inscriptions confirm its trade route significance, and it is often associated with the Land of Dumah[1].\" -> \"The Land of Tema, Te''-ma, Tema or Thaiman is a place mentioned in the Bible where the descendants of Ishmael''s son Tema dwelt [1]. It was most likely in northern Saudi Arabia and has been identified with the modern Teima'', an oasis which lies about 200 miles north of Medina, and some 40 miles south of Dumat, or Duma [1]. Teima'' is on the ancient caravan road connecting the Persian Gulf with the Gulf of Aqaba which in antiquity brought wealth to the town [1]. Doughty has described at Teima the ruins of an old city wall 3 miles in circuit [1]. An Aramaic stele recently discovered, belonging to the 6th century BC, shows the influence of Assyrian in the town [1]. Nabunaid of Babylon captured the city and settled there until Cyrus the Great captured the city and moved him to Carmania [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:108805", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s019: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:108806", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s019: period_start ''-1500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:162289", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-028: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/cdd84f55/hero.webp'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:243664", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s010: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:243665", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s010: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:334789", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-009-002: thumbnail_url None -> ''/data/images/wiki/cdd84f55/hero.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
