    ('ced822e8-13cd-49db-9011-808dc23aac45'::uuid, 'mystery', '3', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:ced822e8-13cd-49db-9011-808dc23aac45:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Brauron', 'ffb19fde9f155e7c600965da2ba620ca', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 3 from (''City/town/settlement'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 105 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:87582", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2175:chunk-0001: description ''(5th-3rd c. BC) Brauron was one of the twelve cities of ancient Attica, located on the eastern coast between Steiria and Halae Araphenides near the Erasinus River [1]. The site is renowned for its sanctuary of Artemis Brauronia, in whose honour a festival was celebrated [1]. Archaeological remains include a temple, a stoa, and a theatre [1]. A duplicate sanctuary existed on the Acropolis, serving as the city branch of the cult [2]. The settlement continued functioning into later times [1].'' -> ''Brauron was one of the twelve cities of ancient Attica, but never mentioned as a deme, though it continued to exist down to the latest times [1]. Brauron is celebrated on account of the worship of Artemis Brauronia [1]. The sanctuary of Artemis at Brauron is an early sacred site on the eastern coast of Attica near the Aegean Sea in a small inlet [1]. A nearby hill, c. 24 m high and 220 m to the southeast, was inhabited during the Neolithic era, c. 2000 BCE, and flourished particularly from Middle Helladic to early Mycenaean times as a fortified site (acropolis) [1]. Occupation ceased in the LHIIIb period, and the acropolis was never significantly resettled after this time [1]. This gap in the occupation of the site lasted from LHIIIb until the 8th century BCE [1]. An epigram in the Anthologia Graeca concerns the offerings of childish playthings a nubile young girl dedicates to Artemis on the eve of marriage; many such tokens have been recovered from the spring at Brauron [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:108840", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s019: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:108841", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s019: period_start ''-500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:243682", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s010: period_name None -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:243683", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s010: period_start None -> ''-5000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:317567", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-013: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/ced822e8/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/ced822e8/Brauron-2.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
