    ('cfaf38c0-0365-4841-a42a-1cfc5c45c42d'::uuid, 'mystery', '8', '9', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:cfaf38c0-0365-4841-a42a-1cfc5c45c42d:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''9'' restored on Archaeological site Puma Punku', 'd5fa2bd23ab69b951138037cca51b1ea', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 8 from (''Temple'', ''500 - 1000 AD'') is shared by 3 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:81334", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2028:chunk-0001: description ''Pumapunku is a 6th-century T-shaped man-made terraced platform mound with a sunken court and monumental structure, located near Tiwanaku in the Andean highlands [1]. Part of the Tiwanaku archaeological complex — a UNESCO World Heritage Site — the site belongs to the Tiwanaku civilization that flourished between c. 200-1000 CE at an altitude of 3,850 metres in the Titicaca Basin [1][2].'' -> \"Pumapunku or Puma Punku is a 6th-century T-shaped and strategically aligned man-made terraced platform mound with a sunken court and monumental structure on top, near Tiwanaku, La Paz [1]. It is part of the Pumapunku complex, at the Tiwanaku Site, an ancient archeological complex in the Andes of western Bolivia that has been designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site [1]. The Pumapunku complex is a collection of plazas and ramps centered on the Pumapunku platform mound [1]. Construction of Puma Punku is believed to have begun after AD 536 [1]. The fragments of five andesite gateways with similar characteristics to the Gateway of the Sun were found [1]. The largest of Pumapunku''s stone blocks is 7.81 metres long, 5.17 metres wide, averages 1.07 metres thick, and is estimated to weigh about 131 tonnes [1]. Notable features at Pumapunku are I-shaped architectural cramps, composed of a unique copper-arsenic-nickel bronze alloy [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:124338", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s009: site_type ''Megalithic stones'' -> ''Temple''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:162901", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-029: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/cfaf38c0/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/cfaf38c0/Puma_Punku14.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
