 H. Hartshorne visited the site in 1841, commenting that three stones then remained, all leaning [1]. He referred to these as being \"mutilated fragments\" of the ring\\''s original appearance [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:108850", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s019: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:108851", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s019: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:253615", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s019: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:253616", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s019: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:352839", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s008: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:352840", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s008: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('d013bd60-0008-4319-85ff-1523f13edf5b'::uuid, 'antiquity', '5', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:d013bd60-0008-4319-85ff-1523f13edf5b:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''8'' restored on Trink Hill', '17b14d05db8e364da55a038db71f7bb8', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 5 from period_start None"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:87614", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2176:chunk-0001: description \"Trink Hill is a 212-meter-high hill between the hamlets of Trink and Cripplesease near Nancledra, Cornwall, England. A Bronze Age round barrow sits at its summit, while on the western slope stands the Giew Mine menhir (or Trink Hill menhir), a 2-meter-tall standing stone near a spring. The stone sits on a westward alignment from the summit barrow with direct sightlines to Trencrom Hill Fort and St. Michael''s Mount. Twelve O''Clock Rock, a granite outcrop on the hill, is reputed to be a logan stone that can only be rocked at midnight.\" -> ''Trink Hill is a 212-metre-high hill that lies between the hamlets of Trink and Cripplesease, near to the village of Nancledra [1]. A Round barrow exists at the summit, an OS Trig point within it [1]. A stone named after the nearby Giew Mine (or Trink Hill menhir) stands on the western slope of the hill [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:124352", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s009: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:124353", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09