tion of Sumer in the mid-4th millennium BC [1]. William Kennett Loftus visited the site of Uruk in 1849, identifying it as \"Erech\", known as \"the second city of Nimrod\", and led the first excavations from 1850 to 1854 [1]. The city was formed when two smaller Ubaid settlements developed into the cities of Unug and Kullaba and later merged to become Uruk [1]. The temple complexes at their cores became the Eanna District dedicated to Inanna and the \"Anu\" District of Kullaba [1]. The Mask of Warka, also known as the \\''Lady of Uruk\\'' and the \\''Sumerian Mona Lisa\\'', dating from 3100 BC, is one of the earliest representations of the human face [1]. The Eanna district is historically significant as both writing and monumental public architecture emerged here during Uruk periods VI–IV [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:162967", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-029: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/41/White_Temple_ziggurat_in_Uruk.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/d0b9e72f/معبد_كاريوس_...اوروك_...._مدينة_الوركاء_..._المثنى.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:333004", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-006-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/d0b9e72f/معبد_كاريوس_...اوروك_...._مدينة_الوركاء_..._المثنى.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/d0b9e72f/White Temple ziggurat in Uruk.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:356206", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s003: site_type ''City'' -> ''City/town/settlement''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('d0dab028-351d-48e1-b749-821e89458654'::uuid, 'antiquity', '5', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:d0dab028-351d-48e1-b749-821e89458654:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''8'' restored on Blakey Topping Standing Stones', '50cecfe060a150c4956fa16f9a39fc1c', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 5 from period_start None"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:87632", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2177:chunk-0001: description ''Blakey Topping Standing Stones are the remains of a probable Bronze Age stone circle at the foot of Blakey Topping hill in the North York Moors. Robert Knox recorded three large standing stones in 1855, with the tallest measuring nine feet high, forming a triangle within an estimated circle diameter of 18 yards. The Elgees documented the site in 1930, noting ground hollows suggesting additional lost stones. Archaeologists Aubrey Burl and John Barnatt interpreted the surviving stones as remnants of a stone circle dating from the late Neolithic to Bronze Age (c.3000-800 BC). Some stones were later repurposed as gateposts and wall material.'' -> ''Blakey Topping standing stones is a small group of standing stones near the Hole of Horcum in the North York Moors [1]. It sits at the foot of Blakey Topping hill [1]. There are at least four standing stones currently surviving in this group, although some of them are of uncertain status [1]. The tallest stone is 1.9 metres high and is much-weathered and leaning [1]. A second stone is 1.0 metres high and appears to have had its top broke