    ('d13b661b-37ac-4ccb-bc4e-83b0bdd1da9c'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:d13b661b-37ac-4ccb-bc4e-83b0bdd1da9c:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Duggleby Howe', '87d15418735fa53bec38ae32eb1928e3', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Barrow'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 33 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:87640", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2177:chunk-0001: description ''Duggleby Howe is one of the largest round barrows in Britain, located in the Great Wold Valley of East Yorkshire. Dating to the Late Neolithic period (c. 3000 BC), the mound has a base diameter of 37 metres and consists of a chalk core covered by clay and an outer chalk capping. First excavated by Rev. Christopher Sykes in 1798-99, then extensively by J. R. Mortimer in 1890, the barrow contained a deep shaft grave with at least 10 adult and child skeletons. Grave goods included bone pins, a flint knife and axe, an antler mace head, a Towthorpe bowl, and Grimston-ware pottery. The barrow sits within a roughly circular interrupted ditch enclosure approximately 370 metres in diameter.'' -> ''Duggleby Howe is one of the largest round barrows in Britain, located on the southern side of the Great Wold Valley in the county of North Yorkshire, and is one of four such monuments in this area, known collectively as the Great barrows of East Yorkshire [1]. Duggleby Howe is believed on the basis of artefacts recovered to be of Late Neolithic date, but no radiocarbon dates are available [1]. The monument consists of a mound, the base of which was 120 feet in diameter [1]. The mound was 22 feet high at the eastern end and 18 or 19 feet high at the western end [1]. The barrow lies within a roughly circular enclosure, approximately 370 metres in diameter, formed from interrupted ditches, and open to the south [1]. The barrow was first excavated in either 1798 or 1799 by the Reverend Christopher Sykes, but no records of his excavation remain [1]. Beginning on 21 July 1890 J. R. Mortimer, under the sponsorship of Sir Tatton Sykes, excavated \"an area of 40 feet square over the centre of the barrow, and a portion of the east side\" over a period of more than six weeks [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:108870", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s019: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:108871", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s019: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:243700", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s010: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:243701", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s010: period_start None -> ''-3500''"}]'::jsonb),
