lbeaker culture, located near Keitum on the island of Sylt in Schleswig-Holstein [1]. The megalithic Harhoog burial chambers were originally located near the mud-flats between Keitum and Tinnum [1]. The stones were moved to the area near the Tipkenhoog on the coast near Keitum in 1954, when Sylt Airport was under development [1]. The chambers contain parallel and transverse sections [1]. The graves at Harhoog are dated to the Neolithic and belonged to ancient settlements of the island''s Funnelbeaker culture, probably around 3000 BC [1]. Harhoog dolmen is an extended dolmen, under Ernst Sprockhoff''s six-category classification; the other five types are simple dolmen, great dolmen, passage grave, long barrows (without a burial chamber) and cist [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:109677", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s020: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:109678", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s020: period_start ''-3000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:163700", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-030: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/d7c375d0/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/d7c375d0/Harhoog vom Tipkenhoog.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:243755", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s010: period_name None -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:243756", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s010: period_start None -> ''-3000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:318331", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-014: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/d7c375d0/Harhoog vom Tipkenhoog.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/d7c375d0/Megalithic_grave_Harhoog_in_Keitum,_Sylt,_Germany.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('d7c688fd-ca27-4a8b-91fd-affbcc948eee'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:d7c688fd-ca27-4a8b-91fd-affbcc948eee:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''5'' restored on The King''s Grave', '49f2b95ed9142b43749272d86833bcd0', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Necropolis/tombs complex'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 24 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29460", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0290:chunk-0001: period_start ''-3000'' -> ''-1400''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30517", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-period-name: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:87772", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2182:chunk-0001: description ''A monumental Nordic Bronze Age burial site near Kivik in southeastern Scania, Sweden, dating to approximately 1500 BC. With a diameter of 75 metres, it is the largest known burial mound in Sweden. The sarcophagus, measuring 3.25 m long, is the only Bronze Age stone burial chamber decorated with rock carvings in all of Scandinavia. Its petroglyphs depict people, animals, ships, lur players, and a horse-drawn chariot with four-spoked wheels. Discovered in the 1750s when quarrying ceased, it was systematically excavated between 1931 and 1933, revealing remains of a Stone Age settlement beneath the cairn.'' -> \"The King''s Grave lies near Kivik in southeastern Scania, Sweden [1]. The monument is a cairn, a burial mound constructed largely from stone, and is one of the largest known burial monuments in northern Europe [1]. In its restored form, the circular cairn measures about 75 metres across and 3.5 metres high [1]. Near its centre is a cist, a stone-built burial chamber approximately 4 metres long and 1.5 metres wide [1]. The principal cist is formed from ten upright stone slabs, eight of which are decorated with petroglyphs on the surfaces facing the burial chamber [1]. The carvings depict people, ships, birds, fish, horses, axes, circular symbols and figures playing lurs [1]. Only a small quantity of human remains survived within the principal cist because the monument had been looted before archaeological excavation [1]. Radiocarbon dating showed that the burials took place during at least three separate periods between approximately 1400 and 800 BCE [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:163701", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-030: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/48/Rock_carving_Kivik_Sweden.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/d7c688fd/0228Kungagraven Kivik.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:318334", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-014: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/d7c688fd/0228Kungagraven Kivik.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/d7c688fd/Rock_carving_Kivik_Sweden.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
