enian period; approximately 15000 years BP [1]. This site was studied by Pierre David from 1924 onwards, who discovered the frieze in 1926 [1]. The only human remains discovered are a single molar, discovered in 1933 and attributed to the Magdelenian [1]. The frieze is the only example of parietal art still visible in Charente [1]. The frieze includes depictions of an aurochs without its head, a pregnant tarpan, and a mating scene of tarpans [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:109681", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s020: period_name ''< 4500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:109682", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s020: period_start ''-13000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:163712", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-030: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/d82e4a95/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/d82e4a95/MouthiersB1.3.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:253697", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s019: period_name None -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:253698", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s019: period_start None -> ''-500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:335250", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-009-003: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/d82e4a95/MouthiersB1.3.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/d82e4a95/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:353683", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s009: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:353684", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s009: period_start ''-500'' -> ''-13050''"}]'::jsonb),
