    ('d99323af-e720-4cdd-9182-c4a73af9c764'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:d99323af-e720-4cdd-9182-c4a73af9c764:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''6'' restored on Corrimony Chambered Cairn', 'dd87f9e7c44d681b65a372ce8cd345ca', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Cairn'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 35 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:87820", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2183:chunk-0001: description ''Corrimony Chambered Cairn is a well-preserved Bronze Age burial monument near Glen Urquhart in the Scottish Highlands [1]. It belongs to the distinctive group of circular chambered cairns known as Clava cairns [1]. Archaeologist Professor Stuart Piggott excavated the site in 1952, uncovering one human skeleton and a single artefact [1]. Historic Environment Scotland designated it a scheduled monument in 1994 [1].'' -> ''The Corrimony chambered cairn, located near the village of Glen Urquhart in the Highlands of Scotland, is a well-preserved Bronze Age burial monument belonging to the group of circular chambered cairns, known as Clava cairns [1]. The site was excavated by archaeologist Professor Stuart Piggott, in 1952 [1]. The historic site consists of a burial mound, edged by a stone kerb, and surrounded by a circle of 11 large stones [1]. A narrow entrance passage, 1 m in height, leads to the central chamber of the tomb [1]. The chamber was constructed from large vertical boulders and drystone walls [1]. The monument is covered by a cup-marked, single slab and piled with small stones [1]. It is thought that the large, flat stone was the original capstone [1]. During excavation, a crouched inhumation burial was found in the central chamber under a floor of flagstones [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:163765", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-030: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/d99323af/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/d99323af/Corrimony Chambered Cairn 1.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
