and [1]. The wide enclosure between this and the ramparts further west is protected by sea-cliffs on the northern side; on the south side there is a smaller drop to the sea [1]. There is a gap in the peninsula, crossed by a modern bridge where there may have been originally a land-bridge; immediately before this there are three massive banks and ditches [1]. About 600 items of worked stone, over 4,000 shards of pottery, and objects of tin, copper and iron and glass were found [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:125580", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s010: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:125581", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s010: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:125582", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s010: site_type ''Fortress/citadel'' -> ''Fort''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:254097", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s020: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:254098", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s020: period_start None -> ''-800''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:353704", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s009: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:353705", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s009: period_start ''-800'' -> ''-10000''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('dae9bc10-f90d-45d4-9ab6-1408912b461e'::uuid, 'cultural_influence', '8', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:dae9bc10-f90d-45d4-9ab6-1408912b461e:cultural_influence', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: cultural_influence ''7'' restored on Wroxeter Stone', 'a2674fafff0ae24275be963e05559485', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "cultural_influence = 8 from description yes, thumbnail yes, 4 content link(s), 1 image(s), source_url yes"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28084", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0067:chunk-0001: site_type ''Rock relief/carving'' -> ''Inscription''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:32779", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0005:chunk-0001: description \"The Wroxeter Stone (also known as the Cunorix Stone) is an inscribed memorial stone dated to c. 460-475 AD, ploughed up in 1967 just outside the defenses of the Roman city of Viroconium Cornoviorum. The inscription reads CVNORIX MACVSMA QVICO[L]I[N]E, translated as ''Cunorix son of Maqui-Coline'' in partly-Latinized Primitive Irish, evidencing post-Roman Irish settlement in western Britain. The name Cunorix means ''hound-king'' in Celtic and may relate to Cynric of Wessex. The stone was reused from an earlier Roman tombstone that had been incorporated into a building.\" -> \"The Wroxeter Stone is the name given to a c. 460–75 AD inscribed stone unearthed in 1967 at Wroxeter, England (earlier the Roman city of Viroconium Cornoviorum) [1]. The text reads: CVNORIX | MACVSM/A | QVICO[L]I[N]E, traditionally normalised as Cunorix macus Maqui Coline and translated as Cunorīx son of Maqqos Colinī, where Cunorīx and Maqqos Colinī are personal names [1]. Cunorīx, literally meaning ''hound-king'', is a well attested Celtic name, and may relate to the etymology of the name of Cynric of Wessex, a 6th-century king [1]. Maqui Coline was read by Kenneth H. Jackson as a rendering of Primitive Irish *Maqqī-Colinī (genitive of *Maqqos-Colinī); this name literally means ''son of holly'', but is an example of a type of name fashionable in early Irish that, despite looking like patronyms, were in fact given names [1]. It is one of a number of such names that include words for trees and is attested in later Old Irish in the form Macc-Cuilinn [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:125583", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s010: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''500 - 1000 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:125584", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s010: period_start ''1'' -> ''501''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:163822", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-030: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d4/Wroxeter_Roman_City_remains.JPG'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:326204", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-005-003: thumbnail_url None -> ''/data/images/wiki/dae9bc10/Wroxeter Roman City remains.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
