d translated as Cunorīx son of Maqqos Colinī, where Cunorīx and Maqqos Colinī are personal names [1]. Cunorīx, literally meaning ''hound-king'', is a well attested Celtic name, and may relate to the etymology of the name of Cynric of Wessex, a 6th-century king [1]. Maqui Coline was read by Kenneth H. Jackson as a rendering of Primitive Irish *Maqqī-Colinī (genitive of *Maqqos-Colinī); this name literally means ''son of holly'', but is an example of a type of name fashionable in early Irish that, despite looking like patronyms, were in fact given names [1]. It is one of a number of such names that include words for trees and is attested in later Old Irish in the form Macc-Cuilinn [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:125583", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s010: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''500 - 1000 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:125584", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s010: period_start ''1'' -> ''501''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:163822", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-030: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d4/Wroxeter_Roman_City_remains.JPG'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:326204", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-005-003: thumbnail_url None -> ''/data/images/wiki/dae9bc10/Wroxeter Roman City remains.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('daf2b273-b9a9-4fe9-bd71-b76ff56e7671'::uuid, 'antiquity', '5', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:daf2b273-b9a9-4fe9-bd71-b76ff56e7671:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''8'' restored on Falkner''s Circle', '37dbe0367d27ce2745ae85a1487e4306', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 5 from period_start None"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28351", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0116:chunk-0001: period_start ''-4500'' -> ''-2300''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30519", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-period-name: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:87862", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2184:chunk-0001: description \"Falkner''s Circle was a stone circle near the village of Avebury in the south-western English county of Wiltshire. Built from twelve sarsen megaliths, it measured about 37 metres (121 ft) in diameter. Today, only one stone remains standing, representing a fragment of a broader tradition of stone circle construction that spread across Britain, Ireland, and Brittany during the Late Neolithic and Early Bronze Age.[1]\" -> \"Falkner''s Circle was a stone circle near the village of Avebury in the south-western English county of Wiltshire [1]. Built from twelve sarsen megaliths, it measured about 37 metres in diameter, although only one of these stones remains standing [1]. The ring was part of a tradition of stone circle construction that spread throughout much of Britain, Ireland, and Brittany during the Late Neolithic and Early Bronze Age [1]. Positioned in a dry valley, the circle was erected on the southern end of a field that contained a range of natural sarsens [1]. Extensive flint knapping took place at the site during the Late Neolithic or Early Bronze Age [1]. The earliest known report of the site came from a Mr Falkner, who discovered it in 1840 while riding in the area [1]. The site was excavated in 2002 in an archaeological project led by Mark Gillings and Joshua Pollard [1]. Falkner''s Circle is 750m south-east of Avebury [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:109748", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s020: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:109749", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s020: period_start ''-2300'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:254099", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s020: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:254100", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s020: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:353706", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s009: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:353707", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s009: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
