eau of western Germany [1]. Systematic excavations have revealed evidence of 160 houses from eight distinct settlement sites, plus three enclosures and a cemetery, belonging to the period 5300-4900 BC [1]. The site is a key region for understanding the nature of the earliest farming societies in west-central Europe (Bandkeramik era) [1]. Extensive surveys done from 1973 to 1981 contributed to recovery of the largest settlements in the valley before destruction of the area for mining [1]. The settlement at Langweiler 8 lasted over 300 years, with permanent occupation of about 15 houses [1]. The agricultural basis for these Bandkeramik settlements is reconstructed as small-scale cereal cultivation in fixed plots adjacent to the settlements [1]. During the latest stage of the Merzbach Bandkeramik period, special enclosures were constructed which may have been the setting for social or ritual activities by whole communities [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:109812", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s020: period_name ''< 4500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:109813", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s020: period_start ''-5300'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:109814", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s020: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:243805", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s010: period_name None -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:243806", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s010: period_start None -> ''-5300''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:243807", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s010: site_type None -> ''Settlement''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:356225", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s003: site_