líes Province, Tantamayo District [1]. The complex consists of two parts named Piruro I and Piruro II [1]. This archaeological zone has had a long occupation: Its first vestiges date from 3000 to 2500 years B.C. (Final Preceramic), and they extend until the Inca Empire (Late Horizon) [1]. The Fortress of Piruro II can be found on the right side of the river Tantamayo over 3.8 km above sea level [1]. The fortress is composed of walls built with stones hewn in squared shape and uniform size [1]. The building is a five-story structure with rectangular doors and windows on its front area [1]. Currently the building is enclosed by stone walls measuring approximately 1 1/2 meters in height [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:109830", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s020: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:109831", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s020: period_start ''-3000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:109832", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s020: site_type ''Temple complex'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:164381", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-031: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/de/Complejo_arqueol%C3%B3gico_piruro_hu%C3%A1nuco.PNG'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/df70e98f/Piruro 02123.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:243813", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s010: period_name None -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:243814", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s010: period_start None -> ''-3000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:243815", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s010: site_type None -> ''Fortress''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:318454", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote