    ('e14380ae-7474-4960-8aa2-0c4b85569cae'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:e14380ae-7474-4960-8aa2-0c4b85569cae:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''6'' restored on Mên-an-Tol', 'e74c563e41016bcafd5bfa5f9c31834c', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Megalithic stones'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 33 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:42254", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0064:chunk-0001: description \"The Men-an-Tol (Cornish for ''the stone with a hole'') is a late Neolithic or early Bronze Age monument near Madron in Cornwall, England. It consists of a 1.3 m wide circular holed granite stone flanked by two 1.2 m tall standing stones. First investigated by William Borlase in 1749, his plan showed the stones formed an angle of about 135 degrees rather than today''s alignment. A 1993 study by the Cornwall Archaeological Unit found evidence of 11 additional buried stones, suggesting the formation originated from a circle of 18-20 stones. Local folklore attributes healing powers to crawling through the hole.\" -> ''The Mên-an-Tol is a small formation of standing stones in Cornwall [1]. The Mên-an-Tol consists of three upright granite stones: a round stone with its middle holed out with two standing stones to each side, in front of and behind the hole [1]. The two side stones are both about 1.2 metres high [1]. The westernmost stone was moved and brought into a straight line with the other two stones sometime after 1815 [1]. The holed stone is roughly octagonal in outline [1]. It is 1.3 metres wide and 1.1 metres high; the circular hole is 0.5 m in diameter [1]. The Mên-an-Tol is thought to date to either the late Neolithic or early Bronze Age [1]. In 1749 the site was first archaeologically investigated by William Borlase, who also drew a plan [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:110515", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s021: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:110516", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s021: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-2500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:164473", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-031: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/e14380ae/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/e14380ae/Men_An_Tol_Cornwall_by_Kernow_Skies.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:318492", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-014: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/e14380ae/Men_An_Tol_Cornwall_by_Kernow_Skies.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/e14380ae/Menantol5.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
