    ('e1548be0-b310-4afd-82dc-bce186b01410'::uuid, 'antiquity', '5', '3', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:e1548be0-b310-4afd-82dc-bce186b01410:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''3'' restored on Uley', 'ff23096afd8748891632d16fc8135331', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 5 from period_start None"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:88018", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2188:chunk-0001: description ''Romano-British temple complex on West Hill dedicated to Mercury, built atop a Neolithic sanctuary. Excavated 1977-79, the site yielded over 60,000 pottery fragments, 250,000 animal bones, and a limestone cult statue of Mercury. The stone temple, erected c. 100 AD, measured 14 x 12 metres and was surrounded by living quarters and shops. Inscribed lead curse tablets and votive offerings attest to a thriving Mercury cult. In the 5th century, a timber church replaced the destroyed pagan temple.'' -> ''The Romans built a temple at West Hill, near Uley, on the site of an earlier prehistoric shrine [1]. Following the laying of a water main pipe there in 1976, many discoveries were made including numerous Roman writing tablets or lead curse tablets from the temple area [1]. These writing tablets appear often to relate to theft, and here the mention of animals and farm implements is a regular theme [1]. Other remains from this temple, including a fine stone head of Mercury, can now be seen in the British Museum [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:110519", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-