    ('e20a253e-63e2-4423-9852-c05615206f93'::uuid, 'mystery', '3', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:e20a253e-63e2-4423-9852-c05615206f93:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Lepenski Vir', '36641871fa222d77a4f714baa9b94ea9', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 3 from (''City/town/settlement'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 105 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:88042", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2189:chunk-0001: description \"Lepenski Vir is a Mesolithic archaeological site in Serbia featuring one of Europe''s earliest known permanent settlements, with evidence of hunter-gatherer occupation dating from circa 9500–7200 BC and transition to Neolithic farming communities by 5500 BC [1]. The site is renowned for its distinctive trapezoidal house plans and monumental sculpture, including carved stone figures representing hybrid human-fish motifs that reflect the inhabitants'' ritual relationship with the Danube River [1]. Discovered in 1960, Lepenski Vir demonstrates the complex cultural evolution from Mesolithic foragers to Early Neolithic agriculturalists in the Balkans [1].\" -> ''Lepenski Vir is an important archaeological site of the Lepenski Vir culture [1]. The latest radiocarbon and AMS data suggests that the chronology of Lepenski Vir spans between 9500/7200 and 5500 BC, divided into Early–Middle Mesolithic, Late Mesolithic, Transition and Neolithic [1]. Lepenski Vir is located on the right bank of the Danube in eastern Serbia, within the Iron Gates gorge [1]. Found artifacts include tools made from stone and bone, the remains of houses, and numerous sacral objects including unique piscine (fish) with human-like face stone sculptures [1]. The layout of the houses, which are on an inclined promontory that opens to the river, is terrace-like and spread in a fan-shaped arrangement, allowing access to the river by people from houses further from the bank [1]. Some of the dead were buried in the houses under the exceptionally preserved floors [1]. All the sculptures were carved from round sandstone cobbles found on the river banks [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:164491", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-031: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/e20a253e/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/e20a253e/Lepenski Vir (1).webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:318510", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-014: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/e20a253e/Lepenski Vir (1).webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/e20a253e/Lepenski_vir_Praroditeljka_(Foremother,_cca_7000_BCE).webp''"}]'::jsonb),
