in the Iron Gates gorge [1]. Found artifacts include tools made from stone and bone, the remains of houses, and numerous sacral objects including unique piscine (fish) with human-like face stone sculptures [1]. The layout of the houses, which are on an inclined promontory that opens to the river, is terrace-like and spread in a fan-shaped arrangement, allowing access to the river by people from houses further from the bank [1]. Some of the dead were buried in the houses under the exceptionally preserved floors [1]. All the sculptures were carved from round sandstone cobbles found on the river banks [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:164491", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-031: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/e20a253e/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/e20a253e/Lepenski Vir (1).webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:318510", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-014: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/e20a253e/Lepenski Vir (1).webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/e20a253e/Lepenski_vir_Praroditeljka_(Foremother,_cca_7000_BCE).webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('e21d32e0-beba-4e70-9a7f-2a56cf70a476'::uuid, 'mystery', '3', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:e21d32e0-beba-4e70-9a7f-2a56cf70a476:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Huaca Prieta', '394db2112796b983bf7f46fecc621b30', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 3 from (''City/town/settlement'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 105 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:81378", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2031:chunk-0001: description ''Huaca Prieta (\"Black Pyramid\") is a preceramic archaeological site at the mouth of the Chicama River in northern Peru, part of the El Brujo Archaeological Complex. Human presence dates back approximately 14,000 years, while mound construction began around 7,500 years ago and continued for 4,000 years, reaching 30 meters tall. The inhabitants grew squash, chilies, and cotton, caught fish, and wove baskets and cloth without pottery or maize. Researchers discovered the world\\''s earliest known use of indigo dye, a 6,000-year-old textile predating Egyptian indigo by 1,500 years. First excavated by Junius B. Bird in 1946-1947.'' -> \"Huaca Prieta is the site of an Andean preceramic settlement along the Pacific Ocean in the Chicama Valley, just north of Trujillo, La Libertad Province [1]. It is a part of the El Brujo Archaeological Complex, which also includes sites of the Moche culture [1]. The inhabitants fished, gathered shellfish, and grew fruit, gourds, squash, peppers, beans, tubers and, importantly, cotton [1]. In 2016, 6000-year-old dyed cotton fabric was discovered at the Preceramic site of Huaca Prieta [1]. This marks the earliest recorded use of cotton worldwide [1]. Analysis of the pigment used on the cloth identified it as indigotin, an indigoid dye [1]. This marks the earliest recorded use of indigo dye to date, predating the use of indigo in Egypt''s Fifth Dynasty by about 1,500 years [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:110534", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s021: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:110535", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s021: period_start ''-500'' -> ''-12000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:164492", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-031: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/e21d32e0/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/e21d32e0/Huaca Prieta-9.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:318513", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-014: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/e21d32e0/Huaca Prieta-9.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/e21d32e0/View_from_top_of_Huaca_Cao_Viejo.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('e21d32e0-beba-4e70-9a7f-2a56cf70a476'::uuid, 'rarity_score', '23', '26', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:e21d32e0-beba-4e70-9a7f-2a56cf70a476:rarity_score', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: rarity_score ''26'' restored on Huaca Prieta', '394db2112796b983bf7f46fecc621b30', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "rarity_score = 23 from mystery 3, cultural_influence 7"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:81378", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2031:chunk-0001: description ''Huaca Prieta (\"Black Pyramid\") is a preceramic archaeological site at the mouth of the Chicama River in northern Peru, part of the El Brujo Archaeological Complex. Human presence dates back approximately 14,000 years, while mound construction began around 7,500 years ago and continued for 4,000 years, reaching 30 meters tall. The inhabitants grew squash, chilies, and cotton, caught fish, and wove baskets and cloth without pottery or maize. Researchers discovered the world\\''s earliest known use of indigo dye, a 6,000-year-old textile predating Egyptian indigo by 1,500 years. First excavated by Junius B. Bird in 1946-1947.'' -> \"Huaca Prieta is the site of an Andean preceramic settlement along the Pacific Ocean in the Chicama Valley, just north of Trujillo, La Libertad Province [1]. It is a part of the El Brujo Archaeological Complex, which also includes sites of the Moche culture [1]. The inhabitants fished, gathered shellfish, and grew fruit, gourds, squash, peppers, beans, tubers and, importantly, cotton [1]. In 2016, 6000-year-old dyed cotton fabric was discovered at the Preceramic site of Huaca Prieta [1]. This marks the earliest recorded use of cotton worldwide [1]. Analysis of the pigment used on the cloth identified it as indigotin, an indigoid dye [1]. This marks the earliest recorded use of indigo dye to date, predating the use of indigo in Egypt''s Fifth Dynasty by about 1,500 years [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:110534", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s021: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:110535", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s021: period_start ''-500'' -> ''-12000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:164492", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-031: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/e21d32e0/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/e21d32e0/Huaca Prieta-9.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:318513", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-014: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/e21d32e0/Huaca Prieta-9.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/e21d32e0/View_from_top_of_Huaca_Cao_Viejo.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('e21d32e0-beba-4e70-9a7f-2a56cf70a476'::uuid, 'total_power', '26', '27', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:e21d32e0-beba-4e70-9a7f-2a56cf70a476:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: total_power ''27'' restored on Huaca Prieta', '394db2112796b983bf7f46fecc621b30', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 26 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 10 + 5 + 7 + 3 + 1"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:81378", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2031:chunk-0001: description ''Huaca Prieta (\"Black Pyramid\") is a preceramic archaeological site at the mouth of the Chicama River in northern Peru, part of the El Brujo Archaeological Complex. Human presence dates back approximately 14,000 years, while mound construction began around 7,500 years ago and continued for 4,000 years, reaching 30 meters tall. The inhabitants grew squash, chilies, and cotton, caught fish, and wove baskets and cloth without pottery or maize. Researchers discovered the world\\''s earliest known use of indigo dye, a 6,000-year-old textile predating Egyptian indigo by 1,500 years. First excavated by Junius B. Bird in 1946-1947.'' -> \"Huaca Prieta is the site of an Andean preceramic settlement along the Pacific Ocean in the Chicama Valley, just north of Trujillo, La Libertad Province [1]. It is a part of the El Brujo Archaeological Complex, which also includes sites of the Moche culture [1]. The inhabitants fished, gathered shellfish, and grew fruit, gourds, squash, peppers, beans, tubers and, importantly, cotton [1]. In 2016, 6000-year-old dyed cotton fabric was discovered at the Preceramic site of Huaca Prieta [1]. This marks the earliest recorded use of cotton worldwide [1]. Analysis of the pigment used on the cloth identified it as indigotin, an indigoid dye [1]. This marks the earliest recorded use of indigo dye to date, predating the use of indigo in Egypt''s Fifth Dynasty by about 1,500 years [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:110534", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s021: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:110535", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s021: period_start ''-500'' -> ''-12000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:164492", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-031: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/e21d32e0/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/e21d32e0/Huaca Prieta-9.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:318513", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-014: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/e21d32e0/Huaca Prieta-9.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/e21d32e0/View_from_top_of_Huaca_Cao_Viejo.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('e244d46c-a958-4c42-a154-5ed1ae613600'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:e244d46c-a958-4c42-a154-5ed1ae613600:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''6'' restored on Greek Theatre of Syracuse', '72dd2f784b86d5643f0179ce3fd951cb', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Theatre'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 28 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:81380", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2031:chunk-0001: description ''One of the largest theatres of the ancient Greek world, first built in the 5th century BC on the slopes of Temenite Hill in Syracuse, Sicily. The current Hellenistic structure dates to 238-215 BC under Hieron II, featuring a 138.5-metre-wide cavea divided into nine sections with seating for approximately 15,000 spectators. Aeschylus likely premiered works here around 476-472 BC. Romans later modified the orchestra for gladiatorial contests and added a water pool for aquatic performances. In the 16th century, Spanish forces under Charles V dismantled portions to build fortifications on Ortygia. Since 1914, the INDA has hosted annual classical drama performances at the site.'' -> ''The Greek theatre of Syracuse lies on the south slopes of the Temenite hill, overlooking the modern city of Syracuse in southeastern Sicily [1]. The theatron had a diameter of 138.6 metres, one of the largest in the Greek world, and originally had 67 rows of seating, mostly cut into rock [1]. A pathway runs around the theatre halfway up the cavea, dividing it in two [1]. The decoration of the scene probably underwent renovations in the Flavian and/or Antonine periods [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:110536", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s021: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:110537", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s021: period_start ''-1500'' -> ''-500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:164496", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-031: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/e244d46c/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/e244d46c/Syracuse AncientGreekTheatre 0257.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
