n inhabited since the 3rd millennium BCE [1]. Lilaea was destroyed during the Third Sacred War by Philip II of Macedon in 346 BCE, but it was rebuilt during the following years in the course of the project for the reconstruction of the Phocian citadels [1]. When Pausanias visited Lilaea, in the second century, he reported seeing a theatre, an agora, and baths, with temples of Apollo and Artemis [1]. Lilaea is located near the modern namesake village of Lilaia [1]. Its walls and gates are to the east of the modern settlement [1]. The fortification of the city is evident on the hill \"Pyrgos\" or \"Palaeokastro\" [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:110551", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s021: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:110552", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s021: period_start ''-3000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:164509", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-031: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/e2aa3754/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/e2aa3754/Acropolis of Lilaia 3.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:254177", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s020: period_name None -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:254178", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s020: period_start None -> ''-500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:353758", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s009: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:353759", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s009: period_start ''-500'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('e2bb7b81-bdd5-4713-893f-3c2c516a3d7a'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:e2bb7b81-bdd5-4713-893f-3c2c516a3d7a:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''8'' restored on Vindobona', 'a3e0341d73f2b0e7b0775a05c68c04a4', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''1 - 500 AD'') is shared by 91 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29701", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0327:chunk-0001: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Fortress''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:45365", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0110:chunk-0001: description \"A Roman legionary fortress established in the 1st century AD on the Danube frontier in the province of Pannonia, on the site of modern Vienna. The camp covered about 20 hectares and housed around 6,000 soldiers, with its wall trace still visible in Vienna''s inner-city streets including Graben, Naglergasse, and Tiefer Graben. Emperor Marcus Aurelius died here on 17 March 180 AD during a campaign against Germanic tribes. A civilian settlement grew southeast of the fort from the 3rd century. The fortress suffered a devastating fire in the early 5th century. Now part of the UNESCO Danube Limes World Heritage Site, remains are displayed at the Romermuseum beneath Hoher Markt.\" -> ''Vindobona was a Roman military camp (or castra) in the province of Pannonia, located on the site of the modern city of Vienna in Austria [1]. Early references to Vindobona are made by the geographer Ptolemy in his Geographica and the historian Aurelius Victor, who recounts that emperor Marcus Aurelius died in Vindobona on 17 March 180 from an unknown illness while on a military campaign against invading Germanic tribes [1]. Vindobona was a military camp contiguous to the civilian city of Canabae [1]. The Danube marked the border of the Roman Empire, and Vindobona was part of a defensive network including the camps of Carnuntum, Brigetio and Aquincum [1]. Over 3,000 stamped bricks, several stone monuments and written sources prove that several legions, cavalry units and marines were stationed in Vindobona [1]. Legio X Gemina from Aquincum arrived in 114 AD and remained in Vindobona until the 5th century [1]. Excavations have revealed that Vindobona received its supply through a 17 km long water pipeline [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:164512", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-031: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ee/Wien.Hofburg10.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/e2bb7b81/Canabae legionis Vindobona.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:318528", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-014: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/e2bb7b81/Canabae legionis Vindobona.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/e2bb7b81/Wien.Hofburg10.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:356229", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s003: site_type ''Fortress'' -> ''Fortress/citadel''"}]'::jsonb),
