nd the historian Aurelius Victor, who recounts that emperor Marcus Aurelius died in Vindobona on 17 March 180 from an unknown illness while on a military campaign against invading Germanic tribes [1]. Vindobona was a military camp contiguous to the civilian city of Canabae [1]. The Danube marked the border of the Roman Empire, and Vindobona was part of a defensive network including the camps of Carnuntum, Brigetio and Aquincum [1]. Over 3,000 stamped bricks, several stone monuments and written sources prove that several legions, cavalry units and marines were stationed in Vindobona [1]. Legio X Gemina from Aquincum arrived in 114 AD and remained in Vindobona until the 5th century [1]. Excavations have revealed that Vindobona received its supply through a 17 km long water pipeline [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:164512", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-031: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ee/Wien.Hofburg10.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/e2bb7b81/Canabae legionis Vindobona.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:318528", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-014: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/e2bb7b81/Canabae legionis Vindobona.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/e2bb7b81/Wien.Hofburg10.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:356229", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s003: site_type ''Fortress'' -> ''Fortress/citadel''"}]'::jsonb),
