rsor to the present day city [1]. It consists of two or three interlinked sub-enclosures, with what appears to be a field system extending to the east; an unusual layout, the site remains relatively little studied and has not been archaeologically excavated [1]. It is a Scheduled Ancient Monument, and shares its name with the surrounding King''s Castle Wood—today a Somerset Wildlife Trust nature reserve—though this name is probably a modern invention [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:125687", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s010: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:125688", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s010: period_start ''-1000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:125689", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s010: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Settlement''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:254193", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s020: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:254194", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s020: period_start None -> ''-1500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:356231", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s003: site_type ''Settlement'' -> ''City/town/settlement''"}]'::jsonb),
