    ('e5e74a71-b666-42a3-917b-cec6e80c81ca'::uuid, 'cultural_influence', '9', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:e5e74a71-b666-42a3-917b-cec6e80c81ca:cultural_influence', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: cultural_influence ''7'' restored on Altar of the Twelve Gods', '902c96dc6e00163bd716bcb50a6a9f3e', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "cultural_influence = 9 from description yes, thumbnail yes, 4 content link(s), 2 image(s), source_url yes"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29114", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0240:chunk-0001: site_type ''Temple complex'' -> ''Sanctuary''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:88132", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2191:chunk-0001: description \"The Altar of the Twelve Gods was a sacred sanctuary in ancient Athens, situated in the northwest corner of the Classical Agora. Established by Pisistratus the Younger during his archonship in 522/1 BC, it served as the central marker from which all distances from Athens were measured, functioning as the city''s official geographic point of reference. The sanctuary provided a place of supplication and refuge for individuals seeking asylum, reflecting its protected religious status. The altar honored the twelve Olympian gods, though the precise identities of these deities remain a subject of scholarly debate. Archaeological excavations have revealed foundations of the altar, confirming its location and scale within the Agora complex.\" -> \"The Altar of the Twelve Gods (also called the Sanctuary of the Twelve Gods), was an important altar and sanctuary at Athens, located in the northwest corner of the Classical Agora [1]. The altar was set up by Pisistratus the Younger, (the grandson of the tyrant Pisistratus) during his archonship [1]. The altar was dismantled c.\\u2009267 AD [1]. From at least the 5th century BC, the altar became the zero point from which distances to Athens were calculated [1]. The altar also functioned as a place of supplication and refuge [1]. The exact location of the altar within the Classical Agora, unspecified in ancient sources, was discovered through excavations, in 1891 and 1934, at a site just opposite the Stoa of Zeus, and adjacent to the Panathenaic Way [1]. The Altar''s rectangular enclosure or peribolos, was roughly square, 9.5m per side [1]. A parapet wall surrounded a paved enclosure containing a rectangular altar proper, presumably at its center [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:165070", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-032: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/e5e74a71/hero.webp'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:342629", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "insert-2026-10-07-007-001: thumbnail_url None -> ''/data/images/wiki/e5e74a71/Ancient Greece Marble Cylindrical altar of the Twelve Gods, found near the Agora, 350-340 BC.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
