nctuary at Athens, located in the northwest corner of the Classical Agora [1]. The altar was set up by Pisistratus the Younger, (the grandson of the tyrant Pisistratus) during his archonship [1]. The altar was dismantled c.\\u2009267 AD [1]. From at least the 5th century BC, the altar became the zero point from which distances to Athens were calculated [1]. The altar also functioned as a place of supplication and refuge [1]. The exact location of the altar within the Classical Agora, unspecified in ancient sources, was discovered through excavations, in 1891 and 1934, at a site just opposite the Stoa of Zeus, and adjacent to the Panathenaic Way [1]. The Altar''s rectangular enclosure or peribolos, was roughly square, 9.5m per side [1]. A parapet wall surrounded a paved enclosure containing a rectangular altar proper, presumably at its center [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:165070", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-032: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/e5e74a71/hero.webp'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:342629", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "insert-2026-10-07-007-001: thumbnail_url None -> ''/data/images/wiki/e5e74a71/Ancient Greece Marble Cylindrical altar of the Twelve Gods, found near the Agora, 350-340 BC.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
