    ('e8f52f85-51d3-426c-8c5b-117fb9dfd6c3'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:e8f52f85-51d3-426c-8c5b-117fb9dfd6c3:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''6'' restored on Vinquoy Chambered Cairn', '81f9dbfea04fce4739abe1f3ebc27e4d', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Cairn'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 35 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:88246", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2194:chunk-0001: description ''Vinquoy Chambered Cairn is a Neolithic chambered cairn on the island of Eday in Orkney, Scotland. Built in the early third millennium BC, it dates to approximately 3000-2500 BC [1]. The tomb is similar in design to Maeshowe, a famous Neolithic tomb on Mainland Orkney [1]. The monument was designated as a scheduled ancient monument by Historic Environment Scotland in 1936 [1]. The cairn represents the prehistoric funerary practices of the Orkney islands.'' -> ''Vinquoy chambered cairn is a Neolithic chambered cairn located on the island of Eday in Orkney, Scotland [1]. The tomb probably dates to the early third millennium BC, and is similar in design to Maeshowe Neolithic tomb found on Mainland Orkney [1]. The monument was built on the highest point in the island, on Vinquoy Hill on the North side of Eday, overlooking Calf Sound [1]. The tomb is around 18 m in diameter with a height of 3 m, and was constructed with red sandstone [1]. It has a 5 m entrance passage leading to a central chamber with four small side-cells [1]. The chambered cairn is partly below ground, carved into the hill [1]. The central chamber originally had a corbelled roof [1]. The tomb was first excavated in 1857 by antiquarian James Farrer and landowner Robert Fraser Hebden, who dug into the chamber through the top of the mound [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:165220", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-032: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2c/Vinquoy_chambered_tomb_Eday.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/e8f52f85/hero.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
