ed that a second and perhaps a third chamber exist [1]. In or prior to 1911, Dr Beveridge excavated within and near the cairn, and he found evidence of burnt burials as well as pieces of pottery (some with patterned lines), wood ashes, burnt bones, a flint arrowhead, a scraper, and a piece of pierced talc [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:111352", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s022: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:111353", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s022: period_start ''-4500'' -> ''-3000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:165254", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-032: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/eae0879c/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/eae0879c/Barpa Langass - 47092123164.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:319273", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-015: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/eae0879c/Barpa Langass - 47092123164.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/eae0879c/Barpa_Langais_20090608.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('eaf2f471-84d2-4661-8781-6a81241b11fb'::uuid, 'antiquity', '7', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:eaf2f471-84d2-4661-8781-6a81241b11fb:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: antiquity ''8'' restored on Fernacre', 'd7351346aa5319953a2bed79e843f6a0', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 7 from period_start -2500"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43113", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0066:chunk-0001: description \"One of Cornwall''s largest stone circles, located on Bodmin Moor northeast of St Breward. The oval monument measures 46 x 44 metres and contains approximately 70 irregularly shaped granite stones, with 39 still standing. Aubrey Burl identified Fernacre and nearby Stannon Circle as among the oldest on the moor, dating to the Late Neolithic or Early Bronze Age. Roger Mercer''s 1968 excavation recovered cord-ornamented pottery, querns, flints, and two greenstone axes indicating substantial tree clearance. The circle aligns with Brown Willy at the equinox sunrise.\" -> ''Fernacre, also known as Fernacre stone circle or Fernacre circle, is a stone circle located on the slopes of the De Lank River, 1.25 miles northeast of St Breward on Bodmin Moor in Cornwall in the United Kingdom [1]. Fernacre is one of the biggest stone circles in Cornwall [1]. Fernacre is a marker for the start of a medieval drove road from Rough Tor to Garrow [1]. Fernacre carries a pivotal location between Rough Tor located due north and Brown Willy located due east [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:111356", "url": "remed