    ('ecc0e85d-51c0-47a0-a63f-a427b1f94b01'::uuid, 'mystery', '2', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:ecc0e85d-51c0-47a0-a63f-a427b1f94b01:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''8'' restored on Durovernum Cantiacorum', '9deaa6c6211b959b15ab0aaeaedd2b54', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 2 from (''City/town/settlement'', ''1 - 500 AD'') is shared by 180 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:88314", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2196:chunk-0001: description ''Durovernum Cantiacorum, meaning \"fort of the Cantiaci by the alder swamp,\" was a major Roman town at modern Canterbury in Kent, strategically located at a ford on the River Stour. Occupied shortly after the Roman invasion of 43 AD, it developed significantly during the Flavian period (69-96 AD) as the civitas capital of the Cantiaci tribe. A large religious and administrative complex included a forum, basilica, temple enclosure, and a theatre originally built around 80 AD and rebuilt in the early 3rd century. Defensive walls were added in the late 3rd century with single-arched gateways. The Canterbury Roman Museum, built on remains of a Roman townhouse, preserves in situ mosaics and artifacts. Connected to London and coastal ports via Watling Street, the town survived until the Roman withdrawal around 410 AD.'' -> \"Durovernum Cantiacorum was a town and hillfort in Roman Britain at the site of present-day Canterbury in Kent [1]. It occupied a strategic location on Watling Street at the best local crossing of the Stour, which prompted a convergence of roads connected to the ports of Dubris, Rutupiae, Regulbium and Portus Lemanis [1]. The Iron-Age oppidum at the site was triple-ditched [1]. The site seems to have been occupied and fortified by the Romans shortly after their invasion in AD 43 [1]. Military occupation continued until at least the time of Boudica''s rebellion [1]. A large religious and administrative complex was soon established at its centre, consisting of forum and basilica, temple enclosure and theatre [1]. The theatre, originally built around AD 80, was totally rebuilt in the early 3rd century [1]. Because of its links with Gaul, Durovernum seems to have survived in good order until the Roman administration left around AD 410 [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:111392", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s022: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Town''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:319306", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-015: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/ecc0e85d/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/ecc0e85d/Roman_Museum_146.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:356242", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s003: site_type ''Town'' -> ''City/town/settlement''"}]'::jsonb),
