red Neolithic pottery including Grooved ware and Beaker ware, stone tools, and cremated human bones. The name references the game of quoits, with local legend claiming King Arthur himself played with the capstone. The site is managed by Cadw as a scheduled ancient monument.'' -> ''Carreg Coetan Arthur is a Neolithic dolmen in Newport, Pembrokeshire, Wales [1]. Carreg Coetan Arthur dates from around 3000 BC and is the remains of a Neolithic burial chamber (also known as a quoit) [1]. The remains consist of a 4-metre-long capstone on smaller supporting rocks [1]. It would have originally been earth covered, but this has eroded away [1]. The site is managed by Cadw and it is a scheduled monument [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:111427", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s022: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:111428", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s022: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:165832", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-033: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/eecda91b/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/eecda91b/Carreg Coetan Arthur H0a.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:244741", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s011: period_name None -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:244742", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s011: period_start None -> ''-3000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:353841", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s009: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:353842", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s009: period_start ''-3000'' -> ''-3650''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ef0072cc-9021-4f21-8e22-4163967650d6'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:ef0072cc-9021-4f21-8e22-4163967650d6:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''5'' restored on Tregeseal East Stone Circle', 'c5d8269727b4e5b9a0a3c4b1fb54dda4', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Stone circle'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 57 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:88334", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2197:chunk-0001: description ''Tregeseal East (Cornish: Meyn an Dons, meaning \"Stones of the Dance\") is a heavily restored prehistoric stone circle located 1.6 km (1 mi) northeast of St Just in Cornwall, England [1]. The circle consists of 19 granite blocks, also known as \"The Dancing Stones\" [1]. It is the one surviving circle of three that once stood aligned along an east-west axis on the hillside south of Carn Kenidjack [1].'' -> ''The Tregeseal East stone circle is a heavily restored prehistoric stone circle around one mile northeast of the town of St Just in Cornwall [1]. The 19 granite stones are also known as The Dancing Stones [1]. The stone circle is in west Cornwall north of the road from Penzance to St Just in Penwith and is approximately one kilometer east of the hamlet of Tregeseal [1]. The stone circle consists of 19 granite blocks with a height between 1–1.4 m, which describe an approximate circle with a diameter of around 21 m [1]. The stone circle was subjected over the centuries to substantial rebuilding and restoration work, so that today only the stones in the eastern half of the circle may be in their original positions [1]. The earliest known modern description appears in 1754 in Antiquities, Historical and Monumental, of the County of Cornwall by the Cornish antiquarian William Borlase [1]. Borlase recorded 17 upright stones arranged in a circular form on the hillside south of Carn Kenidjack [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:111429", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s022: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:111430", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s022: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:165834", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-033: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2a/Tregeseal_east_towards_carn_kenidjack.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/ef0072cc/Tregeseal East Stone Circle.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:244743", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s011: period_name None -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:244744", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s011: period_start None -> ''-2500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:326424", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-005-003: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/ef0072cc/Tregeseal East Stone Circle.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/ef0072cc/Tregeseal east towards carn kenidjack.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
