ula, about 4 km north of Apostolos Andreas Monastery [1]. The settlement is situated on a little plateau at the steep flank of the limestone promontory, about halfway between the main plateau of the peninsula and the sea in a very inaccessible location [1]. Between 1970 and 1973 three campaigns of excavations were conducted by a French team headed by Alain Le Brun [1]. The settlement consists of small round or roundish houses [1]. The houses contain a hearth and sometimes container-bins in the floor [1]. The site belongs to the aceramic Neolithic and dates to the 6th Millennium BC [1]. The carbonised remains of einkorn, emmer and some barley have been found [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:83132", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26c_fields-wd1-s001: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Settlement''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:353853", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s009: lat ''35.53869568390137'' -> ''35.69442''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:353854", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s009: lon ''34.27675771835874'' -> ''34.5859''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:356246", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s003: site_type ''Settlement'' -> ''City/town/settlement''"}]'::jsonb),
