    ('f1710818-b1bc-401e-95ca-cae1bb55ada8'::uuid, 'mystery', '3', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:f1710818-b1bc-401e-95ca-cae1bb55ada8:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Khirokitia', '7b2830ed0521c7c218ef9646be29cec6', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 3 from (''City/town/settlement'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 105 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:88374", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2198:chunk-0001: description ''7th–4th millennium BC: Khirokitia (also spelled Choirokoitia) is a Neolithic archaeological site in Cyprus, designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1998. It is one of the most important and best-preserved prehistoric settlements in the eastern Mediterranean, featuring a collective settlement with surrounding fortifications that demonstrate an organized, functional society with communal protection. The site exemplifies the Neolithic aceramic period, with approximately 20 similar settlements distributed across the island.[1][2]'' -> \"Khirokitia (sometimes spelled Choirokoitia; Greek: Χοιροκοιτία [çiɾociˈti.a], suggested meaning Pig-cradle, from χοίρος ''pig, boar'' + κοιτίς ''place of origin, cradle'') is an archaeological site on the island of Cyprus dating from the Neolithic age [1]. The site is known as one of the most important and best preserved prehistoric sites of the eastern Mediterranean [1]. Much of its importance lies in the evidence of an organised functional society in the form of a collective settlement [1]. The site was discovered in 1934 by Porphyrios Dikaios [1]. The settlement of Khirokitia was situated on the slope of a hill in the valley of the Maroni River, towards the southern coast of the island about 6 km from the sea [1]. Subsistence methods practiced by its Neolithic inhabitants included farming crops like wheat, barley, lentils, peas, horse bean, and vetch, foraging olives, flax, figs, pistachios, plums, and pears, herding sheep, goats, and pigs [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:165915", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-033: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/f1710818/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/f1710818/Khirokitia_near_Larnaca_01-2017_img7.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:319398", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-015: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/f1710818/Khirokitia_near_Larnaca_01-2017_img7.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/f1710818/Khirokitia3.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
