re after the Battle of Roncevaux Pass, though the dolmen predates the legend by millennia.'' -> ''The Caixa de Rotllan is made of three upright stones in a H-shape, supporting a thick roofing stone and delimiting a rectangular, medium-sized chamber [1]. The entrance faces south-east [1]. The building has been listed as a Monument historique since 1889 but has never been excavated by archaeologists [1]. The Caixa de Rotllan is one of 148 dolmens listed in the Pyrénées-Orientales department [1]. Like most of the dolmens in the Pyrénées-Orientales, the Caixa de Rotllan has a simple plan — that is, without a corridor — which relates it to other dolmens from the Chalcolithic and Bronze Age of the second half of the third millennium BC [1]. During the Middle Ages it marked the boundary between Arles and Montbolo [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:165931", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-033: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/f1c3510d/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/f1c3510d/Caixa de Rottlan 1.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:319401", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-015: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/f1c3510d/Caixa de Rottlan 1.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/f1c3510d/Caixa de Rottlan 4.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
