ft × 50 ft, enclosed by a thin wall of unbaked bricks [1]. Incorporating one wall of this first structure, the second temple of about 40 ft square was built with walls about 10 ft thick [1]. An outer temenos wall surrounded the grounds [1]. This outer wall was made wider some time around the Second or Third Dynasty [1]. The old temple entirely vanished in the Fourth Dynasty, and a smaller building was erected behind it, enclosing a wide hearth of black ashes [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:166565", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-034: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/33/AbydosFacade.jpg'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:319452", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-015: thumbnail_url None -> ''/data/images/wiki/f523b5df/AbydosFacade.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f524b8bf-c69d-4dd3-8fb6-549de566d297'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:f524b8bf-c69d-4dd3-8fb6-549de566d297:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''5'' restored on White Horse Stone', '5c80c47cd1d3f3d9df07326bcb71f1d4', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Megalithic stones'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 17 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43197", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0068:chunk-0001: description \"The White Horse Stone comprises two sarsen megaliths on Blue Bell Hill near Aylesford, Kent, England. The Lower Stone was destroyed before 1834; the surviving Upper Stone took on the name and associated folklore. Archaeologists speculate—though unverified—that both stones formed parts of chambered long barrows from the fourth millennium BC, during Britain''s Early Neolithic period.\" -> ''The White Horse Stone is a name given to two separate sarsen megaliths on the slopes of Blue Bell Hill, near the village of Aylesford in the south-eastern English county of Kent [1]. The Lower White Horse Stone was destroyed prior to 1834, at which time the surviving Upper White Horse Stone took on its name and folkloric associations [1]. Various archaeologists have suggested—although not proven—that the stones were each part of chambered long barrows constructed in the fourth millennium BC [1]. Excavation has revealed the existence of an Early Neolithic longhouse near to the stone [1]. The Upper White Horse Stone is 2.9 metres in length, 1.65 metres in height, and about 0.6 metres thick [1]. The White Horse Stone is situated in \"a narrow strip of woodland\", known as Westfield Wood, which is adjacent to the Pilgrim\\''s Way [1]. To the south of the White Horse Stone was a building—termed \"Structure 4806\" by its excavators in the 2000s—that was constructed in the Early Neolithic period [1]. Radiocarbon dating from the site suggests a usage date of between 4110-3820 and 3780-3530 calibrated BC [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:112209", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s023: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:112210", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s023: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:166587", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-034: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Upper_White_Horse_Stone.jpg/400px-Upper_White_Horse_Stone.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/f524b8bf/Detail on the Northern Face of the White Horse Stone, Kent (I).webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:244811", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s011: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:244812", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s011: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}]'::jsonb),
