    ('fa2bcb15-3d7f-4c62-8215-514ae296e375'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:fa2bcb15-3d7f-4c62-8215-514ae296e375:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Windmill Hill, Avebury', '91eaca1ea6aa89b9ca3d29ac9480d82b', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Earthwork'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 13 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:88564", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2204:chunk-0001: description ''Windmill Hill is a Neolithic causewayed enclosure in Wiltshire, England, forming part of the Avebury World Heritage Site[1]. Located about 2 km (1.2 mi) northwest of Avebury, it encloses 21 acres (8.5 ha), making it the largest known causewayed enclosure in Britain[1]. The site was first occupied around 3800 BC, evidenced by pits containing Hembury pottery[1].'' -> ''Windmill Hill is a Neolithic causewayed enclosure in the English county of Wiltshire, part of the Avebury World Heritage Site, about 1 mile northwest of Avebury [1]. Enclosing an area of 21 acres, it is the largest known causewayed enclosure in Britain [1]. The site was first occupied around 3800 BC, although the only evidence is a series of pits apparently dug by an agrarian society using Hembury pottery [1]. During a later phase, c. 3700 BC, three concentric segmented ditches were placed around the hilltop site, the outermost with a diameter of 365 metres [1]. The causeways interrupting the ditches vary in width from a few centimetres to 7 m [1]. The site was bought by Alexander Keiller in 1924 and excavated over several seasons from 1925 to 1929 by Keiller and Harold St George Gray, whose work established it as the type site for causewayed camps [1]. Pottery from the bottom of the ditches was the type style for the Windmill Hill culture [1]. The camp remained in use throughout the rest of the Neolithic with Grooved ware and Beaker potsherds having been found in later deposits [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:167297", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-035: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/25/Windmill_Hill_from_the_White_Horse_Trail%2C_Winterbourne_Monkton_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1010976.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/fa2bcb15/Charlie The Skeleton.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:320024", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-016: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/fa2bcb15/Charlie The Skeleton.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/fa2bcb15/Windmill Hill from the White Horse Trail, Winterbourne Monkton - geograph.org.uk - 1010976.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
