 [1]. The site was bought by Alexander Keiller in 1924 and excavated over several seasons from 1925 to 1929 by Keiller and Harold St George Gray, whose work established it as the type site for causewayed camps [1]. Pottery from the bottom of the ditches was the type style for the Windmill Hill culture [1]. The camp remained in use throughout the rest of the Neolithic with Grooved ware and Beaker potsherds having been found in later deposits [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:167297", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-035: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/25/Windmill_Hill_from_the_White_Horse_Trail%2C_Winterbourne_Monkton_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1010976.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/fa2bcb15/Charlie The Skeleton.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:320024", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-016: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/fa2bcb15/Charlie The Skeleton.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/fa2bcb15/Windmill Hill from the White Horse Trail, Winterbourne Monkton - geograph.org.uk - 1010976.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
