in northern Wiltshire, England, about a mile north of the village of Alton Priors [1]. At the top of the hill is a causewayed enclosure, a form of Neolithic earthwork that was constructed in England from about 3700 BC onwards, characterized by the full or partial enclosure of an area with ditches that are interrupted by gaps [1]. Knap Hill is notable as the first causewayed enclosure to be excavated and identified [1]. Two barrows lay within the Neolithic enclosure, and at least one more outside it [1]. The hilltop also contains the remains of a Romano-British settlement on an adjoining smaller area called the plateau enclosure, along with some evidence of occupation in the 17th century [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:112361", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s023: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:112362", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s023: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:167452", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-035: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/fd9e31aa/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/fd9e31aa/Knap Hill - panoramio.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:245191", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s012: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:245192", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s012: period_start None -> ''-3530''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('fe021c51-e3fb-407d-9f6c-b770ba056f15'::uuid, 'mystery', '3', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:fe021c51-e3fb-407d-9f6c-b770ba056f15:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: mystery ''4'' restored on Bacho Kiro Cave', 'a41fa67ee5bb269c9c97fff54e2ec446', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 3 from (''Cave Structures'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 113 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43269", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0070:chunk-0001: description ''Bacho Kiro Cave is a four-storey labyrinthine cave system 5 km west of Dryanovo, Bulgaria, located 300 m from the Dryanovo Monastery [1]. With a total length of 3,600 m, it features an underground river that carved galleries containing stalactite, stalagmite, and stalactone speleothems of great beauty [1]. The cave contains archaeological evidence of Initial Upper Palaeolithic Homo sapiens occupation dating to approximately 46,000–43,000 years before present, making it the earliest directly dated site of Upper Palaeolithic modern humans in Europe [2][3]. Of the cave, 700 m are open to the public with electrical lighting installed in 1964 [1].'' -> ''The Bacho Kiro Cave is situated 5 km west of the town Dryanovo, Bulgaria, only 300 m away from the Dryanovo Monastery [1]. It is embedded in the canyons of the Andaka and Dryanovo River [1]. The cave is a four-storey labyrinth of galleries and corridors with a total length of 3,600 m [1]. The site has yielded the oldest human remains ever to be found in Bulgaria [1]. At one of the earliest known Aurignacian burials (layer 11), two pierced animal teeth were found and ordered into the distinct Bachokiran artifact assemblage [1]. Radiocarbon dated to over 43,000 years ago, they currently represent the oldest known ornaments in Europe [1]. With an approximate age of 46,000 years, human fossils consist of a pair of fragmented mandibles including at least one molar [1]. Whether these early humans were in fact Homo sapiens or Neanderthals was disputed until morphological analysis of a tooth and mitochondrial DNA of bone fragments established that remains were those of Homo sapiens [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
