ecorded by the antiquarian William Stukeley although the stones were destroyed by local farmers in the 1720s [1]. The Sanctuary underwent archaeological excavation by Maud and Ben Cunnington in 1930 [1]. The first stage of activity at the site, some time around 3000 BCE, consisted of a ring of eight wooden posts 4.5 metres across, with a central post, presumed to be a round hut [1]. Neolithic pottery and animal bone were recovered by the Cunningtons, indicating that the site saw some degree of occupation activity [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:112375", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s023: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:112376", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s023: period_start ''-4500'' -> ''-3000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:167470", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-035: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c0/Thesanctuary.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/fe7c453a/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:320084", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-016: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/fe7c453a/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/fe7c453a/Thesanctuary.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('fe7c453a-cabe-4fb2-9009-2ed71a7632fd'::uuid, 'total_power', '27', '28', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:fe7c453a-cabe-4fb2-9009-2ed71a7632fd:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: total_power ''28'' restored on The Sanctuary', '0f2b237ff3decb8e88bfd2d3e4432d62', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 27 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 7 + 8 + 7 + 4 + 1"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:81526", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2035:chunk-0001: description \"The Sanctuary was a late Neolithic stone and timber ceremonial monument on Overton Hill near Avebury, built around 2500 BC. Maud Cunnington''s 1930 excavation revealed two concentric stone circles and five timber post rings, with the outermost stone circle measuring 138 feet in diameter. The site yielded flint tools, animal bones, human remains, and pottery, suggesting ritualistic use. Connected to Avebury''s henge via the 1.5-mile West Kennet Avenue, it is now part of the Stonehenge and Avebury UNESCO World Heritage Site.\" -> ''The Sanctuary was a stone and timber circle near the village of Avebury in the south-western English county of Wiltshire [1]. The ring was part of a tradition of stone circle construction that spread throughout much of Britain, Ireland, and Brittany during the Late Neolithic and Early Bronze Age, over a period between 3300 and 900 BCE [1]. The Sanctuary was erected on Overton Hill [1]. It was connected to the Late Neolithic henge and stone circle at Avebury via the West Kennet Avenue of stones [1]. In the early 18th century, the site was recorded by the antiquarian William Stukeley although the stones were destroyed by local farmers in the 1720s [1]. The Sanctuary underwent archaeological excavation by Maud and Ben Cunnington in 1930 [1]. The first stage of activity at the site, some time around 3000 BCE, consisted of a ring of eight wooden posts 4.5 metres across, with a central post, presumed to be a round hut [1]. Neolithic pottery and animal bone were recovered by the Cunningtons, indicating that the site saw some degree of occupation activity [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:112375", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s023: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:112376", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s023: period_start ''-4500'' -> ''-3000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:167470", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-035: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c0/Thesanctuary.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/fe7c453a/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:320084", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-016: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/fe7c453a/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/fe7c453a/Thesanctuary.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('fe7d7ee7-ca93-4cf5-93d8-0cc80564ccd8'::uuid, 'cultural_influence', '8', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-05-rollback:fe7d7ee7-ca93-4cf5-93d8-0cc80564ccd8:cultural_influence', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-05: cultural_influence ''7'' restored on Calcaria', '474afd5993883d6286b21311cd2b8025', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "cultural_influence = 8 from description yes, thumbnail yes, 4 content link(s), 1 image(s), source_url yes"}, {"source": "production export 21200f1e3f26a29a (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 5519 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04f''s own export (2026-10-04 19:19:41.515456+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43273", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0070:chunk-0001: description \"Calcaria was a Roman settlement at the crossing of the River Wharfe on the road from Danum (Doncaster) to Eburacum (York), in what is now Tadcaster, North Yorkshire. Named from the Latin for lime kilns, reflecting the area''s valuable Magnesian Limestone quarrying industry, the town served as a staging post and river-port dispatching stone to York. Archaeological finds include Roman coins discovered in churchyard graves, pottery, and burial sites, though no visible structural remains survive. The settlement''s identification as the Calcaria of the Antonine Itinerary is supported by its position approximately 10 Roman miles from York.\" -> ''The Romans founded the settlement and named it Calcaria from the Latin word for lime kilns [1]. The nature of the settlement is uncertain [1]. It is possible that it started as an Imperial staging post with a mansio because of its location at a river crossing on the road from Danum (Doncaster) to Eburacum (York) [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:112377", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s023: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:112378", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s023: period_start ''1'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:167471", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-035: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/fe7d7ee7/hero.webp'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:255111", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s022: period_name None -> ''1 - 500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:255112", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s022: period_start None -> ''1''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:327211", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-005-004: thumbnail_url None -> ''/data/images/wiki/fe7d7ee7/Calcaria.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:354489", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s010: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:354490", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s010: period_start ''1'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:354803", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-19r_fields-wd5-s001: period_name ''Undated'' -> ''1 - 500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:354804", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-19r_fields-wd5-s001: period_start None -> ''43''"}]'::jsonb),
